+// ─── CHANNEL: bug ─────────────────────────────────────────────
+{id:'b1',ch:'bug',type:'mc',
+ q:'Spot the bug. A pre-a20fbb1 SIL test did this and passed. What was it actually testing?',
+ snip:`TIMERA0:MODE UP
+TIMERA0:PERIOD 1000        <span class="cm"># set the top value</span>
+TIMERA0:PERIOD?            <span class="cm"># → 1000  ✓ assertion passed</span>
+<span class="cm"># firmware's PWM module meanwhile reads CCR0 for its duty math…</span>
+TIMERA0:CC0:VALUE?         <span class="cm"># → 0     (!!)</span>`,
+ opts:[
+  {t:'Nothing real: PERIOD and CC0 were two independent stores, so the test verified a phantom register while the CCR0 the firmware depends on still held 0',c:true, w:'Correct. The old model round-tripped writes through the standalone period field, so PERIOD? == 1000 proved only that a Python attribute works. On silicon both names are TAxCCR0 — the state the test thought it verified did not exist as tested. After the fix both queries return 1000.'},
+  {t:'The test forgot to start the timer, so the period write was ignored',c:false,w:'Writes are never gated on the timer running, and nothing was ignored — the value landed, just in a store that hardware doesn\'t have.'},
+  {t:'1000 exceeds the period register\'s width',c:false,w:'1000 fits easily in 16 bits. The defect was aliasing (two stores for one register), not width.'},
+  {t:'UP mode disables CCR0 reads',c:false,w:'No mode disables register reads. CC0:VALUE? returned 0 simply because the PERIOD write never touched capture_compare[0].'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'b2',ch:'bug',type:'w',
+ q:'Describe the double-store bug class in general terms: what creates it, why it is worse than a missing feature, and how the a20fbb1 fix pattern (delete-and-delegate) prevents its recurrence. Give the PWM duty-cycle example.',
+ c:[{l:'created when two API names get two backing stores for one hardware register',t:['two stores','two names','independent','duplicate','one register']},
+    {l:'the model can hold contradictory state silicon cannot represent',t:['contradict','diverge','two truths','inconsistent','cannot represent']},
+    {l:'worse than missing: reads succeed and look plausible — silent',t:['silent','plausible','looks','passes','no error','worse than missing']},
+    {l:'fix: one store, alias delegates; PWM duty = CCR1/CCR0 breaks when PERIOD and CCR0 disagree',t:['delegate','one store','delete','duty','ccr1','alias']}],
+ m:`It is created whenever an API exposes two names for one hardware register and the implementer backs each name with its own variable — here, ts.period and ts.capture_compare[0] for the single TAxCCR0. The model can then hold two different "period" values simultaneously, a configuration that has no counterpart in silicon (one set of flip-flops cannot store 1000 and 0 at once).
+It is worse than a missing feature because both paths WORK in isolation: every write round-trips, every read returns something plausible, no error is ever queued. The contradiction only bites when a test or firmware mixes the names — e.g. PWM: duty = CCR1/CCR0. Configure the period via TIMERA0:PERIOD 1000 and duty via CC1:VALUE 250, then compute duty from CC0:VALUE? — you divide 250 by 0. Or worse, each half of the system reads its own store and both look self-consistent.
+The fix pattern is delete-and-delegate: remove the duplicate store entirely and make the alias a one-line call into the canonical handler (return self._timer_cc_val(cmd, ts, 0)). Because there is physically one store left, divergence is unrepresentable in the model — the bug class, not just the bug, is gone. Synchronising two stores instead would leave the class alive, waiting for the next unsynchronised write path.`
+},
