+<script>
+// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
+// QUESTION BANK  (26 questions — grounded in commit 701853d)
+// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
+const BANK = [
+
+// ─── CHANNEL: tree ────────────────────────────────────────────
+{id:'t1',ch:'tree',type:'w',
+ q:'Explain the claim "a clock signal is source × divider, not a stored number." What three steps must a frequency query walk, and what does ClockState store instead of per-clock frequencies?',
+ c:[{l:'MCLK/SMCLK/ACLK are mux routes, not oscillators',t:['mux','route','not an oscillator','routes','multiplex']},
+    {l:'walk: resolve source → base frequency → apply divider',t:['resolve','base frequency','divider','walk','three steps','then divide']},
+    {l:'ClockState stores configuration: source + div per clock',t:['configuration','mclk_source','stores the source','source and div','aclk_source']},
+    {l:'no per-clock frequency field — storing one would duplicate derivable truth',t:['no frequency field','derivable','duplicate','double-store','derived']}],
+ m:`MCLK, SMCLK, and ACLK have no frequency of their own — each is the output of a source multiplexer (CSCTL2.SELM/SELS/SELA) followed by a divider (CSCTL3.DIVM/DIVS/DIVA). The frequency is a derived value, recomputed per query:
+1. Resolve the source: which oscillator does the mux select right now (e.g. clock.mclk_source)?
+2. Get that source's base frequency: _clock_source_frequency() — DCO setting × drift, a crystal field, or a fixed internal rate.
+3. Apply the divider: divide by mclk_div/smclk_div/aclk_div.
+ClockState stores only configuration (source string + divider int per clock) plus base frequencies for DCO/LFXT/HFXT. There is deliberately no mclk_frequency field: storing the answer would be a second copy of derivable truth that drifts out of sync — the same double-store bug class as the old independent timer period field.`
+},
+
+{id:'t2',ch:'tree',type:'mc',
+ q:'CLOCK:ACLK:SOURCE LFMODCLK, then CLOCK:ACLK:DIVIDER 8. What does CLOCK:ACLK:FREQUENCY? return?',
+ snip:`<span class="cm"># msp430.py — after 701853d</span>
+LFMODCLK_FREQUENCY_HZ = MODCLK_FREQUENCY_HZ / <span class="num">128</span>   <span class="cm"># 4.8e6/128 = 37500</span>
+freq = self._clock_source_frequency(self.clock.aclk_source) / self.clock.aclk_div
+<span class="kw">return</span> f<span class="str">"{freq:.0f}"</span>`,
+ opts:[
+  {t:'"4688" — 37500/8 = 4687.5, and the :.0f format rounds',c:true, w:'Correct. LFMODCLK = 4.8e6/128 = 37500 Hz; /8 gives 4687.5; f"{4687.5:.0f}" rounds (ties-to-even) to "4688". The clock path rounds — unlike the ADC path, which truncates with int().'},
+  {t:'"4687" — fractional frequencies are truncated like ADC codes',c:false,w:'The clock handlers use f"{freq:.0f}", which rounds to nearest. int() truncation is the ADC path. 4687.5 → "4688".'},
+  {t:'"37500" — dividers only apply to MCLK and SMCLK',c:false,w:'DIVA is real and 8 is a valid encoding; ACLK applies it. 37500 is LFMODCLK before the divider.'},
+  {t:'"600000" — LFMODCLK is 4.8 MHz divided by 8',c:false,w:'That skips the fixed /128: LFMODCLK is MODCLK/128 = 37.5 kHz, not raw MODCLK. Then /8 → 4687.5.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'t3',ch:'tree',type:'mc',
+ q:'CLOCK:MCLK:SOURCE VLO and CLOCK:MCLK:DIVIDER 4 are accepted. What does CLOCK:MCLK:FREQUENCY? report?',
+ opts:[
+  {t:'"2350" — VLO is fixed at 9400 Hz and the /4 divider applies',c:true, w:'Correct. _clock_source_frequency("VLO") returns VLO_FREQUENCY_HZ = 9400.0 (no configuration register exists for VLO); 9400/4 = 2350 exactly.'},
+  {t:'"250000" — the DCO 1-MHz default divided by 4',c:false,w:'That is the pre-701853d lie: the old handler computed _dco_actual()/div regardless of the selected source. The fix resolves VLO → 9400.'},
+  {t:'"8192" — VLO is the 32.768-kHz crystal',c:false,w:'32768 Hz is LFXT, the external watch crystal. VLO is the internal ~9.4-kHz RC oscillator — a different source entirely.'},
+  {t:'SCPI error -224 — VLO is not a valid MCLK source',c:false,w:'VLO IS in VALID_MCLK_SMCLK_SOURCES (SELM encoding 1). It is valid for all three clocks.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'t4',ch:'tree',type:'w',
+ q:'Compute what CLOCK:SMCLK:FREQUENCY? reports for each configuration, showing the arithmetic and the output formatting: (a) SOURCE MODCLK, DIVIDER 32; (b) SOURCE LFMODCLK, DIVIDER 1; (c) SOURCE VLO, DIVIDER 32.',
+ c:[{l:'(a) 4.8e6/32 = 150000',t:['150000','150 000']},
+    {l:'(b) 4.8e6/128 = 37500',t:['37500','37 500']},
+    {l:'(c) 9400/32 = 293.75 → ":.0f" rounds → "294"',t:['294','293.75']},
+    {l:'format is f"{freq:.0f}" — rounds, not truncates',t:['round','.0f','not truncat','format']}],
+ m:`All three use _clock_source_frequency(source)/div, formatted with f"{freq:.0f}":
+(a) MODCLK/32: 4 800 000 / 32 = 150 000 → "150000".
+(b) LFMODCLK/1: 4 800 000 / 128 = 37 500 → "37500" (the /128 is baked into the LFMODCLK constant, not a divider you configure).
+(c) VLO/32: 9400 / 32 = 293.75 → the :.0f format ROUNDS to "294" (truncation would give 293 — that is the ADC path's behaviour, not the clock path's).
+The general shape: resolve the source to its base rate, apply the configured divider, round for the string reply. The underlying float is what _timer_tick_frequency consumes un-rounded.`
+},
+
+{id:'t5',ch:'tree',type:'mc',
+ q:'Fresh device, no configuration sent. What do the three FREQUENCY? queries report, and why?',
+ opts:[
+  {t:'MCLK "1000000", SMCLK "1000000", ACLK "32768" — defaults are DCO/1, DCO/1, LFXT/1',c:true, w:'Correct. ClockState defaults: mclk_source="DCO", smclk_source="DCO" (dco_frequency=1e6), aclk_source="LFXT" (lfxt_frequency=32768), all dividers 1. Under the NOMINAL profile dco_factor() is exactly 1.0.'},
+  {t:'All three report "1000000" — everything defaults to the DCO',c:false,w:'ACLK defaults to LFXT (matching hardware, where ACLK is the low-frequency clock): 32768/1 = "32768".'},
+  {t:'All three report "0" until a source is explicitly selected',c:false,w:'The dataclass defaults ARE a valid selection — DCO/DCO/LFXT with /1 dividers — mirroring a sane post-reset configuration.'},
+  {t:'MCLK "1000000", SMCLK "4800000", ACLK "9400" — DCO, MODCLK, VLO',c:false,w:'SMCLK defaults to DCO like MCLK (they share the source set and reset behaviour); ACLK defaults to LFXT, not VLO.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+// ─── CHANNEL: enc ─────────────────────────────────────────────
+{id:'e1',ch:'enc',type:'w',
+ q:'List the source set MCLK/SMCLK may select from and the set ACLK may select from (with the CSCTL2 field names), and explain WHY ACLK\'s set is narrower.',
+ c:[{l:'MCLK/SMCLK (SELM/SELS): LFXT, VLO, LFMODCLK, DCO, MODCLK, HFXT',t:['hfxt','dco','modclk','six','selm','sels']},
+    {l:'ACLK (SELA): LFXT, VLO, LFMODCLK only',t:['sela','lfmodclk','three','only','subset','narrower']},
+    {l:'ACLK is the auxiliary low-frequency clock for low-power operation',t:['low-power','low power','auxiliary','lpm','keeps ticking','low-frequency','low frequency']},
+    {l:'the sets mirror register encodings — two frozensets in msp430.py',t:['frozenset','encoding','valid_mclk','valid_aclk','register']}],
+ m:`MCLK and SMCLK share one six-entry menu (CSCTL2.SELM and CSCTL2.SELS): {LFXT, VLO, LFMODCLK, DCO, MODCLK, HFXT} — VALID_MCLK_SMCLK_SOURCES in msp430.py.
+ACLK (CSCTL2.SELA) selects only from {LFXT, VLO, LFMODCLK} — VALID_ACLK_SOURCES.
+Why narrower: ACLK is the auxiliary low-frequency clock, architected to keep running in low-power modes and feed slow peripherals (RTC-style timers, watchdog). Its mux simply has no encodings for the fast sources — you physically cannot route DCO/MODCLK/HFXT to ACLK on this silicon. The simulator mirrors the encodings with two frozensets, so CLOCK:ACLK:SOURCE DCO draws -224 while CLOCK:SMCLK:SOURCE DCO succeeds. The comment in the code also notes what is missing from BOTH sets on the FR59xx CS: REFO and DCOCLKDIV have no encoding at all.`
+},
+
+{id:'e2',ch:'enc',type:'mc',
+ q:'Why does the fixed model reject CLOCK:MCLK:SOURCE REFO with -224?',
+ snip:`<span class="cm"># CSCTL2.SELM/SELS: MCLK and SMCLK share one source mux. REFO and DCOCLKDIV</span>
+<span class="cm"># have no encoding on the FR59xx CS; MODCLK and LFMODCLK do.</span>
+VALID_MCLK_SMCLK_SOURCES = <span class="fn">frozenset</span>({<span class="str">"LFXT"</span>, <span class="str">"VLO"</span>, <span class="str">"LFMODCLK"</span>, <span class="str">"DCO"</span>, <span class="str">"MODCLK"</span>, <span class="str">"HFXT"</span>})`,
+ opts:[
+  {t:'REFO belongs to the F5xx/6xx UCS clock module; the FR59xx CS module has no SELM encoding for it — the simulator must mirror this chip\'s registers, not the family\'s folklore',c:true, w:'Correct. REFO (a trimmed 32-kHz reference oscillator) exists on other MSP430 generations. On the FR5994, SLAU367P §3.3.2 defines SELM encodings only for LFXT/VLO/LFMODCLK/DCO/MODCLK/HFXT. Accepting REFO would bless a configuration this silicon cannot express — the old model did exactly that, and even rejected MODCLK, which IS encoding 4.'},
+  {t:'REFO is too imprecise to be worth simulating',c:false,w:'Precision is irrelevant — VLO is far less precise and is fully supported. The question is whether a SELM encoding exists on the FR5994, and for REFO none does.'},
+  {t:'REFO is reserved for ACLK only',c:false,w:'REFO appears in neither set — SELA offers LFXT/VLO/LFMODCLK. There is no REFO anywhere on the FR59xx CS.'},
+  {t:'REFO conflicts with the LFXT crystal pins',c:false,w:'Pin muxing is not the reason; the CS module simply has no such oscillator on this part.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'e3',ch:'enc',type:'mc',
+ q:'A script sends CLOCK:ACLK:SOURCE DCO. What happens?',
+ opts:[
+  {t:'ScpiError -224 "Illegal clock source: DCO" is queued and aclk_source is unchanged — DCO has no SELA encoding',c:true, w:'Correct. DCO is in VALID_MCLK_SMCLK_SOURCES but not in VALID_ACLK_SOURCES; the _clk_aclk_src handler validates against the narrower set and rejects before storing. Same token, different mux, different verdict.'},
+  {t:'It succeeds — DCO is a valid source for every clock',c:false,w:'Only for MCLK and SMCLK. ACLK\'s SELA mux offers just LFXT/VLO/LFMODCLK on the FR5994.'},
+  {t:'ScpiError -222 is queued — DCO is out of ACLK\'s frequency range',c:false,w:'-222 is the divider error code (numeric data out of range). An unencodable source token is -224, Illegal parameter value.'},
+  {t:'ACLK falls back to LFXT silently with no error',c:false,w:'Rejection queues an error AND leaves prior state untouched; there is no silent substitution anywhere in the clock handlers.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'e4',ch:'enc',type:'mc',
+ q:'Before commit 701853d, what did CLOCK:SMCLK:SOURCE BANANA do, and what does it do now?',
+ snip:`<span class="cm"># scripts/audit_msp430.py — probe rewritten by 701853d</span>
+<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">probe_smclk_source_unvalidated</span>(dev):
+    <span class="str">"""SMCLK's source set matches MCLK's: MODCLK accepted, BANANA rejected."""</span>
+    dev.process(<span class="str">"CLOCK:SMCLK:SOURCE MODCLK"</span>)
+    accepted_modclk = _errors(dev) == <span class="num">0</span> <span class="kw">and</span> dev.clock.smclk_source == <span class="str">"MODCLK"</span>
+    dev.process(<span class="str">"CLOCK:SMCLK:SOURCE BANANA"</span>)
+    rejected_banana = _errors(dev) == <span class="num">1</span>
+    <span class="kw">return</span> accepted_modclk <span class="kw">and</span> rejected_banana`,
+ opts:[
+  {t:'Before: stored "BANANA" with no error (the handler had no validation at all). Now: -224 queued, previous source kept',c:true, w:'Correct. The old _clk_smclk_src did self.clock.smclk_source = cmd.params[0].upper() unconditionally. The fix validates against VALID_MCLK_SMCLK_SOURCES first. The rewritten probe checks both directions: the valid MODCLK is accepted, the nonsense token rejected.'},
+  {t:'Before: -224. Now: -224 — only the error message changed',c:false,w:'SMCLK\'s source had NO validation before the fix — that was the audit finding (probe name: smclk_source_unvalidated). Only MCLK had a (wrong) validation set.'},
+  {t:'Before and after: BANANA is coerced to the nearest valid token',c:false,w:'No coercion exists. Before: stored verbatim. After: strict frozenset membership, rejected with -224.'},
+  {t:'Before: crashed with KeyError when FREQUENCY? was next queried. Now: -224',c:false,w:'The old FREQUENCY? never read smclk_source (it always used the DCO), so the stored BANANA never even mattered — that is what made the hole so silent.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'e5',ch:'enc',type:'w',
+ q:'Classify the six clock sources by where their frequency comes from in the model: which are configurable, which are fixed constants, and which one is a simulator-only default? Give the three fixed-rate values.',
+ c:[{l:'configurable: DCO (CLOCK:DCO:FREQUENCY × drift), LFXT field 32768',t:['dco','configurable','lfxt','32768','clock:dco']},
+    {l:'fixed constants: VLO 9400 Hz, MODCLK 4.8 MHz — no configuration register',t:['9400','4.8','no register','no configurable','fixed']},
+    {l:'LFMODCLK = MODCLK/128 = 37500 Hz, computed not restated',t:['128','37500','37.5','computed','derived from modclk']},
+    {l:'HFXT 4e6 is a simulator-only default (external crystal, CSCTL4.HFFREQ unmodeled)',t:['hfxt','simulator-only','simulator only','external crystal','4e6','hffreq']}],
+ m:`Three kinds:
+1. Configurable state: DCO — clock.dco_frequency (default 1e6), set via CLOCK:DCO:FREQUENCY, multiplied by faults.dco_factor() at read time (±3.5% in CORNER); LFXT — clock.lfxt_frequency, default 32768 (the watch crystal).
+2. Fixed constants (SLAU367P §3.2.5/§3.2.6 — internal RC oscillators with no configuration register): VLO_FREQUENCY_HZ = 9400.0 (~9.4 kHz typical) and MODCLK_FREQUENCY_HZ = 4.8e6 (~4.8 MHz typical). LFMODCLK_FREQUENCY_HZ = MODCLK_FREQUENCY_HZ / 128 = 37 500 Hz — written as the relationship, not a second literal, so the two constants cannot drift apart.
+3. Simulator-only default: hfxt_frequency = 4e6. HFXT is an external crystal with no hardware nominal — its range is set by CSCTL4.HFFREQ, which the model does not simulate — so the code documents the 4-MHz default as a simulator convenience, not a hardware fact.`
+},
+
+// ─── CHANNEL: div ─────────────────────────────────────────────
+{id:'d1',ch:'div',type:'mc',
+ q:'A test sends CLOCK:MCLK:DIVIDER 64. Per the fixed code and the rewritten audit probe, what is the device state afterwards?',
+ snip:`<span class="cm"># scripts/audit_msp430.py — after 701853d</span>
+<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">probe_mclk_divider_64</span>(dev):
+    <span class="str">"""DIVM stops at /32 (SLAU367P §3.3.3 CSCTL3); /64 is rejected."""</span>
+    dev.process(<span class="str">"CLOCK:MCLK:DIVIDER 64"</span>)
+    <span class="kw">return</span> _errors(dev) == <span class="num">1</span> <span class="kw">and</span> dev.clock.mclk_div == <span class="num">1</span>`,
+ opts:[
+  {t:'One SCPI error (-222 "Illegal divider: 64") is queued and mclk_div is still 1 — the bad value is never stored',c:true, w:'Correct. 64 is not in VALID_CLOCK_DIVIDERS = {1,2,4,8,16,32}; the handler raises before assignment, so the default /1 survives. The probe asserts exactly this pair: one error, unchanged state.'},
+  {t:'mclk_div becomes 64 with no error — the old permissive set is kept for compatibility',c:false,w:'That was the pre-fix behaviour (the old VALID set went up to 128). DIVM\'s 3-bit encodings stop at /32; the fix enforces it.'},
+  {t:'-222 is queued and mclk_div is clamped to the maximum, 32',c:false,w:'Rejection never coerces: prior state (1, the default) is preserved. Clamping would silently misconfigure the clock tree.'},
+  {t:'-224 is queued — same code as an illegal source',c:false,w:'The commit deliberately changed the divider error from -224 to -222: a number outside the encodable range is "Data out of range", while an unencodable token is "Illegal parameter value".'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'d2',ch:'div',type:'mc',
+ q:'Commit 701853d changed the MCLK divider\'s rejection code from -224 to -222 while sources stayed at -224. What distinction is being drawn?',
+ opts:[
+  {t:'-222 "Data out of range" for numeric parameters outside the encodable range (DIVIDER 64); -224 "Illegal parameter value" for tokens outside an enumerated set (SOURCE REFO)',c:true, w:'Correct. Both leave state unchanged and queue one error; the codes classify the failure. A divider is a number with a bounded encodable range; a source is an enum. The same split appears elsewhere in the model: timer register writes beyond 16 bits draw -222, while BANANA-style tokens draw -224.'},
+  {t:'-222 is retryable, -224 is fatal',c:false,w:'Neither is fatal or retryable in any special way — both queue an error and continue. Only the classification differs.'},
+  {t:'-222 applies to queries, -224 to writes',c:false,w:'Both errors arise on writes here. Query vs write is not the axis; parameter kind is.'},
+  {t:'No distinction — it was a typo the tests happened to encode',c:false,w:'It is deliberate and consistent across all three divider handlers (DIVM/DIVS/DIVA all raise -222 after the fix), matching SCPI\'s standard error taxonomy.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'d3',ch:'div',type:'w',
+ q:'Why is CLOCK:MCLK:DIVIDER 7 rejected even though 7 < 32? Explain what CSCTL3.DIVM physically is, list the accepted set, and contrast it with the Timer_A divider chain from lesson 0010.',
+ c:[{l:'DIVM is a 3-bit field: only power-of-two encodings /1../32 exist',t:['3-bit','power','encoding','power-of-two','powers of two']},
+    {l:'VALID_CLOCK_DIVIDERS = {1,2,4,8,16,32}',t:['{1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32}','1,2,4,8,16,32','frozenset']},
+    {l:'not a magnitude test — a membership test',t:['membership','not a range','not magnitude','in the set','not in']},
+    {l:'Timer_A: ID × TAIDEX product gives non-powers (e.g. /6, /40), max /64 — different encoding, different set',t:['taidex','id ×','timer','/64','product','20 reachable']}],
+ m:`The check is set membership, not magnitude: v not in VALID_CLOCK_DIVIDERS = frozenset({1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32}) → ScpiError -222. CSCTL3.DIVM is a 3-bit register field whose defined encodings map to power-of-two dividers /1 through /32 (SLAU367P §3.3.3); there is no bit pattern meaning /7, so no FR5994 can divide MCLK by 7 — accepting it would put the simulator outside the hardware\'s reachable state space.
+Contrast with Timer_A (lesson 0010): the timer\'s effective divider is the PRODUCT of two fields, ID (/1/2/4/8) × TAIDEX (/1../8), which reaches non-powers like /6, /12, /40 and tops out at 8×8 = /64. Different silicon structure → different accepted set. The shared principle: the accepted values are exactly the reachable encodings of the underlying register field(s), never a numeric range check.`
+},
+
+{id:'d4',ch:'div',type:'mc',
+ q:'The audit probe probe_aclk_divider_unvalidated originally PASSED by asserting dev.clock.aclk_div == 7 after CLOCK:ACLK:DIVIDER 7. What does its post-fix version assert?',
+ snip:`<span class="cm"># BEFORE: dev.process("CLOCK:ACLK:DIVIDER 7"); return _errors(dev)==0 and dev.clock.aclk_div==7</span>
+<span class="cm"># AFTER:</span>
+<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">probe_aclk_divider_unvalidated</span>(dev):
+    <span class="str">"""ACLK divider matches DIVA's /1-/32 set (SLAU367P §3.3.3); /64 is rejected."""</span>
+    dev.process(<span class="str">"CLOCK:ACLK:DIVIDER 64"</span>)
+    <span class="kw">return</span> _errors(dev) == <span class="num">1</span> <span class="kw">and</span> dev.clock.aclk_div == <span class="num">1</span>`,
+ opts:[
+  {t:'That /64 queues exactly one error and aclk_div keeps its default of 1 — the probe now verifies compliant rejection instead of documenting the acceptance bug',c:true, w:'Correct. Audit probes always assert the behaviour that SHOULD hold. Before the fix the probe demonstrated the defect (any integer accepted — even the non-encodable 7); after it, the probe pins DIVA\'s /1-/32 bound and the reject-without-side-effects contract.'},
+  {t:'That /7 is now accepted but /64 is not',c:false,w:'7 is rejected too — it has no DIVA encoding. The rewritten probe uses 64 as its out-of-set representative; the accepted set is only {1,2,4,8,16,32}.'},
+  {t:'That /64 is accepted but clamped to /32',c:false,w:'No clamping: _errors(dev) == 1 and aclk_div == 1 means rejected outright with prior state preserved.'},
+  {t:'The probe was deleted because ACLK dividers no longer need auditing',c:false,w:'It was rescored, not removed — flipped probes become permanent compliance checks, the same pattern as the strict-xfail tests.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+// ─── CHANNEL: code ────────────────────────────────────────────
+{id:'c1',ch:'code',type:'mc',
+ q:'What was the exact defect in _clk_mclk_freq before commit 701853d?',
+ snip:`<span class="cm"># BEFORE</span>
+freq = self._dco_actual() / self.clock.mclk_div
+<span class="cm"># AFTER</span>
+freq = self._clock_source_frequency(self.clock.mclk_source) / self.clock.mclk_div`,
+ opts:[
