+    {l:'a wrong number propagates silently; an error stops the test — downstream math inherits the lie',t:['silent','propagat','inherit','downstream','confident','no error','trust']}],
+ m:`Old behaviour: _clk_mclk_freq computed _dco_actual()/mclk_div, so after CLOCK:MCLK:SOURCE LFXT the query still reported "1000000" while the modeled clock tree — and real silicon so configured — runs MCLK at 32768 Hz. That is a factor of 1000000/32768 ≈ 30.5×.
+Why worse than an error: an error is loud — the test fails at the point of the defect. A confidently wrong number is trusted by everything downstream: test assertions cross-checking firmware clock math, interval calculations, baud divisor checks. Worse, wrong-in-the-same-direction pairs cancel: firmware that also wrongly assumes the DCO agrees with the simulator perfectly, so the SIL suite certifies the bug (the exact cancelling-errors structure as the ADC\'s hardcoded 3.3 V in lesson 0009). The fix makes the query derive from the selected source, so disagreement between firmware assumption and configured reality becomes observable in simulation.`
