+{id:'s4',ch:'sil',type:'mc',
+ q:'Why must FREQUENCY? resolve the tree at query time instead of caching a frequency when the source is selected?',
+ opts:[
+  {t:'Because the answer depends on state that changes after selection: CLOCK:DCO:FREQUENCY writes, divider changes, and CORNER-profile DCO drift are all applied at read time',c:true, w:'Correct. A snapshot taken at SOURCE-selection time goes stale three ways: the DCO can be retuned afterwards (8e6 must show through an already-selected DCO route), dividers change independently, and _dco_actual() applies faults.dco_factor() live. Derived values must be computed from current configuration, every read.'},
+  {t:'Caching would be too memory-hungry',c:false,w:'One float per clock is negligible. The problem is correctness — a cache is a second copy of derivable truth that goes stale.'},
+  {t:'SCPI forbids devices from storing computed values',c:false,w:'SCPI says nothing of the sort. The reason is the dependency structure: the answer is a function of several independently mutable inputs.'},
+  {t:'It must not — the model does cache, and invalidates on every write',c:false,w:'There is no cache anywhere: _clk_*_freq calls _clock_source_frequency() fresh on each query. Recompute-on-read needs no invalidation logic at all, which is why it is the simpler and safer design.'},
