+ opts:[
+  {t:'pullup = 0x10, pulldown = 0x20',c:true, w:'Correct. REN covers pins 5,4. OUT bit 4 = 1 → pin 4 pulls up: 0x30 &amp; 0x96 = 0x10. OUT bit 5 = 0 → pin 5 pulls down: 0x30 &amp; ~0x96 &amp; 0xFF = 0x30 &amp; 0x69 = 0x20. Union is 0x30 = REN, intersection is 0.'},
+  {t:'pullup = 0x20, pulldown = 0x10',c:false,w:'Reversed. Check bits: 0x96 = 1001 0110 has bit 4 set (0x10) and bit 5 clear (0x20). OUT=1 selects pull-UP, so pullup gets 0x10.'},
+  {t:'pullup = 0x30, pulldown = 0x00',c:false,w:'Only if OUT had both bits 5 and 4 set. Bit 5 of 0x96 is 0, so pin 5 pulls down.'},
+  {t:'pullup = 0x96, pulldown = 0x69',c:false,w:'Both views are masked by REN — bits without an enabled resistor never appear in either view. Only pins 5 and 4 can show up.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'d2',ch:'derive',type:'w',
+ q:'Write the two derived-view expressions exactly as the GPIOPort properties implement them, and explain why the trailing "&amp; 0xFF" is required in Python but would be unnecessary in C on a uint8_t.',
+ c:[{l:'pullup = resistor_enable &amp; output &amp; 0xFF',t:['resistor_enable & output','ren & out','ren&out']},
+    {l:'pulldown = resistor_enable &amp; ~output &amp; 0xFF',t:['~output','~out','& ~']},
+    {l:'Python ~ is unbounded / negative (two\'s complement of an infinite int)',t:['unbounded','infinite','negative','-','arbitrary precision','bignum']},
+    {l:'0xFF clamps the view to the port\'s 8 lines (width discipline)',t:['0xff','8 bits','8-bit','clamp','width','eight']}],
+ m:`pullup   = self.resistor_enable & self.output & 0xFF
+pulldown = self.resistor_enable & ~self.output & 0xFF
+The & 0xFF matters because Python ints are unbounded: ~0xA5 is -166, conceptually an infinite string of leading 1-bits, not 0x5A. Without the mask, pulldown could carry set bits above bit 7 whenever resistor_enable did (and the property must defend against that). ANDing with 0xFF clamps the result to the port's 8 physical I/O lines — the same explicit-width discipline lesson 0010 required for 16-bit timer registers. In C, a uint8_t variable is physically 8 bits, so ~out is already truncated by the type; Python has no such type, so the model must enforce width by hand.`
+},
+
+{id:'d3',ch:'derive',type:'mc',
+ q:'For ANY values of resistor_enable and output, what is pullup &amp; pulldown?',
+ opts:[
+  {t:'0 — identically: (REN &amp; OUT) &amp; (REN &amp; ~OUT) contains OUT &amp; ~OUT, which is always zero',c:true, w:'Correct. The mutual exclusion is algebraic, not enforced by validation code. No sequence of writes can make both views claim the same pin — the inexpressible both-pulls state became unrepresentable, which is stronger than being checked.'},
+  {t:'resistor_enable — every enabled pin appears in both',c:false,w:'REN is their UNION (pullup | pulldown), not their intersection. Each enabled pin appears in exactly one view, selected by its OUT bit.'},
+  {t:'It depends on the write order of PULLUP/PULLDOWN commands',c:false,w:'Write order determines which view each pin lands in, but the intersection is zero regardless — OUT bit is either 0 or 1, never both.'},
+  {t:'0xFF when all resistors are enabled',c:false,w:'Even with REN = 0xFF, each pin\'s OUT bit routes it to exactly one view. The AND is still 0.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'d4',ch:'derive',type:'mc',
+ q:'And what is pullup | pulldown, for any state?',
+ opts:[
+  {t:'resistor_enable — (REN &amp; OUT) | (REN &amp; ~OUT) = REN &amp; (OUT | ~OUT) = REN',c:true, w:'Correct. Every enabled resistor pulls exactly one way, so the two views partition REN. Together with pullup &amp; pulldown == 0, the pair of invariants fully characterises the fix: a partition of the enable mask, keyed by OUT.'},
+  {t:'output — the views reconstruct PxOUT',c:false,w:'Bits of OUT on pins without an enabled resistor appear in neither view. You cannot recover OUT from the views, only OUT &amp; REN.'},
+  {t:'0xFF — all pins are always either up or down',c:false,w:'Pins with REN clear have NO pull at all — they float (or read the injected value). Only enabled pins appear.'},
+  {t:'Undefined — properties can disagree transiently between writes',c:false,w:'Properties are computed at read time from one consistent snapshot of REN/OUT; there is no transient window, which is exactly why they replaced stored copies.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'d5',ch:'derive',type:'w',
+ q:'Define "inexpressible state", give issue #21\'s concrete example, and explain why a simulator that can represent one silently voids SIL test results.',
+ c:[{l:'a state with no hardware encoding / silicon cannot reach',t:['no encoding','cannot express','cannot reach','unreachable','no silicon','not exist','physically impossible']},
+    {l:'example: pull-up AND pull-down active on one pin (old independent masks)',t:['both pull','pullup and pulldown','same pin','simultaneous']},
+    {l:'tests that pass in such a state certify nothing about hardware',t:['certif','void','meaningless','blesses','pass in a state','not valid on hardware']},
+    {l:'ties to reachable state space (lesson 0010)',t:['reachable','state space','subset']}],
+ m:`An inexpressible state is a simulator configuration that no combination of register writes can produce on the real part — it lies outside the hardware's reachable state space (lesson 0010's term).
+Issue #21's example: with independent pullup/pulldown masks, "GPIO:PORT1:PULLUP 0x01" then "GPIO:PORT1:PULLDOWN 0x01" left both masks with bit 0 set — one pin pulling toward VCC and GND at once. On silicon there is one PxREN bit and one PxOUT direction bit per pin: both-pulls has no encoding.
+Why it voids SIL results: the whole value of software-in-the-loop is that behaviour observed in simulation transfers to hardware. A test that runs (and passes) while the simulated device sits in an unreachable configuration verifies firmware against physics that will never occur — the pass is evidence of nothing, and the miswritten firmware config that created the state ships unflagged. The fix makes the state unrepresentable: pullup & pulldown == 0 by construction, so the bug class cannot even be expressed to test.`
+},
+
+// ─── CHANNEL: alias ───────────────────────────────────────────
+{id:'a1',ch:'alias',type:'mc',
+ q:'Fresh device. GPIO:PORT1:PULLUP 0x03, then GPIO:PORT1:PULLDOWN 0x01. What are resistor_enable and output afterwards?',
+ snip:`<span class="cm"># _gpio_pullup write path:            # _gpio_pulldown write path:</span>
+p.resistor_enable |= mask             p.resistor_enable |= mask
+p.output |= mask                      p.output &amp;= ~mask`,
+ opts:[
+  {t:'resistor_enable = 0x03, output = 0x02 — pin 1 pulls up, pin 0 was retargeted to pull down',c:true, w:'Correct. PULLUP 0x03: REN 0x00→0x03, OUT 0x00→0x03. PULLDOWN 0x01: REN |= 0x01 (still 0x03), OUT &amp;= ~0x01 → 0x02. Queries: PULLUP? = 0x03 &amp; 0x02 = 0x02; PULLDOWN? = 0x03 &amp; ~0x02 = 0x01. Last write per pin wins.'},
+  {t:'resistor_enable = 0x02, output = 0x03 — the PULLDOWN write disabled pin 0\'s resistor',c:false,w:'PULLDOWN never clears REN — it ORs the mask in (the resistor stays connected) and clears the OUT bit (points it at GND).'},
+  {t:'resistor_enable = 0x03, output = 0x03 — PULLDOWN only records intent in its own mask',c:false,w:'There is no "own mask" anymore. The alias composes the real registers, and its OUT &amp;= ~mask is what flips pin 0 to pull-down.'},
+  {t:'An error is queued — the pin already has a pull configured',c:false,w:'No error: reconfiguring a pull is a legal register write on hardware too. The second command simply retargets pin 0.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'a2',ch:'alias',type:'w',
+ q:'Describe exactly what a PULLUP write and a PULLDOWN write do to stored state after 4cb71a1, what their queries return, and why the commit message calls them aliases that "compose the real registers".',
+ c:[{l:'PULLUP mask: REN |= mask; OUT |= mask',t:['|= mask','or the mask','out |=','sets ren and out']},
+    {l:'PULLDOWN mask: REN |= mask; OUT &amp;= ~mask',t:['&= ~','clears out','clear the out']},
+    {l:'queries return the derived views REN &amp; OUT / REN &amp; ~OUT',t:['derived','ren & out','ren & ~out','property','view']},
+    {l:'no state of their own — every effect lands in REN/OUT',t:['no state','no own','compose','single source','real registers','deprecated']}],
+ m:`Write paths (mask = int(cmd.params[0], 0) & 0xFF):
+- PULLUP mask: p.resistor_enable |= mask; p.output |= mask — connect the resistor(s) AND point them at VCC.
+- PULLDOWN mask: p.resistor_enable |= mask; p.output &= ~mask — connect the resistor(s) AND point them at GND.
+Queries return the derived properties: PULLUP? → f"0x{p.pullup:02X}" (REN & OUT), PULLDOWN? → f"0x{p.pulldown:02X}" (REN & ~OUT & 0xFF).
+"Compose the real registers" means the aliases own no storage: every observable effect is a combination of writes to resistor_enable and output — the two registers SLAU367P documents. Old scripts keep working (backward compatibility is the only reason the commands survive, marked Deprecated in their docstrings), but they can no longer construct a state the registers can't hold: PULLDOWN after PULLUP retargets pins instead of contradicting them.`
+},
+
+{id:'a3',ch:'alias',type:'mc',
+ q:'Why did commit 4cb71a1 keep PULLUP/PULLDOWN at all instead of deleting them along with the stored masks?',
+ opts:[
+  {t:'Backward compatibility — existing SCPI scripts and tests use them; re-expressed as compositions of REN/OUT they stay correct while impossible states stay impossible',c:true, w:'Correct. Deleting them would break every existing user for no semantic gain. The deprecation pattern: keep the old surface, reroute it through the new single source of truth, document the preferred replacement (RESISTOR:ENABLE + OUTPUT).'},
+  {t:'SLAU367P documents PULLUP and PULLDOWN commands',c:false,w:'It documents registers (PxREN/PxOUT), not SCPI commands. The vendor-doc diff actually classifies PULLUP/PULLDOWN as MISSING_FROM_GENERATED — simulator conveniences with no doc basis.'},
+  {t:'The GUI depends on them and cannot be updated',c:false,w:'Nothing in the commit says that; the motivation in the docstrings is the alias/deprecation relationship to RESISTOR:ENABLE.'},
+  {t:'They are faster than separate REN and OUTPUT writes',c:false,w:'Convenience, not performance — one command instead of two is ergonomics; the reason they SURVIVE is compatibility.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'a4',ch:'alias',type:'mc',
+ q:'A script sends GPIO:PORT1:PULLUP 0x80 and later switches pin 7 to output. Which hidden side effect of the alias matters here?',
+ opts:[
+  {t:'The alias set OUT bit 7, so the moment pin 7 becomes an output it drives HIGH — PxOUT resumed its drive-level role',c:true, w:'Correct. PULLUP writes OUT as well as REN, because on hardware the pull-up IS "OUT bit = 1". Register repurposing cuts both ways: that same latch is the drive level once PxDIR flips. The identical write sequence on a real FR5994 also drives high.'},
+  {t:'The alias set DIR bit 7, making the pin an output immediately',c:false,w:'Neither alias touches direction — only resistor_enable and output.'},
+  {t:'The resistor keeps pulling while the pin drives, causing a simulated short',c:false,w:'The model\'s input read puts driven pins entirely under OUT &amp; DIR; there is no contention model. The interesting side effect is the OUT bit itself.'},
+  {t:'Nothing — aliases only affect derived views, not stored registers',c:false,w:'Exactly backwards: aliases affect ONLY stored registers (REN, OUT); the views are derived from them.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'a5',ch:'alias',type:'w',
+ q:'probe_gpio_pull_both: quote (approximately) its return expression before and after 4cb71a1, and explain what flipping the expected value means for how audit probes are written.',
+ c:[{l:'before: pullup &amp; pulldown &amp; 0x01 == 0x01 (both active = bug reproduced)',t:['== 0x01','both','reproduc','demonstrat']},
+    {l:'after: pullup &amp; pulldown &amp; 0x01 == 0 (mutually exclusive)',t:['== 0','mutual','exclusive']},
+    {l:'same stimulus (PULLUP 0x01; PULLDOWN 0x01) both times',t:['same command','same stimulus','same sequence','0x01']},
+    {l:'probes assert the compliant behaviour, so they flip when fixed',t:['compliant','assert the correct','docstring','flip','sla','12.2.4']}],
+ m:`Stimulus (unchanged): dev.process("GPIO:PORT1:PULLUP 0x01"); dev.process("GPIO:PORT1:PULLDOWN 0x01").
+Before: return dev.ports[1].pullup & dev.ports[1].pulldown & 0x01 == 0x01 — docstring "Pull-up and pull-down can be enabled simultaneously on one pin." The probe returned True when the BUG was present, documenting the non-compliance.
+After: return dev.ports[1].pullup & dev.ports[1].pulldown & 0x01 == 0 — docstring "Pull-up and pull-down are mutually exclusive on one pin (one PxREN bit)."
+The convention this shows: audit probes always encode the behaviour SLAU367P specifies for the current verdict. While a finding is open, the probe verifies the defect is still reproducible (so the report can't go stale); when the fix lands, the probe and docstring flip to assert compliance and become a permanent regression guard. Same stimulus, opposite expectation — the diff of the probe IS the specification of the fix.`
+},
+
+// ─── CHANNEL: input ───────────────────────────────────────────
+{id:'i1',ch:'input',type:'w',
+ q:'Reproduce the body of _gpio_inp after 4cb71a1 (four mask lines plus the OR). For each term, say which class of pin it captures and which register wins there.',
+ c:[{l:'driven = output &amp; direction (output pins read back OUT)',t:['output & direction','out & dir','driven']},
+    {l:'undriven_mask = ~direction &amp; 0xFF',t:['~direction','~dir','undriven']},
+    {l:'pulled = output &amp; resistor_enable &amp; undriven (REN input pins read the pull level = OUT bit)',t:['resistor_enable','ren','pull level','pulled']},
+    {l:'injected = input &amp; undriven &amp; ~resistor_enable (no-pull input pins read injection)',t:['~resistor','~ren','inject']},
+    {l:'effective = driven | pulled | injected',t:['driven | pulled','or','union','effective']}],
+ m:`driven        = p.output & p.direction
+undriven_mask = ~p.direction & 0xFF
+pulled        = p.output & p.resistor_enable & undriven_mask
+injected      = p.input & undriven_mask & ~p.resistor_enable
+effective     = driven | pulled | injected      # returned as f"0x{effective & 0xFF:02X}"
+Three disjoint pin classes, each owned by one source:
+1. Output pins (DIR=1): read back PxOUT — "driven".
+2. Input pins with a pull (DIR=0, REN=1): read PxOUT's bit too, but with a different meaning — it is the pull level (0=pulled low, 1=pulled high). This is the pull-aware read §12.2.4 requires.
+3. Input pins without a pull (DIR=0, REN=0): read the externally injected value, as before the commit.
+The masks partition all 8 pins, so the OR never double-assigns a bit. Note injected is masked by ~resistor_enable: on a REN pin the pull always wins because the model has no drive-strength arbitration.`
+},
+
+{id:'i2',ch:'input',type:'mc',
+ q:'direction = 0xF0, output = 0xA5, resistor_enable = 0x0C, injected input = 0x03. What does GPIO:PORT1:INPUT? return?',
+ snip:`driven   = OUT &amp; DIR
+undriven = ~DIR &amp; 0xFF
+pulled   = OUT &amp; REN &amp; undriven
+injected = IN &amp; undriven &amp; ~REN
+<span class="kw">return</span> driven | pulled | injected`,
+ opts:[
+  {t:'0xA7',c:true, w:'Correct. driven = 0xA5 &amp; 0xF0 = 0xA0. undriven = 0x0F. pulled = 0xA5 &amp; 0x0C &amp; 0x0F = 0x04 (pin 2 pull-up reads 1; pin 3 pull-down reads 0). injected = 0x03 &amp; 0x0F &amp; ~0x0C = 0x03. 0xA0 | 0x04 | 0x03 = 0xA7.'},
+  {t:'0xA3',c:false,w:'0xA3 = driven | injected only — that is the PRE-4cb71a1 result, which ignored pulls. Pin 2\'s pull-up (REN bit 2, OUT bit 2 = 1) adds 0x04.'},
+  {t:'0xA5',c:false,w:'0xA5 is OUT itself. OUT only shows through fully on output pins (high nibble); on the input nibble only the REN-enabled bits of OUT appear (0x04), the rest comes from injection.'},
+  {t:'0x07',c:false,w:'0x07 drops the driven high nibble. Output pins read back OUT &amp; DIR = 0xA0 — PxIN reflects driven levels too.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'i3',ch:'input',type:'mc',
+ q:'The flipped audit probe: DIRECTION 0x00, RESISTOR:ENABLE 0xFF, OUTPUT 0xFF — what must INPUT? return for probe_gpio_input_ignores_pulls to report compliance?',
+ opts:[
+  {t:'"0xFF" — every pin is an undriven input with an enabled pull-up, so every pin reads high',c:true, w:'Correct. pulled = OUT &amp; REN &amp; undriven = 0xFF &amp; 0xFF &amp; 0xFF = 0xFF. Before the fix the same setup (via PULLUP 0xFF) returned "0x00" because reads ignored pulls entirely — the PARTIAL finding on GPIO:PORTn:INPUT.'},
+  {t:'"0x00" — input pins with no injected value always read low',c:false,w:'That was the OLD, non-compliant behaviour. §12.2.4: an undriven input with a pull-up reads high — the whole point of the resistor.'},
+  {t:'Whatever was last injected with GPIO:PORT1:INPUT',c:false,w:'Injection is masked out on REN-enabled pins (injected = IN &amp; undriven &amp; ~REN). With REN = 0xFF, injection is invisible.'},
+  {t:'An error — OUTPUT writes are illegal while all pins are inputs',c:false,w:'Writing PxOUT on input pins is not only legal, it is HOW you select pull direction (Table 12-1). No error is queued.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'i4',ch:'input',type:'mc',
+ q:'Pin 3 is an input with REN set and OUT bit 3 = 0 (pull-down). A test sends GPIO:PORT1:INPUT 0x08, injecting a high on pin 3. What does INPUT? bit 3 read?',
+ opts:[
+  {t:'0 — the injected bit is masked by ~resistor_enable; on a REN pin the pull level always wins because the model has no drive-strength arbitration',c:true, w:'Correct. injected = IN &amp; undriven &amp; ~REN kills bit 3; pulled = OUT &amp; REN &amp; undriven contributes OUT bit 3 = 0. Real hardware would let a strong external driver overpower the weak pull — Silmulator deliberately does not model drive strength, so simulating "external device overrides the pull" requires clearing that pin\'s REN bit for the duration.'},
+  {t:'1 — injection models an external driver, which overpowers a weak pull',c:false,w:'That is what real silicon does, but not this model: _gpio_inp masks injection with ~resistor_enable. Know the divergence before writing button/press tests.'},
+  {t:'1 — the injection write also sets OUT bit 3',c:false,w:'GPIO:PORTn:INPUT writes only p.input (the injected value); it never touches output or resistor_enable.'},
+  {t:'SCPI error -221 — injecting onto a pulled pin is a settings conflict',c:false,w:'No error is queued; the injected value is simply not visible while REN is set on that pin.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'i5',ch:'input',type:'w',
+ q:'SIL test for an active-low button on P1.3 with an internal pull-up: give the SCPI configuration, the expected idle read, and the sequence that simulates a press — including why plain injection is not enough.',
+ c:[{l:'config: PIN:DIRECTION 3,INPUT + RESISTOR:ENABLE 0x08 + OUTPUT 0x08',t:['0x08','resistor:enable','output 0x08','direction 3']},
+    {l:'idle: INPUT? bit 3 reads 1 (pull-up)',t:['reads 1','bit 3 = 1','high','0x08']},
+    {l:'injection of 0 is masked while REN is set (~resistor_enable)',t:['masked','ignored','~ren','~resistor','pull wins','not enough']},
+    {l:'press: clear REN (RESISTOR:ENABLE 0x00), inject 0, read 0; restore after',t:['clear ren','resistor:enable 0x00','disable','restore','re-enable']}],
+ m:`Idle configuration (matches firmware P1DIR &= ~BIT3; P1REN |= BIT3; P1OUT |= BIT3):
+GPIO:PORT1:PIN:DIRECTION 3,INPUT
+GPIO:PORT1:RESISTOR:ENABLE 0x08
+GPIO:PORT1:OUTPUT 0x08
+GPIO:PORT1:INPUT?              → bit 3 = 1 (pulled : 0x08 &amp; 0x08 &amp; undriven) — unpressed reads high. ✓
+Simulating the press: GPIO:PORT1:INPUT 0x00 alone does NOT work — injected = IN & undriven & ~REN, so pin 3's injection is masked while its REN bit is set; INPUT? still shows 1. The model treats REN pins as undriven (no drive-strength arbitration), whereas a real button is a hard short to GND that overpowers the ~35 kΩ pull.
+Workaround: model the press as "strong driver replaces the pull" —
+GPIO:PORT1:RESISTOR:ENABLE 0x00   (disconnect the resistor for the press)
+GPIO:PORT1:INPUT 0x00             → INPUT? bit 3 = 0 — press observed
+…and restore RESISTOR:ENABLE 0x08 (and re-inject) on release. Document the divergence if the firmware under test reads PxREN itself.`
+},
+
+// ─── CHANNEL: valid ───────────────────────────────────────────
+{id:'v1',ch:'valid',type:'mc',
+ q:'A test sends GPIO:PORT1:PIN:VALUE 12,1. Per the flipped probe, what is the device state afterwards?',
+ snip:`<span class="cm"># scripts/audit_msp430.py — AFTER 4cb71a1</span>
+<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">probe_gpio_pin_index_unvalidated</span>(dev):
+    <span class="str">"""Pin numbers beyond 7 are rejected with -222 on an 8-bit port."""</span>
+    dev.process(<span class="str">"GPIO:PORT1:PIN:VALUE 12,1"</span>)
+    <span class="kw">return</span> _errors(dev) == <span class="num">1</span> <span class="kw">and</span> dev.ports[<span class="num">1</span>].output == <span class="num">0</span>`,
+ opts:[
+  {t:'One SCPI error (-222, "Pin index out of range (0-7): 12") is queued and output is untouched at 0',c:true, w:'Correct. _validate_pin raises before any shift or mutation happens — validate-then-mutate. The probe asserts BOTH halves: exactly one error, and no state corruption.'},
+  {t:'Bit 12 is set: output becomes 0x1000',c:false,w:'That was the OLD behaviour — (1 &lt;&lt; 12) stored into an "8-bit" register, corrupting every later mask read. The audit called it ACCEPTS_INVALID.'},
+  {t:'The index wraps modulo 8, setting pin 4',c:false,w:'No wrapping. A pin that does not exist is an error, not a remapping — SLAU367P §12.2: each port has exactly 8 I/O lines.'},
+  {t:'Error -224 is queued — illegal parameter value',c:false,w:'-224 is for tokens outside a permitted value SET (like PIN:DIRECTION 0,BANANA). A numeric index outside its RANGE is -222, "Data out of range".'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'v2',ch:'valid',type:'mc',
+ q:'Why does pin 12 draw error -222 but PIN:DIRECTION 0,BANANA draw -224?',
+ opts:[
+  {t:'-222 "Data out of range" = right type, numeric value outside 0-7; -224 "Illegal parameter value" = token outside the permitted value set (a pin is INPUT or OUTPUT, no third mode)',c:true, w:'Correct. Same split as elsewhere in the model: register-width violations use -222 (lesson 0010\'s timer writes), value-set violations use -224 (ADC:REFERENCE BANANA, divider /128). The SCPI error class encodes WHY the parameter is unacceptable.'},
+  {t:'They are interchangeable; the codes were picked arbitrarily per handler',c:false,w:'They follow the SCPI standard\'s classification and the codebase\'s existing precedent — numeric range vs value-set membership.'},
+  {t:'-222 is a warning, -224 halts command processing',c:false,w:'Both queue an error and leave state unchanged; neither halts anything. The difference is meaning, not severity.'},
+  {t:'BANANA fails integer parsing, which is always -224',c:false,w:'Type/parse failures are the -1xx family (e.g. -104 for the VEREF voltage). BANANA parses fine as a token — it is simply not in {INPUT, OUTPUT, IN, OUT, 0, 1}.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'v3',ch:'valid',type:'w',
+ q:'Write _validate_pin from memory (decorator, signature, check, error), and state which handlers call it and at what point in their flow.',
+ c:[{l:'@staticmethod, takes pin: int, returns None',t:['staticmethod','pin: int','none']},
+    {l:'if not 0 <= pin <= 7 → raise',t:['0 <= pin <= 7','0-7','not 0','chained']},
+    {l:'ScpiError(-222, "Pin index out of range (0-7): {pin}")',t:['-222','out of range']},
+    {l:'called by _gpio_pin_dir and _gpio_pin_val, right after parsing, before any read or mutation',t:['pin_dir','pin_val','before','both','first','prior to']}],
+ m:`@staticmethod
+def _validate_pin(pin: int) -> None:
+    """Reject pin indexes outside an 8-bit port's 8 I/O lines (SLAU367P §12.2)."""
+    if not 0 <= pin <= 7:
+        raise ScpiError(-222, f"Pin index out of range (0-7): {pin}")
+It is a @staticmethod because it touches no instance state — pure validation. Both per-pin handlers call it immediately after pin = int(cmd.params[0]) and before anything else: _gpio_pin_dir (which also gained the -224 rejection for illegal mode strings) and _gpio_pin_val. Placement matters: the check runs before the query branch AND before any (1 << pin) shift, so neither reads nor writes can ever touch a nonexistent line — rejection leaves state exactly as it was (the probe asserts output == 0 afterwards). One shared validator, two call sites: the range rule lives in one place.`
+},
+
+{id:'v4',ch:'valid',type:'mc',
+ q:'Which four tests in test_msp430_edge_cases.py lost their @pytest.mark.xfail(strict=True) markers in commit 4cb71a1?',
