+{id:'w2',ch:'width',type:'w',
+ q:'SLAU367P §4.3 says R0-R15 are physically 20 bits on CPUX. State the maximum register value, the size of the address space it implies, and how much of the FR5994\'s 256-KB FRAM is unreachable with only 16 bits.',
+ c:[{l:'max value 0xFFFFF = 1,048,575',t:['0xfffff','1048575','1,048,575']},
+    {l:'2^20 = 1 MB address space (0x00000-0xFFFFF)',t:['1 mb','1mb','2^20','1048576','megabyte']},
+    {l:'FRAM split: 48 KB below 0x10000, 208 KB above',t:['48','208','0x10000','0x43fff','extended']},
+    {l:'~81% of FRAM needs 20-bit addresses',t:['81','most of','majority','cannot','unreachable']}],
+ m:`Max register value: 0xFFFFF = 1,048,575 (20 bits of flip-flops).
+Address space: 2^20 = 1,048,576 addresses = 1 MB, 0x00000-0xFFFFF.
+FR5994 FRAM (SLASE54D memory map): 48 KB at 0x4000-0xFF7F sits below the 64-KB line; the other 208 KB is extended FRAM at 0x10000-0x43FFF. 48 + 208 = 256 KB, so about 81% of the part's FRAM is invisible to a 16-bit pointer. That is why masking register writes with 0xFFFF was not a cosmetic bug: every pointer into extended FRAM was silently relocated into the wrong 64-KB page.`
