+{id:'w5',ch:'width',type:'w',
+ q:'Explain the binary-compatibility contract of SLAU367P §4.3: what do 16-bit (.W) register writes and reads do with bits 19:16 on real CPUX hardware, and why does that let unmodified MSP430 binaries run correctly?',
+ c:[{l:'.W writes CLEAR bits 19:16',t:['clear','zero','19:16','cleared']},
+    {l:'16-bit reads return only the low word',t:['low word','15:0','invisible','only the low']},
+    {l:'extended instructions (MOVA/CALLA/.A) use all 20 bits',t:['mova','calla','.a','extended','20 bits','bra']},
+    {l:'old code can neither set nor observe the upper bits → identical behaviour',t:['cannot see','never sees','identical','unchanged','binary compat','opt in','opts in']}],
+ m:`On CPUX hardware:
+- A 16-bit (.W) write to any register clears bits 19:16 — MOV.W #0x2345, R4 leaves R4 = 0x02345, never a stale upper nibble.
+- A 16-bit read returns bits 15:0 only; the upper 4 bits are invisible to non-extended instructions.
+- Only the extended instructions (MOVA, CALLA, BRA, the .A address-word forms) read and write the full 20 bits.
+Because legacy 16-bit code can neither set nor observe bits 19:16, an unmodified MSP430 binary produces bit-identical register behaviour on a CPUX part — that is binary compatibility. The extra width is opt-in via instructions the old ISA does not contain. Silmulator's SCPI writes behave like full-width stores masked to 20 bits; the model does not distinguish .W from .A, but it must never pretend the flip-flops above bit 15 are absent.`
+},
