+{id:'p2',ch:'pc',type:'mc',
+ q:'CPU:PC 0x123457 violates BOTH rules (too wide and odd). Which error message does the caller actually see?',
+ opts:[
+  {t:'"PC out of range (0-0xFFFFF)" — the range check runs first and raises before the alignment check executes',c:true, w:'Correct. 0x123457 = 1,193,047 > 1,048,575. The checks are sequential; raise aborts the handler, so the odd bit is never examined. probe_cpu_pc_validated uses exactly this value and asserts one error, PC unchanged.'},
+  {t:'"PC must be word-aligned (bit 0 is always 0)"',c:false,w:'The alignment check is second. The range check raises first for 0x123457, so the word-aligned message is only seen for in-range odd values like 0x4001.'},
+  {t:'Both errors are queued, one per violated rule',c:false,w:'raise aborts the handler at the first failed check — one command, at most one queued error here.'},
+  {t:'Neither — the value is masked to 0x23457, which is in range',c:false,w:'The PC handler validates and rejects; masking is the REGISTER policy. Coercing 0x123457 into 0x23457 would silently change the write\'s meaning.'},
