+{id:'p3',ch:'pc',type:'w',
+ q:'Write out the two validation rules _cpu_pc enforces after 07f1f69, the SCPI error each raises, and the hardware fact behind each rule.',
+ c:[{l:'rule 1: 0 <= v <= 0xFFFFF (20-bit space)',t:['0xfffff','range','20-bit','20 bit']},
+    {l:'rule 2: v & 1 == 0 (even / word-aligned)',t:['v & 1','odd','even','word-align','bit 0']},
+    {l:'both raise ScpiError -222',t:['-222']},
+    {l:'hardware: 1-MB address space; PC bit 0 hardwired 0 (SLAU367P §4.3.1)',t:['hardwired','always 0','1 mb','slau367','4.3.1','instructions are word']}],
+ m:`Rule 1 — range: the value must satisfy 0 <= v <= 0xFFFFF, else ScpiError(-222, "PC out of range (0-0xFFFFF)"). Hardware fact: CPUX addresses are 20 bits — 1 MB, 0x00000-0xFFFFF. There is no 21st address bit.
+Rule 2 — alignment: v & 1 must be 0, else ScpiError(-222, "PC must be word-aligned (bit 0 is always 0)"). Hardware fact: SLAU367P §4.3.1 — instructions are word-aligned and bit 0 of the PC is hardwired to 0; no instruction fetch, jump, or debugger poke can create an odd PC.
+The checks run in that order, so a value violating both (0x123457) reports the range message. Both use -222 "Data out of range", matching the policy lesson 0010 established for the 16-bit timer registers.`
