+{id:'r3',ch:'reset',type:'mc',
+ q:'Why did the audit score the old "self._pc = 0xC000" reset as NON_COMPLIANT — what two distinct things were wrong with it?',
+ opts:[
+  {t:'Reset should FETCH the PC from the vector at 0xFFFE (mechanism), and 0xC000 is not even the right constant for the FR5994, whose code FRAM starts at 0x4000 (value)',c:true, w:'Correct. Mechanism: a real BOR/POR/PUC ends with a memory read of the reset vector — the entry point belongs to the firmware image, not the silicon. Value: 0xC000 is the flash start of older, smaller MSP430s; the audit\'s wording is "0xC000 is not FR5994-specific (code FRAM starts at 0x4000)".'},
+  {t:'Only that 0xC000 is odd and therefore an illegal PC',c:false,w:'0xC000 is even and perfectly legal as a PC value. The problems were the hardcoded MECHANISM (no vector fetch) and the wrong-part CONSTANT.'},
+  {t:'Only that the constant should have been 0x4000',c:false,w:'Swapping one constant for another would still fail the probe: probe_cpu_reset_vector_pc requires that a WRITTEN vector (0xC000 via MEMORY:WRITE) actually redirects reset. The fetch mechanism is the core fix; 0x4000 is only the unwritten-vector fallback.'},
+  {t:'CPU:RESET should not touch the PC at all',c:false,w:'Reset absolutely must set the PC — that is its defining effect. The question is WHERE the value comes from: the vector word, not a literal in the Python source.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'r4',ch:'reset',type:'w',
+ q:'The PC is 20 bits wide, yet reset can never land above 0xFFFF. Explain why that is faithful to hardware, and how code in extended FRAM (0x10000+) ever gets executed.',
+ c:[{l:'MSP430X interrupt/reset vectors are still 16-bit words',t:['16-bit word','16 bit','vectors are','stayed 16']},
+    {l:'(hi << 8) | lo has a ceiling of 0xFFFF',t:['0xffff','ceiling','cannot exceed','max']},
+    {l:'entry point must live in the lower 64 KB',t:['lower 64','below 0x10000','64 kb','64kb']},
+    {l:'upper FRAM reached after startup via CALLA/BRA (20-bit targets)',t:['calla','bra','extended instruction','after startup','20-bit target']}],
+ m:`MSP430X widened the registers and the address space but kept the interrupt/reset vector table as 16-bit words (the table sits at 0xFF80-0xFFFF). _reset_vector_pc() mirrors this exactly: ((hi or 0) << 8) | (lo or 0) is built from two bytes and mathematically cannot exceed 0xFFFF.
+Consequently the reset entry point — and every interrupt service routine entry — must live in the lower 64 KB. That is a real constraint linkers enforce on FR5994 projects.
+Code in extended FRAM (0x10000-0x43FFF, 208 KB of the FR5994\'s 256 KB) is reached only after startup, using the extended instructions whose operands carry full 20-bit addresses: CALLA, BRA, and the .A forms. A model whose reset could land at 0x28000 would ACCEPT a state real silicon cannot produce.`
+},
+
+{id:'r5',ch:'reset',type:'mc',
+ q:'Only MEMORY:WRITE 0xFFFE,0x34 has been executed (0xFFFF untouched). What PC does the next CPU:RESET produce?',
+ opts:[
+  {t:'0x0034 — the guard needs BOTH bytes unwritten for the default; the missing high byte is treated as 0',c:true, w:'Correct. lo = 0x34, hi = None → the "lo is None and hi is None" guard is false, so the default is skipped and PC = ((None or 0) << 8) | 0x34 = 0x0034. A half-written vector is honoured as-is — the model does not "fix up" partial state.'},
+  {t:'0x4000 — the default applies whenever the vector is incomplete',c:false,w:'The default requires lo is None AND hi is None. One written byte disables it — check the boolean carefully.'},
+  {t:'0x3400 — the single byte is taken as the high byte',c:false,w:'0xFFFE is the LOW byte of the little-endian word. The high byte (0xFFFF) is the one missing, and it defaults to 0.'},
+  {t:'-222 is raised because 0x0034 is not a plausible entry point',c:false,w:'_reset_vector_pc() performs no validation — like hardware, reset loads whatever the vector holds. 0x0034 is even and in range anyway.'},
+ ]
