+// ─── CHANNEL: code ────────────────────────────────────────────
+{id:'c1',ch:'code',type:'mc',
+ q:'What exactly does the renamed audit probe probe_cpu_reg_20bit assert?',
+ snip:`<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">probe_cpu_reg_20bit</span>(dev: <span class="cls">MSP430</span>) -> bool:
+    <span class="str">"""CPUX registers are 20-bit; the model masks writes to 20 bits (0xFFFFF)."""</span>
+    dev.process(<span class="str">"CPU:REGISTER 4,0x12345"</span>)
+    <span class="kw">return</span> dev.process(<span class="str">"CPU:REGISTER? 4"</span>) == <span class="str">"0x12345"</span>`,
+ opts:[
+  {t:'Writing 0x12345 to R4 and querying it returns the exact string "0x12345" — the 20-bit value survives the round trip',c:true, w:'Correct. The probe was probe_cpu_reg_16bit, which asserted the BUG (read-back == "0x2345"). Renamed and flipped, it now asserts compliance: bits 19:16 survive. Note it compares the formatted string, so it also pins the 04X minimum-width formatting.'},
+  {t:'Writing 0x12345 queues a -222 error',c:false,w:'Register writes mask, they never reject on width. No error is involved in this probe.'},
+  {t:'The register file has exactly 16 registers',c:false,w:'The 0-15 index range is checked by a different path (-222 "Register number out of range"). This probe is about VALUE width.'},
+  {t:'R4 is cleared to 0 by CPU:RESET',c:false,w:'That is _cpu_reset behaviour (registers = [0]*16), tested elsewhere. This probe never resets.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'c2',ch:'code',type:'w',
+ q:'probe_cpu_reset_vector_pc has two phases. Describe each phase\'s SCPI traffic and expected PC, and explain why the probe needs both to pass.',
+ c:[{l:'phase 1: CPU:RESET with unwritten vector → PC == 0x4000 default',t:['0x4000','default','unwritten','default_ok']},
+    {l:'phase 2: MEMORY:WRITE 0xFFFE,0x00 + 0xFFFF,0xC0 + reset → 0xC000',t:['0xc000','0xfffe','0xffff','vector_ok','memory:write']},
+    {l:'both required: default alone could be a new constant; fetch alone misses the fallback',t:['both','constant','fallback','would pass with','and']}],
+ m:`Phase 1 (default_ok): send CPU:RESET with the vector bytes never written; assert dev._pc == 0x4000 — the FR5994 FRAM-code-start fallback.
+Phase 2 (vector_ok): write the vector little-endian — MEMORY:WRITE 0xFFFE,0x00 then MEMORY:WRITE 0xFFFF,0xC0 — reset again, and assert dev._pc == 0xC000 = (0xC0 << 8) | 0x00.
+The probe returns default_ok AND vector_ok. Both are needed because each alone can be satisfied by a wrong implementation: a model that just swapped the constant 0xC000 for 0x4000 passes phase 1 but fails phase 2 (the written vector would be ignored); a model that always fetched but defaulted to something arbitrary — or crashed on an empty memory dict — would fail phase 1. Together they pin the real semantics: reset FETCHES the vector, with a part-accurate fallback when none exists.`
+},
