+<script>
+// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
+// QUESTION BANK  (24 questions — grounded in commits 5e2ba08,
+// fa7c595 and 615b4e1 from issue #23)
+// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
+const BANK = [
+
+// ─── CHANNEL: wdt ─────────────────────────────────────────────
+{id:'w1',ch:'wdt',type:'w',
+ q:'Describe the WDTCTL register layout modeled by Silmulator (SLAU367P §24.2, Figure 24-2) and walk through _wdt_password step by step: what happens for a missing parameter, a wrong password, and a correct one?',
+ snip:`WDT_PASSWORD = <span class="num">0x5A</span>
+WDT_HOLD_BIT = <span class="num">0x80</span>
+<span class="cm"># ...</span>
+value = int(cmd.params[<span class="num">0</span>], <span class="num">0</span>) &amp; <span class="num">0xFFFF</span>
+<span class="kw">if</span> (value &gt;&gt; <span class="num">8</span>) != self.WDT_PASSWORD:
+    self.<span class="fn">_do_reset</span>()  <span class="cm"># wrong password: immediate PUC</span>
+    <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="str">""</span>
+self.wdt.hold = bool(value &amp; self.WDT_HOLD_BIT)`,
+ c:[{l:'bits 15-8 = WDTPW, must be 0x5A on every write',t:['15-8','upper byte','0x5a','wdtpw','password']},
+    {l:'bit 7 = WDTHOLD (0x80): 1 = held/stopped',t:['bit 7','0x80','wdthold','hold']},
+    {l:'missing parameter → ScpiError -109',t:['-109','missing parameter']},
+    {l:'wrong password → _do_reset() (PUC), no SCPI error',t:['_do_reset','puc','reset','no error','not an error']},
+    {l:'correct password → wdt.hold = bool(value & 0x80)',t:['wdt.hold','& 0x80','bool(','hold bit','updates']}],
+ m:`WDTCTL is 16 bits: bits 15-8 are the write password (WDTPW) and must be 0x5A on every write; bit 7 is WDTHOLD (1 = watchdog stopped, 0 = running — the PUC default); bits 6-0 (clock select, mode, counter-clear, interval select) are not modeled.
+_wdt_password:
+1. No parameter → raise ScpiError(-109, "Missing parameter: password value") — a malformed COMMAND is a SCPI error.
+2. value = int(cmd.params[0], 0) & 0xFFFF — parse hex or decimal, mask to the 16-bit register width.
+3. (value >> 8) != 0x5A → self._do_reset(): an immediate PUC through the same shared reset path *RST uses. It returns "" normally — no ScpiError, because a password violation is a hardware event, not a command error.
+4. Password correct → self.wdt.hold = bool(value & 0x80): bit 7 of the low byte drives the stored WDTHOLD state.`
+},
+
+{id:'w2',ch:'wdt',type:'mc',
+ q:'A test sends WDT:PASSWORD 0x5A80 on a fresh device. What is the outcome?',
+ opts:[
+  {t:'Accepted: 0x5A80 >> 8 = 0x5A matches, 0x5A80 & 0x80 is set — WDT:STATE? now reads HOLD',c:true, w:'Correct. This is the canonical WDTPW | WDTHOLD write every MSP430 program starts with. Password checks out, bit 7 is set, wdt.hold = True. test_hold_start_state asserts exactly this.'},
+  {t:'PUC — 0x80 in the low byte corrupts the password',c:false,w:'The password check only looks at the UPPER byte: value >> 8 = 0x5A. The low byte carries the payload bits (WDTHOLD at bit 7).'},
+  {t:'Accepted, but the watchdog keeps running',c:false,w:'0x5A80 & 0x80 = 0x80 → truthy → wdt.hold = True. The watchdog is held. 0x5A00 would keep it running.'},
+  {t:'SCPI error -224: 0x5A80 is not HOLD or START',c:false,w:'HOLD/START tokens belong to WDT:STATE. WDT:PASSWORD takes a numeric WDTCTL-style value; -224 is the WDT:STATE handler\'s error for bad tokens.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'w3',ch:'wdt',type:'mc',
+ q:'What does WDT:PASSWORD 0x5A08 do?',
+ opts:[
+  {t:'Accepted — but the watchdog is STARTED, because 0x08 & 0x80 = 0: bit 3 is not WDTHOLD',c:true, w:'Correct. The password (0x5A) is right so no PUC, but WDTHOLD is specifically bit 7 (0x80). 0x08 is bit 3 — on real silicon that\'s WDTCNTCL territory. wdt.hold = bool(0x5A08 & 0x80) = bool(0) = False.'},
+  {t:'Accepted and the watchdog is held — any nonzero low byte sets HOLD',c:false,w:'Only bit 7 counts: bool(value & WDT_HOLD_BIT). 0x08 & 0x80 = 0, so hold stays False.'},
+  {t:'PUC — the low byte must be exactly 0x00 or 0x80',c:false,w:'Only the upper byte is validated. Any low-byte pattern is accepted with a correct password; the model just reads bit 7 out of it.'},
+  {t:'SCPI error -222 — 0x08 is out of range for the hold field',c:false,w:'There is no range check on the low byte; the handler masks to 16 bits and tests one bit. No error path exists for a correct-password write.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'w4',ch:'wdt',type:'mc',
+ q:'WDT:PASSWORD 0xA55A — the value contains 0x5A. What happens and why?',
+ opts:[
+  {t:'PUC — 0xA55A >> 8 = 0xA5 ≠ 0x5A: the password must be in the UPPER byte, and 0x5A in the lower byte doesn\'t count',c:true, w:'Correct. The password field is bits 15-8. 0xA5 fails the check, so _do_reset() runs — a full PUC. (0xA5 happens to be the CSCTL0 clock-system password: right key, wrong lock.)'},
+  {t:'Accepted — 0x5A appears in the value, which satisfies WDTPW',c:false,w:'The check is positional: (value >> 8) != 0x5A. WDTPW is the upper byte specifically; 0x5A in the low byte is just payload bits.'},
+  {t:'Accepted with hold set — 0x5A & 0x80 lands on bit 7',c:false,w:'The write never reaches the hold logic: the upper byte 0xA5 fails the password test first and the device resets.'},
+  {t:'SCPI error -104 — mixed password/payload bytes are a data-type error',c:false,w:'0xA55A parses fine as an integer; there is no -104 path in _wdt_password. A parseable value with a wrong upper byte is a password violation → PUC.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'w5',ch:'wdt',type:'w',
+ q:'For each of these four WDT:PASSWORD writes, state the outcome and show the byte math: 0x5A00, 0x5AFF, 23168 (decimal), 0x1234.',
+ c:[{l:'0x5A00 → accepted, START (0x00 & 0x80 = 0)',t:['5a00','start','& 0x80 = 0','running']},
+    {l:'0x5AFF → accepted, HOLD (0xFF & 0x80 = 0x80)',t:['5aff','hold','0xff']},
+    {l:'23168 = 0x5A80 → accepted, HOLD (int(param, 0) parses decimal too)',t:['23168','0x5a80','decimal','int(']},
+    {l:'0x1234 → PUC (0x12 ≠ 0x5A)',t:['1234','puc','0x12','reset']}],
+ m:`All four go through value = int(cmd.params[0], 0) & 0xFFFF, then (value >> 8) vs 0x5A:
+- 0x5A00: upper byte 0x5A ✓. Low byte 0x00 & 0x80 = 0 → wdt.hold = False. Accepted, watchdog STARTED.
+- 0x5AFF: upper byte 0x5A ✓. 0xFF & 0x80 = 0x80 → wdt.hold = True. Accepted, HOLD — the other low bits (real-silicon WDTIS/WDTSSEL territory) are simply ignored by this model.
+- 23168: int("23168", 0) = 23168 = 0x5A80 — the base-0 parse accepts decimal as well as 0x-prefixed hex. Upper byte 0x5A ✓, bit 7 set → HOLD. Identical to writing 0x5A80.
+- 0x1234: upper byte 0x12 ≠ 0x5A → self._do_reset(), an immediate PUC. PC to the reset vector, all peripherals rebuilt, wdt.hold back to False, error queue EMPTY.`
+},
+
+{id:'w6',ch:'wdt',type:'mc',
+ q:'What does bare WDT:PASSWORD (no parameter) do, and how is that different from a wrong password?',
+ opts:[
+  {t:'Raises ScpiError -109 "Missing parameter" with NO reset — a malformed command is a SCPI error; a well-formed write with a bad password is a hardware event (PUC)',c:true, w:'Correct. The handler checks "if not cmd.params: raise ScpiError(-109, ...)" before any password logic. The two failure modes are deliberately distinct: broken test scripts get told via the error queue; simulated runaway writes get the reset real hardware gives.'},
+  {t:'It also causes a PUC — any invalid write to WDTCTL resets',c:false,w:'No WDTCTL-style write happened — there is no value to check. The command itself is malformed, which is the SCPI error queue\'s job: -109, no reset.'},
+  {t:'It queries the current password',c:false,w:'There is no password query (real WDTCTL reads return 0x69 in the upper byte, and the model doesn\'t expose a read at all). Missing parameter → -109.'},
+  {t:'It writes 0x0000, which fails the password check',c:false,w:'The handler raises -109 before constructing any value. It never fabricates a default write.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+// ─── CHANNEL: puc ─────────────────────────────────────────────
+{id:'p1',ch:'puc',type:'w',
+ q:'Define the three MSP430 reset levels (SLAU367P §1.2), their trigger relationships, and list at least three events that cause ONLY a PUC. Which level does a WDT password violation cause?',
+ c:[{l:'BOR deepest: power-up / supply droop; triggers POR',t:['bor','brownout','power-up','powerup','supply','deepest']},
+    {l:'POR: caused by BOR / security violation; triggers PUC',t:['por','power-on','power on','security']},
+    {l:'PUC mildest: WDT expiry, password violations, vacant-memory fetch',t:['puc','power-up clear','mildest','vacant','expiry','password violation']},
+    {l:'each deeper level triggers the shallower ones',t:['nested','hierarchy','triggers','cascade','implies']},
+    {l:'WDT password violation → PUC only',t:['only a puc','puc only','just a puc','wdt password violation causes a puc','violation → puc','violation -> puc']}],
+ m:`BOR (brownout reset) — deepest: device power-up, supply falling below the brownout threshold, RST/NMI pin, software PMMSWBOR. A BOR triggers a POR.
+POR (power-on reset) — middle: caused by a BOR, a security violation, or software PMMSWPOR. A POR triggers a PUC.
+PUC (power-up clear) — mildest: caused by a POR, plus software-fault events: watchdog expiry in watchdog mode, a WDTCTL password violation, FRAM/CS/PMM register password violations, and instruction fetch from vacant memory.
+The hierarchy is nested: BOR ⇒ POR ⇒ PUC, never the reverse. Almost every PUC-only source is a symptom of broken software — which is why a WDT password violation causes exactly a PUC: a CPU restart from the reset vector with SR (including GIE) cleared and the watchdog running, without pretending the power failed. Silmulator collapses all three into the single _do_reset path, modeling the consequences rather than the cause machinery.`
+},
+
+{id:'p2',ch:'puc',type:'mc',
+ q:'After WDT:PASSWORD 0x1234 triggers a PUC, what does errors(mcu) return and why?',
+ opts:[
+  {t:'0 — a PUC is a hardware event, not a SCPI error; the reset IS the response',c:true, w:'Correct. test_wrong_password_causes_puc asserts errors(mcu) == 0. The handler calls _do_reset() and returns "" without raising. From the client\'s view the command "succeeded" — the evidence is the device state afterwards, exactly like probing real hardware.'},
+  {t:'1 — the violation queues -224 alongside the reset',c:false,w:'No error is queued. -224 is for illegal parameter VALUES in configuration commands (e.g. WDT:STATE BANANA); a password violation answers with the reset alone.'},
+  {t:'1 — but the PUC then clears the error queue, so it depends on read order',c:false,w:'Nothing is ever queued: the violation branch is _do_reset() then return "". There is no transient error to race against.'},
+  {t:'0 — because _do_reset wipes the error queue as part of the reset',c:false,w:'Right count, wrong reason: no error was raised in the first place. The design point is that hardware events and command errors are different channels.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'p3',ch:'puc',type:'mc',
+ q:'Which of these does the shared _do_reset() path NOT do?',
+ snip:`<span class="cm"># _do_reset: ports/adc/timers/uart/spi/i2c/clock/pm rebuilt,</span>
+<span class="cm"># wdt = WDTState(), _veref_v = 1.2, _memory = {},</span>
+<span class="cm"># _pc = _reset_vector_pc(), _registers zeroed, _gie = False</span>`,
+ opts:[
+  {t:'Preserve FRAM memory contents — the model wipes _memory, though a real PUC does not erase FRAM',c:true, w:'Correct — this is the divergence to remember. _do_reset sets self._memory = {} BEFORE reading the reset vector, so the vector is always unwritten post-PUC and the PC lands at the 0x4000 FRAM-code-start default. Real FRAM survives a PUC.'},
+  {t:'Reload the PC from the reset vector',c:false,w:'It does: self._pc = self._reset_vector_pc(), matching SLAU367P §1.2/§4.3.'},
+  {t:'Clear GIE',c:false,w:'It does: self._gie = False with the comment "SR.GIE is cleared on a PUC (SLAU367P §4.3.3)" — added by 615b4e1.'},
+  {t:'Reset the watchdog to its running (START) default',c:false,w:'It does: self.wdt = WDTState() and WDTState.hold defaults to False — the post-PUC watchdog is ACTIVE, as on silicon.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'p4',ch:'puc',type:'w',
+ q:'Explain "protection by reset": why does WDTCTL answer a wrong password with a PUC instead of an error flag? Frame it in terms of what runaway code can and cannot do.',
+ c:[{l:'watchdog exists to catch runaway code (corrupted PC, wild writes)',t:['runaway','corrupted','wild','garbage','spray']},
+    {l:'runaway code never checks error flags',t:['never checks','doesn\'t check','ignores error','won\'t read','no one reads']},
+    {l:'random 16-bit write has 1/256 chance of a valid password',t:['1/256','1 in 256','255/256','random','probabil']},
+    {l:'wrong write halts execution NOW and restores known-good state',t:['immediately','halts','known-good','known good','reset vector','recovery']},
+    {l:'error-code design could let random writes eventually disable the dog',t:['eventually','silently disable','keep running','keeps trying','slip through']}],
+ m:`The watchdog is the mechanism that catches runaway code, so it must not be disableable BY runaway code. Runaway code — a corrupted PC executing garbage — looks like a stream of random writes, and it never checks status flags; an error-code design would be invisible to it, and the random stream could keep executing until it happened to write a valid 0x5Axx pattern that stops the dog.
+Protection by reset closes that hole: any WDTCTL write without 0x5A in the upper byte (255 of 256 random patterns) triggers an immediate PUC. Execution of the runaway code ends right there — PC reloads from the reset vector, peripherals return to defaults, the watchdog restarts. The protection's failure mode is recovery to a known-good state rather than continued execution.
+Silmulator models this faithfully: _wdt_password calls _do_reset() on a bad upper byte — the same shared path *RST uses — and queues no SCPI error, because the reset is the answer.`
+},
+
+{id:'p5',ch:'puc',type:'mc',
+ q:'A test sets CPU:PC 0x8000, then triggers a PUC via WDT:PASSWORD 0x0000. Where is the PC afterwards?',
+ opts:[
+  {t:'0x4000 — _do_reset wipes _memory first, so _reset_vector_pc() finds no vector at 0xFFFE/0xFFFF and returns the FRAM code-start default',c:true, w:'Correct. Order matters inside _do_reset: self._memory = {} happens before self._pc = self._reset_vector_pc(). With both bytes absent, the function returns 0x4000 (SLASE54D memory map: FRAM code start). test_wrong_password_causes_puc asserts mcu._pc == mcu._reset_vector_pc() — evaluated post-reset, that IS 0x4000.'},
+  {t:'0xFFFE — the PC jumps to the reset vector\'s address',c:false,w:'0xFFFE is where the vector is STORED. The PC gets the vector\'s CONTENTS (or the 0x4000 default when unwritten). Confusing the pointer with the pointee is the classic reset-vector mistake.'},
+  {t:'0x8000 — the PC survives because it was set via SCPI, not by firmware',c:false,w:'The PUC resets CPU state regardless of how it was set: _pc is reassigned inside _do_reset. That\'s what the test\'s CPU:PC 0x8000 setup exists to prove.'},
+  {t:'0x0000 — registers are zeroed, including the PC',c:false,w:'_registers (R0-R15) are zeroed, but _pc is explicitly loaded from _reset_vector_pc(), not zeroed. A real MSP430 fetches the reset vector too — it never starts at address 0.'},
+ ]
