+{id:'i2',ch:'i2c',type:'w',
+ q:'Explain I2CState.addr_buffers introduced by fa7c595: its structure, how an address becomes "known", why the default 0x50 entry exists, and what the NACK path models in real-hardware terms.',
+ c:[{l:'Dict[addr → {rx: [], tx: []}] — one simulated slave per populated address',t:['dict','per-address','per address','queue','rx','tx']},
+    {l:'registered via I2Cn:ADDRESS (setdefault) or inject_i2c_rx(..., address=)',t:['setdefault','inject','address=','i2cn:address','registers','two ways']},
+    {l:'0x50 pre-populated so a fresh device acts as a point-to-point slave',t:['0x50','pre-populated','prepopulated','default','point-to-point','fresh']},
+    {l:'no entry → NACK, modeling UCNACKIFG / no device present',t:['nack','ucnackifg','not present','nobody','no device','missing']}],
+ m:`addr_buffers is a Dict[int, Dict[str, List[int]]] on each I2CState: each key is a bus address holding independent {"rx": [...], "tx": [...]} queues — one simulated slave per populated address, replacing the old flat instance-wide rx/tx buffers that made every address behave identically.
+An address becomes known two ways: (1) configuring it — _i2c_addr ends with ii.addr_buffers.setdefault(addr, {"rx": [], "tx": []}); or (2) an addressed injection — inject_i2c_rx(instance, data, address=0x48) creates/extends that address's rx queue, "placing a device on the bus" from the test harness.
+0x50 is pre-populated in the default_factory so a fresh instance behaves like a single point-to-point slave connection — existing single-device tests keep passing.
+The NACK path: WRITE checks the configured address and TRANSFER checks the target address against addr_buffers; a missing entry returns "NACK" — modeling UCNACKIFG (SLAU367P §32.3.4), a real bus where only devices physically present acknowledge. No SCPI error: NACK is a legitimate bus answer.`
+},
