+{id:'i6',ch:'i2c',type:'w',
+ q:'A SIL test needs a temperature sensor at 0x48 that answers two bytes 0x19,0x80 to a two-byte transfer, while address 0x33 must NACK. Write the harness/SCPI sequence and expected responses, naming the mechanism that routes the data.',
+ c:[{l:'inject_i2c_rx(0, bytes, address=0x48) registers + queues the slave',t:['inject_i2c_rx','address=0x48','address=','inject']},
+    {l:'TRANSFER 0x48 returns "1980" from that address\'s rx queue',t:['1980','0x48','rx queue','returns']},
+    {l:'TRANSFER 0x33 → "NACK" (never registered)',t:['nack','0x33','unregistered','not registered','no slave']},
+    {l:'per-address routing via addr_buffers keeps slaves independent',t:['addr_buffers','per-address','independent','routing','keyed']}],
+ m:`# harness (Python): place the sensor on the bus — registers 0x48 AND queues its reply
+mcu.inject_i2c_rx(0, b"\\x19\\x80", address=0x48)
+
+# SCPI:
+I2C0:TRANSFER 0x48,"0000"   →  "1980"   # two bytes written, two bytes answered
+                                        # from addr_buffers[0x48]["rx"]
+I2C0:TRANSFER 0x33,"0000"   →  "NACK"   # 0x33 has no addr_buffers entry — no
+                                        # simulated device present (UCNACKIFG)
+
+The router is I2CState.addr_buffers: TRANSFER looks up the target address, NACKs on a miss, and otherwise consumes from that address's own rx queue while appending to its tx queue. Because each address has independent queues, the sensor's data can never leak into a transfer aimed at 0x33 — before fa7c595 both transfers would have pulled from one flat buffer and both would have "worked".`
+},
