+{id:'n2',ch:'int',type:'mc',
+ q:'TA0\'s interrupt (enable+flag) and P1\'s interrupt (enable+flag) are both pending, GIE is set. What does INTERRUPT:VECTOR? return?',
+ snip:`_INTERRUPT_PRIORITY = (<span class="str">"TB0"</span>,<span class="str">"TA0"</span>,<span class="str">"TA1"</span>,<span class="str">"P1"</span>,<span class="str">"TA2"</span>,<span class="str">"P2"</span>,...)
+_TIMERA_VECTORS = {<span class="num">0</span>: <span class="num">0xFFE8</span>, <span class="num">1</span>: <span class="num">0xFFE0</span>, <span class="num">2</span>: <span class="num">0xFFDA</span>}
+_GPIO_PORT_VECTORS = {<span class="num">1</span>: <span class="num">0xFFDE</span>, ...}`,
+ opts:[
+  {t:'0xFFE8 — TA0 outranks P1 in Table 9-4 order; _pending_interrupt_vector returns the FIRST pending source in priority order',c:true, w:'Correct. The walk order is TB0, TA0, TA1, P1, TA2, P2..P8 — interleaving timers and ports exactly as SLASE54D Table 9-4 lists them. TA0 hits first → its TA0IV word address 0xFFE8. P1\'s 0xFFDE would only surface once TA0\'s pending state is cleared.'},
+  {t:'0xFFDE — GPIO interrupts always outrank timer interrupts',c:false,w:'Priority comes from the datasheet\'s table position, not the peripheral class: TB0, TA0 and TA1 all sit ABOVE P1, while TA2 sits below it.'},
+  {t:'Both, comma-separated — the query lists all pending vectors',c:false,w:'The query models reading a single vector value: exactly one address (the highest-priority pending one) or 0x0000. Servicing order is what priority means.'},
+  {t:'0x0000 — simultaneous interrupts are a conflict and nothing is reported',c:false,w:'Simultaneous pending sources are the NORMAL case interrupt priority exists to resolve. The highest-priority one wins; the other stays pending.'},
