+// ─── CHANNEL: sil ─────────────────────────────────────────────
+{id:'s1',ch:'sil',type:'w',
+ q:'Before 5e2ba08, WDT:PASSWORD was a no-op that accepted anything. Explain why that made a whole class of firmware bugs invisible to SIL testing — give a concrete firmware defect the old model would have blessed.',
+ c:[{l:'old model: any password accepted silently, no PUC, STATE? constant',t:['no-op','accepted anything','silently','stub','constant','running']},
+    {l:'firmware with a wrong-byte-order or wrong-constant WDTCTL write passed SIL',t:['wrong constant','byte order','0xa55a','wrong byte','passed','blessed']},
+    {l:'on real hardware that write causes a boot-loop / PUC',t:['boot-loop','boot loop','reset loop','on hardware','real part','puc']},
+    {l:'simulator that always says yes cannot test failure handling',t:['always says yes','always-yes','cannot test','invisible','never fails','agree']}],
+ m:`The old handlers were: _wdt_password returned "" unconditionally and _wdt_state always answered "RUNNING". Nothing the firmware wrote could be wrong, and nothing it wrote had any effect.
+Concrete defect the old model blessed: firmware that stops the watchdog with the bytes swapped — writing 0xA55A (or using the clock password 0xA5, or computing WDTPW at the wrong shift). Under the old model the write "succeeded", the test suite passed, and the firmware shipped. On real silicon that write's upper byte fails the 0x5A check and causes an immediate PUC — the device boot-loops: reset, run to the bad write, reset again. The SIL suite certified firmware that cannot boot.
+The general lesson mirrors lesson 0009's cancelling-errors moral: a simulator that always says yes agrees with broken firmware. After 5e2ba08 the same bad write resets the simulated device mid-test — PC moved to 0x4000, peripherals defaulted, subsequent assertions fail loudly — which is exactly the signal that catches the bug before hardware exists.`
+},
