+{id:'s3',ch:'sil',type:'w',
+ q:'Design a SIL test that PROVES a wrong WDT password performs a full PUC rather than being ignored (the pre-5e2ba08 behaviour). What state do you dirty, what command triggers it, and what four assertions do you make?',
+ c:[{l:'dirty unrelated state first: CPU:PC 0x8000, GPIO output 0xFF, WDT HOLD',t:['0x8000','0xff','dirty','hold','setup','unrelated']},
+    {l:'trigger: WDT:PASSWORD with a non-0x5A upper byte (0x1234)',t:['0x1234','wrong password','trigger','non-0x5a','bad upper']},
+    {l:'assert PC at reset vector AND peripherals defaulted AND wdt.hold False',t:['reset vector','_reset_vector_pc','0x00','hold is false','defaulted','wiped']},
+    {l:'assert errors == 0 — reset, not error',t:['errors','== 0','no error','empty queue']}],
+ m:`This is test_wrong_password_causes_puc, and its shape is the point — dirty MULTIPLE UNRELATED subsystems so "reset" is distinguishable from "ignored" and from "only WDT reset":
+  mcu.process("CPU:PC 0x8000")             # CPU state
+  mcu.process("GPIO:PORT1:OUTPUT 0xFF")    # peripheral state
+  mcu.process("WDT:STATE HOLD")            # WDT state
+  mcu.process("WDT:PASSWORD 0x1234")       # 0x12 != 0x5A → PUC
+Four assertions:
+1. mcu._pc == mcu._reset_vector_pc() — the CPU restarted at the reset vector (0x4000 post-wipe), not still at 0x8000.
+2. mcu.ports[1].output == 0x00 — peripherals were rebuilt, proving the reset was device-wide.
+3. mcu.wdt.hold is False — the watchdog returned to its PUC default: RUNNING.
+4. errors(mcu) == 0 — the PUC is a hardware event; nothing was queued.
+If the old no-op were still in place, assertions 1-3 would all fail (state untouched) — so the test cannot false-pass against the stub. The audit probe (probe_wdt_stub) additionally checks the accept path: 0x5A80 with PC at 0x8000 leaves the PC alone and sets hold True.`
+},
