+<section>
+<h2>1. A Register Is a Container With a Physical Size</h2>
+
+<p>The core idea of this lesson: <strong>a hardware register is not a variable — it is a fixed number of flip-flops.</strong> TAxR, the Timer_A counter register (SLAU367P §25.2.1), is sixteen of them. Sixteen bits can represent exactly the integers 0 through 65535 (0xFFFF). The value 0x10000 does not "overflow" TAxR — it simply <em>cannot exist</em> in it, any more than a five-litre bucket can hold six litres.</p>
+
+<p>Python, the language the simulator is written in, has no such limit. Its <code>int</code> is arbitrary-precision: <code>ts.count = 0x12345</code> executes happily and stores 74565 in a "16-bit counter". This mismatch between the modeling language and the modeled hardware is the entire bug class of issue #19:</p>
+
+<table>
+  <tr><th>Register</th><th>Physical width</th><th>Representable values</th><th>What the old model stored</th></tr>
+  <tr><td><code>TAxR</code> (COUNT)</td><td>16 bits</td><td class="mono">0 … 0xFFFF</td><td>Any Python int — <code>0x12345</code> verbatim</td></tr>
+  <tr><td><code>TAxCCRn</code> (CC VALUE)</td><td>16 bits</td><td class="mono">0 … 0xFFFF</td><td>Any Python int, unmasked</td></tr>
+  <tr><td><code>ID</code>+<code>TAIDEX</code> (DIVIDER)</td><td>2 + 3 bits</td><td>products of {1,2,4,8} × {1…8}, max /64</td><td>/128 — a product no encoding produces</td></tr>
+  <tr><td><code>CAP</code> (CC MODE)</td><td>1 bit</td><td>COMPARE / CAPTURE</td><td>Nothing — writes ignored, always read COMPARE</td></tr>
+</table>
+
+<p>The audit probe that pinned the width defect could not be simpler:</p>
+<pre><code><span class="cm"># scripts/audit_msp430.py — BEFORE a20fbb1 (documenting the defect)</span>
+<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">probe_timer_count_over_16bit</span>(dev: <span class="cls">MSP430</span>) -> bool:
+    <span class="str">"""TAxR is a 16-bit counter; the model stores 0x12345 verbatim."""</span>
+    dev.process(<span class="str">"TIMERA0:COUNT 0x12345"</span>)
+    <span class="kw">return</span> _errors(dev) == <span class="num">0</span> <span class="kw">and</span> dev.timer_a[<span class="num">0</span>].count == <span class="num">0x12345</span></code></pre>
+
+<div class="callout tip">
+  <div class="callout-label">💡 The mental model</div>
+  Every register write in a device model should pass through a question: <em>"could these bits actually sit in that register?"</em> If the answer is no, the model must do something other than store the value — because the silicon would. Width is the first, crudest filter; encodings (next section) are the finer one.
+</div>
+</section>
+
+<!-- ============================================================ -->
+<section>
+<h2>2. Encodings Bound Reachable Values — the ID × TAIDEX Worked Example</h2>
+
+<p>Width says a value <em>fits</em>; it does not say a value is <em>reachable</em>. The timer clock divider is the perfect example. There is no "divider register" holding a number like 64. There are two small fields (SLAU367P §25.2.1.1):</p>
+
+<ul>
+  <li><strong>ID</strong> — 2 bits in <code>TAxCTL</code>, selecting a first-stage divide of <strong>1, 2, 4, or 8</strong></li>
+  <li><strong>TAIDEX</strong> — 3 bits in <code>TAxEX0</code>, selecting a second-stage divide of <strong>1 through 8</strong></li>
+</ul>
+
+<p>The effective divider is the <em>product</em> ID × TAIDEX. Enumerate every combination:</p>
+
+<table>
+  <tr><th>ID \ TAIDEX</th><th>÷1</th><th>÷2</th><th>÷3</th><th>÷4</th><th>÷5</th><th>÷6</th><th>÷7</th><th>÷8</th></tr>
+  <tr><td class="mono"><strong>÷1</strong></td><td class="pow">1</td><td class="pow">2</td><td class="hit">3</td><td class="pow">4</td><td class="hit">5</td><td class="hit">6</td><td class="hit">7</td><td class="pow">8</td></tr>
+  <tr><td class="mono"><strong>÷2</strong></td><td class="pow">2</td><td class="pow">4</td><td class="hit">6</td><td class="pow">8</td><td class="hit">10</td><td class="hit">12</td><td class="hit">14</td><td class="pow">16</td></tr>
+  <tr><td class="mono"><strong>÷4</strong></td><td class="pow">4</td><td class="pow">8</td><td class="hit">12</td><td class="pow">16</td><td class="hit">20</td><td class="hit">24</td><td class="hit">28</td><td class="pow">32</td></tr>
+  <tr><td class="mono"><strong>÷8</strong></td><td class="pow">8</td><td class="pow">16</td><td class="hit">24</td><td class="pow">32</td><td class="hit">40</td><td class="hit">48</td><td class="hit">56</td><td class="pow">64</td></tr>
+</table>
+
+<p>Collapse the duplicates and the <strong>reachable state space</strong> is exactly twenty values: {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 10, 12, 14, 16, 20, 24, 28, 32, 40, 48, 56, 64}. Three facts fall straight out of the table:</p>
+
+<ul>
+  <li><strong>The maximum is /64</strong> — ID's largest value (8) times TAIDEX's largest (8). <strong>/128 is unreachable</strong>: it would need a 16 in one of the fields, and no 2-bit or 3-bit encoding provides one.</li>
+  <li><strong>Not every integer below 64 is reachable</strong> — /9, /11, /13, /15, /17… have no factorisation into {1,2,4,8} × {1…8}.</li>
+  <li><strong>The model's accepted set is the power-of-two subset</strong> (gold cells): <code>VALID = {1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64}</code>. Every value it accepts is reachable; it deliberately doesn't expose the non-power-of-two products like /48 — a documented simplification, not a compliance bug. The compliance bug was <em>/128</em>: a value <strong>outside</strong> the reachable set entirely.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<pre><code><span class="cm"># silmulator/devices/msp430.py — the one-line boundary fix</span>
+<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">_timer_clk_div</span>(self, cmd: <span class="cls">ScpiCommand</span>, ts: <span class="cls">TimerState</span>) -> str:
+    <span class="str">"""Timer clock divider (SLAU367P §25.2.1.1/§26.2.1.1): ID x TAIDEX, max /64."""</span>
+    <span class="del">VALID = {1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128}</span>   <span class="cm"># BEFORE — /128 has no encoding</span>
+    <span class="add">VALID = {1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64}</span>        <span class="cm"># AFTER</span>
+    ...
+    <span class="kw">if</span> v <span class="kw">not in</span> VALID:
+        <span class="kw">raise</span> <span class="cls">ScpiError</span>(-<span class="num">224</span>, f<span class="str">"Illegal divider: {v}"</span>)</code></pre>
+
+<div class="callout warn">
+  <div class="callout-label">⚠ Width and encoding are different constraints</div>
+  <code>128</code> fits comfortably in 16 bits — width has no objection. It is the <em>encoding</em> that rules it out: the divider is not a stored number but a derived product of two small fields. When a SCPI parameter mirrors hardware configuration, the accepted set must be (a subset of) the values the encodings can reach — the same rule that made <code>ADC:REFERENCE BANANA</code> illegal in lesson 0009.
+</div>
+</section>
+
+<!-- ============================================================ -->
+<section>
+<h2>3. The Fix — What Commit a20fbb1 Actually Changed</h2>
+
+<p>Issue #19 covered four findings from the MSP430 compliance audit, all in the Timer_A/Timer_B model, all instances of "the model accepts states silicon can't hold". The commit touched one dataclass and four handlers in <code>silmulator/devices/msp430.py</code>.</p>
+
+<h3>Width enforcement on the counter (TAxR)</h3>
+<pre><code><span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">_timer_count</span>(self, cmd: <span class="cls">ScpiCommand</span>, ts: <span class="cls">TimerState</span>) -> str:
+    <span class="add">"""TAxR/TBxR count register (SLAU367P §25.2.1/§26.2.1): 16-bit counter."""</span>
+    <span class="kw">if</span> cmd.is_query:
+        <span class="kw">return</span> str(ts.count)
+    <span class="kw">if</span> cmd.params:
+        <span class="del">ts.count = int(cmd.params[0], 0)</span>              <span class="cm"># BEFORE — any int stored</span>
+        <span class="add">v = int(cmd.params[0], 0)</span>
+        <span class="add"><span class="kw">if not</span> 0 &lt;= v &lt;= 0xFFFF:</span>
+        <span class="add">    <span class="kw">raise</span> <span class="cls">ScpiError</span>(-<span class="num">222</span>, <span class="str">"Timer count out of range (0-0xFFFF)"</span>)</span>
+        <span class="add">ts.count = v</span>
+    <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="str">""</span></code></pre>
+
+<p>The identical guard went into <code>_timer_cc_val</code> for the TAxCCRn compare registers (<code>"CCR value out of range (0-0xFFFF)"</code>). Note the check is two-sided: <code>-1</code> is just as unrepresentable in an unsigned 16-bit register as <code>0x10000</code>.</p>
+
+<h3>The CAP bit becomes real stored state</h3>
+<pre><code><span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">_timer_cc_mode</span>(self, cmd: <span class="cls">ScpiCommand</span>, ts: <span class="cls">TimerState</span>, cc: int) -> str:
+    <span class="add">"""CAP bit (SLAU367P §25.2.4/§26.2.4): COMPARE or CAPTURE mode.</span>
+
+    <span class="add">Stored state only — no waveform capture is simulated (no edge inputs</span>
+    <span class="add">latch TAxCCRn) since time does not pass in this simulation.</span>
+    <span class="add">"""</span>
+    <span class="add">VALID = {"COMPARE", "CAPTURE"}</span>
+    <span class="kw">if</span> cmd.is_query:
+        <span class="del">return "COMPARE"</span>                       <span class="cm"># BEFORE — writes ignored, constant read</span>
+        <span class="add">return ts.cc_mode.get(cc, "COMPARE")</span>
+    <span class="add"><span class="kw">if</span> cmd.params:</span>
+    <span class="add">    v = cmd.params[0].upper()</span>
+    <span class="add">    <span class="kw">if</span> v <span class="kw">not in</span> VALID:</span>
+    <span class="add">        <span class="kw">raise</span> <span class="cls">ScpiError</span>(-<span class="num">224</span>, f<span class="str">"Illegal CC mode: {v}"</span>)</span>
+    <span class="add">    ts.cc_mode[cc] = v</span>
+    <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="str">""</span></code></pre>
+
+<p>Backing it, <code>TimerState</code> gained a per-CC-unit dict — and lost a field (next section):</p>
+<pre><code><span class="cm"># TimerState dataclass</span>
+<span class="del">period: int = 0xFFFF</span>                                    <span class="cm"># BEFORE — an independent store</span>
+capture_compare: <span class="cls">Dict</span>[int, int] = field(default_factory=<span class="kw">lambda</span>: {<span class="num">0</span>: <span class="num">0</span>, <span class="num">1</span>: <span class="num">0</span>, <span class="num">2</span>: <span class="num">0</span>, <span class="num">3</span>: <span class="num">0</span>})
+<span class="add">cc_mode: <span class="cls">Dict</span>[int, str] = field(default_factory=<span class="kw">lambda</span>: {<span class="num">0</span>: <span class="str">"COMPARE"</span>, <span class="num">1</span>: <span class="str">"COMPARE"</span>, <span class="num">2</span>: <span class="str">"COMPARE"</span>, <span class="num">3</span>: <span class="str">"COMPARE"</span>})</span></code></pre>
+
+<h3>Four strict xfails flipped to regression guards</h3>
+<p>Exactly as in issue #18, the tests documenting these defects carried <code>@pytest.mark.xfail(strict=True)</code>. The commit deleted four decorators, promoting the tests:</p>
+<pre><code><span class="cm"># tests/test_msp430_edge_cases.py — now plain passing tests</span>
+<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">test_divider_128_rejected</span>(self, mcu):
+    <span class="cm"># SLAU367P §25.2.1.1: no ID x TAIDEX combination produces /128.</span>
+    mcu.process(<span class="str">"TIMERA0:CLOCK:DIVIDER 128"</span>)
+    <span class="kw">assert</span> <span class="fn">errors</span>(mcu) == <span class="num">1</span>
+
+<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">test_count_over_16bit_rejected_or_masked</span>(self, mcu):
+    <span class="cm"># SLAU367P §25.2.1: a 16-bit counter cannot hold 0x10000.</span>
+    mcu.process(<span class="str">"TIMERA0:COUNT 0x10000"</span>)
+    <span class="kw">assert</span> <span class="fn">errors</span>(mcu) == <span class="num">1</span> <span class="kw">or</span> mcu.timer_a[<span class="num">0</span>].count &lt;= <span class="num">0xFFFF</span>
+
+<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">test_capture_mode_settable</span>(self, mcu):
+    <span class="cm"># SLAU367P §25.2.4 (CAP bit): each CC unit supports capture mode.</span>
+    mcu.process(<span class="str">"TIMERA0:CC1:MODE CAPTURE"</span>)
+    <span class="kw">assert</span> mcu.process(<span class="str">"TIMERA0:CC1:MODE?"</span>) == <span class="str">"CAPTURE"</span>
+
+<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">test_period_is_ccr0_in_up_mode</span>(self, mcu):
+    <span class="cm"># SLAU367P §25.2.3.1: in up mode the period register IS TAxCCR0.</span>
+    mcu.process(<span class="str">"TIMERA0:MODE UP"</span>)
+    mcu.process(<span class="str">"TIMERA0:PERIOD 1000"</span>)
+    <span class="kw">assert</span> int(mcu.process(<span class="str">"TIMERA0:CC0:VALUE?"</span>)) == <span class="num">1000</span></code></pre>
+
+<p>The audit rescored: Timer_A/B <code>COUNT</code> moved ACCEPTS_INVALID → PARTIAL (width enforced; the counter still never advances), <code>CLOCK:DIVIDER</code> and <code>PERIOD</code> → COMPLIANT, <code>CC:MODE</code> NON_COMPLIANT → PARTIAL. The vendor-doc diff moved from 13 MATCH / 5 CONFLICT to <strong>17 MATCH / 1 CONFLICT</strong>.</p>
+</section>
+
+<!-- ============================================================ -->
+<section>
+<h2>4. Reject vs Mask — the Policy Choice for Over-Width Writes</h2>
+
+<p>When a write doesn't fit the register, a simulator has three options. Only one is wrong:</p>
+
+<table>
+  <tr><th>Policy</th><th>Behaviour on <code>COUNT 0x12345</code></th><th>Verdict</th></tr>
+  <tr><td><strong>Store verbatim</strong></td><td><code>count = 74565</code> — a state no TAxR can hold</td><td>❌ The bug. Every later read is fiction.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td><strong>Mask</strong> (truncate to width)</td><td><code>count = 0x12345 &amp; 0xFFFF = 0x2345</code></td><td>✓ Hardware-faithful for <em>bus</em> writes</td></tr>
+  <tr><td><strong>Reject</strong> (queue an error)</td><td><code>count</code> unchanged, SCPI error <code>-222</code> queued</td><td>✓ Hardware-faithful <em>and</em> loud — this repo's choice</td></tr>
+</table>
+
+<p>Both mask and reject keep the state space honest, and the flipped test deliberately accepts either policy — <code>assert errors(mcu) == 1 <strong>or</strong> mcu.timer_a[0].count &lt;= 0xFFFF</code> — because both are defensible readings of the silicon. Why did this repo pick <strong>reject with -222</strong>?</p>
+
+<ul>
+  <li><strong>The write is already a firmware bug.</strong> On the real part a 16-bit bus write physically can't carry 0x12345; C code that computes an over-width timer value has truncated it (or invoked implementation-defined behaviour) <em>before</em> the store. A SCPI parameter is wider than a bus, so the simulator sees the un-truncated intent — and an error queue entry points a finger at it. Masking would reproduce the hardware result while hiding the mistake.</li>
+  <li><strong>Silent masking mimics the silent hardware failure.</strong> A masked <code>0x1F400 → 0xF400</code> "works" and produces a period ~4× off. The whole point of SIL is to catch that in simulation with a diagnosis, not to faithfully reproduce the confusion.</li>
+  <li><strong>It matches the codebase's precedent</strong> — <code>ADC:RESOLUTION 9</code>, <code>ADC:REFERENCE BANANA</code>, illegal GPIO pins: this model consistently validates and rejects rather than coercing.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h3>-222 vs -224: two flavours of rejection</h3>
+<p>Issue #19's handlers use both standard SCPI codes, and the distinction is worth internalising:</p>
+<table>
+  <tr><th>Code</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Used when</th><th>Example</th></tr>
+  <tr><td class="mono">-222</td><td>Data out of range</td><td>Numeric parameter outside the register's representable range</td><td><code>COUNT 0x10000</code>, <code>CC1:VALUE 70000</code>, <code>COUNT -1</code></td></tr>
+  <tr><td class="mono">-224</td><td>Illegal parameter value</td><td>Value not in a finite encoding set (membership test)</td><td><code>DIVIDER 128</code>, <code>CC1:MODE BANANA</code>, <code>MODE SIDEWAYS</code></td></tr>
+</table>
+
+<div class="callout tip">
+  <div class="callout-label">💡 The design rule</div>
+  A <em>range</em> violation (the value type is right, the magnitude isn't) gets -222. A <em>set-membership</em> violation (the value has no encoding at all) gets -224. The divider is -224 even though 128 is numeric, because the divider is an enumerated set of reachable products, not a continuous range.
+</div>
+</section>
+
+<!-- ============================================================ -->
+<section>
+<h2>5. Register Aliasing — PERIOD IS TAxCCR0</h2>
+
+<p>The subtlest of the four fixes. Timer_A in up mode counts from 0 to a top value and wraps. Where does the top value live? <strong>In TAxCCR0</strong> — capture/compare register 0 is drafted as the period register (SLAU367P §25.2.3.1). There is no separate "period register" in the silicon. Two names, one store: that is <strong>register aliasing</strong>.</p>
+
+<h3>The double-store bug class</h3>
+<p>The old model gave the two SCPI names two independent Python attributes:</p>
+<pre><code><span class="cm"># BEFORE a20fbb1 — two stores where hardware has one</span>
+<span class="kw">class</span> <span class="cls">TimerState</span>:
+    <span class="del">period: int = 0xFFFF</span>                <span class="cm"># store #1 — TIMERn:PERIOD reads/writes this</span>
+    capture_compare: <span class="cls">Dict</span>[int, int]    <span class="cm"># store #2 — TIMERn:CC0:VALUE reads/writes [0]</span>
+
+<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">_timer_period</span>(self, cmd, ts):
+    <span class="kw">if</span> cmd.is_query: <span class="kw">return</span> str(<span class="del">ts.period</span>)
+    <span class="kw">if</span> cmd.params:   <span class="del">ts.period = int(cmd.params[0], 0)</span></code></pre>
+
+<p>Watch it diverge:</p>
+<pre><code>TIMERA0:MODE UP
+TIMERA0:PERIOD 1000        <span class="cm"># store #1 ← 1000</span>
+TIMERA0:CC0:VALUE?         <span class="cm"># → 0        (store #2 untouched!)</span>
+TIMERA0:CC0:VALUE 500      <span class="cm"># store #2 ← 500</span>
+TIMERA0:PERIOD?            <span class="cm"># → 1000     (store #1 unaware)</span></code></pre>
+
+<p>On real silicon both queries read the same flip-flops — the answers would be 1000 and then 500. The simulator held <em>two contradictory truths at once</em>, a state the hardware cannot even represent. Any SIL test that mixed the two names (set the period via one, verify PWM duty via the other) was comparing apples to a hallucination.</p>
+
+<h3>The fix: delegate, don't duplicate</h3>
+<pre><code><span class="cm"># AFTER a20fbb1 — one store, two names</span>
+<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">_timer_period</span>(self, cmd: <span class="cls">ScpiCommand</span>, ts: <span class="cls">TimerState</span>) -> str:
+    <span class="str">"""PERIOD — alias of TAxCCR0/TBxCCR0 (SLAU367P §25.2.3.1/§26.2.3.1).
+
+    In up mode the period register IS CCR0; hardware has one register, so
+    PERIOD is not an independent store — it reads/writes capture_compare[0].
+    """</span>
+    <span class="add"><span class="kw">return</span> self._timer_cc_val(cmd, ts, <span class="num">0</span>)</span></code></pre>
+
+<p>The <code>period</code> field was <strong>deleted</strong>, not synchronised. That is the important design move: keeping two stores and copying between them ("shadow-register style") invites the next divergence bug the moment someone adds a third write path. One store per hardware register means the alias <em>cannot</em> drift — and PERIOD inherits CCR0's 16-bit -222 width guard for free.</p>
+
+<div class="callout warn">
+  <div class="callout-label">⚠ A visible side effect: the reset value changed</div>
+  The old independent <code>period</code> defaulted to <code>0xFFFF</code>; TAxCCR0 — like the real register after a reset — defaults to <code>0</code>. So after <code>*RST</code>, <code>TIMERA0:PERIOD?</code> now answers <code>0</code>, not <code>65535</code>. Correctness sometimes changes answers tests were relying on; that is the point of pinning behaviour to the vendor doc rather than to the model's history.
+</div>
+</section>
+
+<!-- ============================================================ -->
+<section>
+<h2>6. Stored-State-Only Modeling — the CAP Bit and the Absence of Time</h2>
+
+<p>Silmulator's devices are <strong>state machines without a clock</strong>: state changes only when a SCPI command changes it. Time does not pass. The counter never increments; interrupts never fire on their own; no waveform edge ever arrives. This is a deliberate architecture, and the CAP-bit fix shows exactly how to model honestly <em>inside</em> it.</p>
+
+<h3>What the CAP bit is</h3>
+<p>Each capture/compare unit has a CAP bit (SLAU367P §25.2.4): <strong>0 = compare mode</strong> (CCRn holds a value you wrote; the timer compares its count against it — PWM, periodic interrupts), <strong>1 = capture mode</strong> (an input edge <em>latches the current count into CCRn</em> — timestamping, pulse-width measurement).</p>
+
+<h3>What the model does — and pointedly does not — simulate</h3>
+<ul>
+  <li><strong>Stored:</strong> the CAP bit itself. <code>TIMERA0:CC1:MODE CAPTURE</code> persists in <code>ts.cc_mode[1]</code> and reads back <code>CAPTURE</code>. Invalid tokens are rejected with -224. Configuration round-trips faithfully.</li>
+  <li><strong>Not simulated:</strong> the capture <em>event machinery</em> — CM edge select, CCIS input select, SCS synchronisation, and the latch of TAxR into TAxCCRn. There are no edges because there is no time.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<p>The old model conflated the two: since it couldn't simulate capture <em>events</em>, it refused to store capture <em>configuration</em> — writes ignored, mode pinned to COMPARE. But those are separate obligations. A SIL test's first question is usually "did my firmware configure the peripheral correctly?" — and that is answerable from stored state alone. The audit's rescoring captures the boundary precisely:</p>
+
+<pre><code><span class="cm"># audit_msp430.py — the probe, before and after</span>
+<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">probe_timer_cc_capture_ignored</span>(dev: <span class="cls">MSP430</span>) -> bool:
+    <span class="del">"""CC mode ignores writes and always reads back COMPARE."""</span>
+    <span class="add">"""CC MODE stores the CAP bit: CAPTURE persists and reads back (§25.2.4)."""</span>
+    dev.process(<span class="str">"TIMERA0:CC1:MODE CAPTURE"</span>)
+    <span class="del">return dev.process("TIMERA0:CC1:MODE?") == "COMPARE" and _errors(dev) == 0</span>
+    <span class="add"><span class="kw">return</span> dev.process(<span class="str">"TIMERA0:CC1:MODE?"</span>) == <span class="str">"CAPTURE"</span> <span class="kw">and</span> _errors(dev) == <span class="num">0</span></span></code></pre>
+
+<p>Verdict: NON_COMPLIANT → <strong>PARTIAL</strong>, with the honest note "capture event latching … is not simulated — time does not pass." Not COMPLIANT — the model knows what it still doesn't do, and says so in the docstring.</p>
+
+<div class="callout tip">
+  <div class="callout-label">💡 The pattern to reuse</div>
+  When a behaviour needs time and the simulator has none, split it: <strong>model the configuration state fully</strong> (store, validate, read back, reset in <code>*RST</code>), and <strong>document the dynamic half as out of scope</strong> in the docstring and the audit. "Stores but never fires" (like TAIE, the interrupt enable) beats both "pretends to fire" and "refuses to store".
+</div>
+</section>
+
+<!-- ============================================================ -->
+<section>
+<h2>7. Why This Matters for SIL Testing</h2>
+
+<p>Accepting an unreachable state doesn't crash anything. That is exactly what makes it dangerous: <strong>every SIL result computed downstream of an impossible state is silently void.</strong> The test ran, the assertions passed, and none of it says anything about real hardware, because the simulated device was in a configuration no silicon can occupy.</p>
+
+<h3>Worked example: the /128 divider</h3>
+<p>Firmware wants a slow tick from SMCLK for a long-interval poll, and a developer writes the driver with a /128 divide:</p>
+<pre><code><span class="cm">/* firmware under test — BUG: no ID×TAIDEX product reaches /128 */</span>
+<span class="fn">timer_set_divider</span>(TIMER_A0, <span class="num">128</span>);          <span class="cm">/* driver silently clamps? wraps? …  */</span>
+<span class="fn">timer_set_period_ms</span>(TIMER_A0, <span class="num">20</span>);         <span class="cm">/* period math assumes /128 tick     */</span></code></pre>
+<ul>
+  <li><strong>Old model:</strong> <code>TIMERA0:CLOCK:DIVIDER 128</code> stored 128; every derived figure (tick frequency, period counts) computed on a clock <em>no MSP430 can produce</em>. The SIL test validated timing math for imaginary hardware — and passed.</li>
+  <li><strong>New model:</strong> the write queues <code>-224 "Illegal divider: 128"</code> and the divider stays put. The test fails at the configuration step, which is where the firmware bug actually lives: the driver's divider table needs entries the hardware has (e.g. /64 with doubled period counts).</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h3>Worked example: the over-width period</h3>
+<p>At a 1-MHz tick, a 200-ms period needs 200,000 counts. <code>0x30D40</code> does not fit in TAxCCR0:</p>
+<ul>
+  <li><strong>Old model:</strong> <code>TIMERA0:PERIOD 200000</code> stored verbatim. A test asserting <code>PERIOD? == 200000</code> passed. On the bench, the register truncates to <code>0x0D40</code> (3392 counts ≈ 3.4 ms) — a ~59× faster interrupt storm the SIL run never hinted at.</li>
+  <li><strong>New model:</strong> <code>-222 "CCR value out of range (0-0xFFFF)"</code>. The firmware must be restructured (bigger divider, or count rollovers in software) — a real design constraint surfaced in simulation, before the bench.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h3>The audit's scoring philosophy, restated</h3>
+<p>Notice that fixing these did <em>not</em> require simulating more behaviour. COUNT is still a static register; capture still never latches. The verdicts improved because the model stopped <em>lying about the state space</em>. In this codebase's terms:</p>
+<ul>
+  <li><strong>ACCEPTS_INVALID</strong> is a worse defect than <strong>MISSING</strong> — a missing command fails loudly; an accepted-invalid state fails silently, downstream, on the bench.</li>
+  <li>The vendor-doc diff (<code>test_vendor_doc_diff.py</code>) flipped its pinned expectation from "this CONFLICT exists" to "this command is a MATCH" — the diff report is itself regression-tested, so the gap can't quietly reopen.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<div class="callout warn">
+  <div class="callout-label">⚠ The general lesson</div>
+  A device simulator's first duty is not to do everything the hardware does — it is to <strong>refuse everything the hardware can't</strong>. Reachability guards are cheap (a range check, a frozen set, a delegated store) and they convert "silently void SIL results" into "loud, diagnosable failures at the exact line that's wrong".
+</div>
+</section>
+
+<!-- ============================================================ -->
+<section>
+<h2>8. Often Confused</h2>
+
+<table>
+  <tr><th>Confusion</th><th>The distinction</th></tr>
+  <tr>
+    <td><strong>Width violation vs encoding violation</strong></td>
+    <td>Width: the number needs more bits than the register has (<code>0x10000</code> in TAxR → -222). Encoding: the number fits, but no field combination produces it (/128 in ID×TAIDEX → -224). The divider bug was an encoding bug — 128 fits in 16 bits just fine.</td>
+  </tr>
+  <tr>
+    <td><strong>"Reject" vs "mask" vs "store verbatim"</strong></td>
+    <td>Reject and mask are both hardware-honest policies (the flipped test accepts either); this repo rejects with -222 to make the firmware bug loud. Store-verbatim is the only wrong answer — it manufactures unreachable state.</td>
+  </tr>
+  <tr>
+    <td><strong>Unreachable vs merely un-modeled values</strong></td>
+    <td>/128 is unreachable in silicon — accepting it is a compliance bug. /48 IS reachable (8×6) but outside the model's power-of-two <code>VALID</code> set — rejecting it is a documented simplification. Both are rejected, for opposite reasons; only one was an audit finding.</td>
+  </tr>
+  <tr>
+    <td><strong>Register alias vs shadow register</strong></td>
+    <td>An alias is one store with two names (PERIOD → <code>capture_compare[0]</code>; nothing to synchronise). A shadow/latch is two real stores with a defined hand-off (Timer_B's TBxCLn compare latches — still unmodeled). The old PERIOD was an accidental shadow with <em>no</em> hand-off: the worst of both.</td>
+  </tr>
+  <tr>
+    <td><strong>CAP bit (configuration) vs capture event (behaviour)</strong></td>
+    <td>The bit is stored state the model now round-trips; the event (edge latches TAxR into CCRn) needs time, which the simulation doesn't have. "Mode reads back CAPTURE" does not mean a capture will ever happen.</td>
+  </tr>
+  <tr>
+    <td><strong>PERIOD? returning 0 after reset: bug or fix?</strong></td>
+    <td>Fix. The old 0xFFFF default belonged to the phantom second store. TAxCCR0 resets to 0, so the alias reads 0 — matching silicon. Tests that assumed 65535 were pinned to the model, not the part.</td>
+  </tr>
+  <tr>
+    <td><strong>-222 vs -224</strong></td>
+    <td>-222 "Data out of range": numeric magnitude outside the representable range (COUNT/CCR width guards). -224 "Illegal parameter value": not a member of a finite value set (DIVIDER 128, CC1:MODE BANANA). Range check vs membership check.</td>
+  </tr>
+</table>
+</section>
+
+<!-- ============================================================ -->
+<section>
+<h2>9. Quiz</h2>
+
+<div class="quiz-wrap">
+
+  <!-- Q1 -->
+  <div class="question" data-question>
+    <p class="q-text"><span class="q-num">Q1.</span> A test sends <code>TIMERA0:COUNT 0x10000</code>. What happens after commit a20fbb1?</p>
+    <ul class="options">
+      <li data-correct="true"  data-why="0x10000 needs 17 bits; TAxR has 16. The guard 'if not 0 <= v <= 0xFFFF' raises ScpiError(-222, 'Timer count out of range (0-0xFFFF)') before the assignment, so ts.count keeps its previous value.">SCPI error -222 is queued and the stored count is unchanged</li>
+      <li data-correct="false" data-why="Masking to 0x0000 would be the other defensible policy — the flipped test accepts it — but this repo rejects instead of masking. Nothing is stored.">The value is masked to 0x0000 and stored</li>
+      <li data-correct="false" data-why="That was the pre-a20fbb1 defect: 0x10000 stored verbatim in a '16-bit' counter, an unreachable state that voided any SIL result built on it.">0x10000 is stored — Python ints have no width limit</li>
+      <li data-correct="false" data-why="-224 is for set-membership violations (DIVIDER 128, MODE BANANA). A numeric magnitude outside the representable range is -222, Data out of range.">SCPI error -224 is queued</li>
+    </ul>
+    <div class="feedback"></div>
+  </div>
+
+  <!-- Q2 -->
+  <div class="question" data-question>
+    <p class="q-text"><span class="q-num">Q2.</span> Why is <code>TIMERA0:CLOCK:DIVIDER 128</code> rejected when 128 fits easily in 16 bits?</p>
+    <ul class="options">
+      <li data-correct="true"  data-why="The divider is not a stored number — it is the product of ID (1/2/4/8, 2 bits) × TAIDEX (1–8, 3 bits). The maximum product is 8×8 = 64. This is an encoding constraint, not a width constraint, so the handler's membership test raises -224.">The divider is the product ID × TAIDEX, whose maximum is 8 × 8 = 64 — no encoding reaches 128</li>
+      <li data-correct="false" data-why="Width is not the issue — 128 fits in 8 bits. The constraint is which values the two divider fields can jointly produce.">The divider register is only 7 bits wide</li>
+      <li data-correct="false" data-why="The model's VALID set is powers of two, but that's the simplification layer. 128 is rejected for a deeper reason: no ID×TAIDEX combination produces it in silicon at all.">128 is rejected only because the model arbitrarily stops at 64</li>
+      <li data-correct="false" data-why="SMCLK's speed is irrelevant to whether the divider fields can encode /128. The rejection is structural, not frequency-based.">Dividing SMCLK by 128 would produce a frequency below the timer's minimum</li>
+    </ul>
+    <div class="feedback"></div>
+  </div>
+
+  <!-- Q3 -->
+  <div class="question" data-question>
+    <p class="q-text"><span class="q-num">Q3.</span> After <code>TIMERA0:MODE UP</code> and <code>TIMERA0:PERIOD 1000</code>, what does <code>TIMERA0:CC0:VALUE?</code> return, and why?</p>
+    <ul class="options">
+      <li data-correct="true"  data-why="_timer_period is now a pure delegate: 'return self._timer_cc_val(cmd, ts, 0)'. PERIOD and CC0:VALUE are two SCPI names for the single capture_compare[0] store, mirroring SLAU367P §25.2.3.1: in up mode the period register IS TAxCCR0.">1000 — PERIOD is an alias that reads and writes capture_compare[0] directly</li>
+      <li data-correct="false" data-why="0 was the OLD answer: the model kept an independent 'period' field, so writing PERIOD never touched capture_compare[0]. That double store was deleted by a20fbb1.">0 — PERIOD and CC0 are independent registers</li>
+      <li data-correct="false" data-why="There is no copying or synchronisation step — that would be a shadow-register design that could still drift. The period field was deleted; there is only one store.">1000 — the handler copies the period value into CCR0 after each write</li>
+      <li data-correct="false" data-why="PERIOD is valid in any mode; §25.2.3.1 just explains its up-mode meaning. No error is queued for this sequence.">SCPI error -221 — PERIOD is only writable in up mode</li>
+    </ul>
+    <div class="feedback"></div>
+  </div>
+
+  <!-- Q4 -->
+  <div class="question" data-question>
+    <p class="q-text"><span class="q-num">Q4.</span> <code>TIMERA0:CC1:MODE CAPTURE</code> succeeds and reads back <code>CAPTURE</code>. What will the model do when firmware later polls <code>TIMERA0:CC1:VALUE?</code> for a captured timestamp?</p>
+    <ul class="options">
+      <li data-correct="true"  data-why="The CAP bit is stored state only. No edge inputs are modeled and time does not pass, so nothing ever latches TAxR into CCR1 — the query returns whatever was last written to CCR1 (0 after reset). Configuration round-trips; capture events are documented as out of scope.">It returns the last value written to CCR1 — no capture event ever latches the count, because time does not pass</li>
+      <li data-correct="false" data-why="That would require simulating CM edge select, CCIS routing, and a passing timeline. The docstring is explicit: 'no edge inputs latch TAxCCRn since time does not pass in this simulation.'">It returns the current count, latched at the moment of the next simulated edge</li>
+      <li data-correct="false" data-why="Reads of CCRn are always legal; capture mode does not make the register write-only or poisoned. The model stores and returns state honestly.">SCPI error -230 — the register is owned by the capture machinery</li>
+      <li data-correct="false" data-why="The mode persists — that persistence is exactly what a20fbb1 added (cc_mode dict). It does not silently revert to COMPARE.">The mode silently reverts to COMPARE on the first CCR1 read</li>
+    </ul>
+    <div class="feedback"></div>
+  </div>
+
+  <!-- Q5 -->
+  <div class="question" data-question>
+    <p class="q-text"><span class="q-num">Q5.</span> Why is a model that <em>accepts</em> an unreachable state considered worse than a model that is <em>missing</em> a command entirely?</p>
+    <ul class="options">
+      <li data-correct="true"  data-why="A missing command errors immediately — the test author sees it and works around it. An accepted unreachable state lets the test run to green while every downstream result describes hardware that cannot exist; the failure surfaces on the bench, with no pointer back to the impossible configuration.">A missing command fails loudly at the call site; an accepted unreachable state silently voids every result computed after it</li>
+      <li data-correct="false" data-why="Both are audit findings, but MISSING and ACCEPTS_INVALID are scored differently precisely because their failure modes differ — one is loud, one is silent.">They are equally bad — both are non-compliances</li>
+      <li data-correct="false" data-why="Performance is irrelevant here. The problem is epistemic: the simulation's answers stop corresponding to any possible silicon.">Unreachable states waste memory and slow the simulation</li>
+      <li data-correct="false" data-why="Crashes would actually be preferable — they are loud. The danger of unreachable state is exactly that nothing crashes.">Unreachable states eventually crash the Python process</li>
+    </ul>
+    <div class="feedback"></div>
+  </div>
+
+  <div id="quiz-score" class="score-box"></div>
+  <button class="btn btn-primary" onclick="scoreQuiz()">See my score</button>
+</div>
+</section>
+
+<!-- ============================================================ -->
+<section>
+<h2>10. Scenarios — Check Your Understanding</h2>
+
+<div class="scenario">
+  <div class="scenario-q">Scenario A: Firmware needs a 100-ms period from a 1-MHz SMCLK. A teammate proposes <code>TIMERA0:CLOCK:DIVIDER 1</code> + <code>TIMERA0:PERIOD 100000</code>. Predict exactly what the simulator does, then fix the configuration using only reachable states.</div>
+  <details>
+    <summary>Show answer</summary>
+    <div class="ans">
+      <p>The divider write succeeds (1 is in the valid set), but <code>PERIOD 100000</code> hits the CCR0 width guard: 100000 &gt; 0xFFFF (65535), so the model queues <strong>-222 "CCR value out of range (0-0xFFFF)"</strong> and CCR0 is unchanged. Before a20fbb1 it would have stored 100000 and the test would have validated an impossible timer.</p>
+      <p>Fix: buy back range with the prescaler. <code>TIMERA0:CLOCK:DIVIDER 2</code> gives a 500-kHz tick, so 100 ms = 50,000 counts — fits: <code>TIMERA0:PERIOD 50000</code>. (Divider 4 and 25,000 counts also works, with coarser resolution.) The general recipe: counts = t × f<sub>clk</sub> / divider; pick the smallest reachable divider that brings counts ≤ 65535.</p>
+    </div>
+  </details>
+</div>
+
+<div class="scenario">
+  <div class="scenario-q">Scenario B: A PWM SIL test written before a20fbb1 did <code>TIMERA0:PERIOD 1000</code>, <code>TIMERA0:CC1:VALUE 250</code>, then asserted duty = CC1 / CC0 = 25% by querying both CC registers. It passed then and passes now — but for different reasons. What changed underneath it, and what sibling test broke?</div>
+  <details>
+    <summary>Show answer</summary>
+    <div class="ans">
+      <p>Before: <code>CC0:VALUE?</code> read the <code>capture_compare[0]</code> store, which the PERIOD write never touched — it read <strong>0</strong>, and duty was 250/0: the test only "passed" if it queried PERIOD? instead of CC0 (two stores, two truths). After: PERIOD delegates to <code>capture_compare[0]</code>, so <code>CC0:VALUE?</code> genuinely returns 1000 and the duty math is grounded in one store, as on silicon.</p>
+      <p>The sibling that breaks: anything pinned to the old defaults or the double store — e.g. a test asserting <code>PERIOD?</code> returns <code>65535</code> after <code>*RST</code> (the deleted field's 0xFFFF default; the alias now reads CCR0's reset value, <strong>0</strong>), or one asserting PERIOD and CC0 can hold <em>different</em> values. Those tests were pinned to the model's fiction, not the part.</p>
+    </div>
+  </details>
+</div>
+
+<div class="scenario">
+  <div class="scenario-q">Scenario C: You're adding a new device command <code>DAC:GAIN</code> mirroring a 2-bit register field with encodings ×1, ×2, ×4, ×8. Using issue #19's patterns, write the handler skeleton: what do you validate, which SCPI errors do you use, and what belongs in the docstring?</div>
+  <details>
+    <summary>Show answer</summary>
+    <div class="ans">
+      <pre style="background:var(--code-bg);padding:.8rem;border-radius:5px;font-size:.82rem">def _dac_gain(self, cmd: ScpiCommand, ds: DACState) -> str:
+    """DAC gain (vendor doc §x.y): 2-bit field, encodings x1/x2/x4/x8."""
+    VALID = {1, 2, 4, 8}                # the reachable set — nothing else
+    if cmd.is_query:
+        return str(ds.gain)
+    if cmd.params:
+        v = int(cmd.params[0])
+        if v not in VALID:
+            raise ScpiError(-224, f"Illegal DAC gain: {v}")
+        ds.gain = v
+    return ""</pre>
+      <p style="margin-top:.5rem">Checklist from issue #19: (1) the accepted set is exactly the register's encodings — a 2-bit field reaches at most 4 values, so ×3 or ×16 must be rejected; (2) <strong>-224</strong> because this is set membership, not a numeric range (a raw 16-bit level register would use a <code>0 ≤ v ≤ 0xFFFF</code> guard with <strong>-222</strong>); (3) reject before assignment so state never holds an illegal value; (4) docstring cites the vendor-doc section and states any stored-state-only limitation; (5) the field's default must be restored by <code>*RST</code> (dataclass default), and a probe/test should pin the rejection.</p>
+    </div>
+  </details>
+</div>
+</section>
+
+<hr style="border:none;border-top:1px solid var(--border);margin:2.5rem 0">
+<p style="color:var(--dim);font-size:.83rem">
+  Lesson 0010 · Silmulator · 2026-07-15 (overnight session, issue #19, commit a20fbb1)<br>
+  Companion drill: <em>drills/0010-register-width-and-encoding-constraints.html</em> · Vocab: <em>vocab/0010-register-width-and-encoding-constraints.html</em>
+</p>
+
+</div>
+
+<script>
+function tick(id){
