+<section>
+<h2>1. A Clock Signal Is Source × Divider, Not a Stored Number</h2>
+
+<p>The single most important idea in this lesson: <strong>a system clock has no frequency of its own.</strong> MCLK, SMCLK, and ACLK are not oscillators — they are <em>routes</em>. Each one is a multiplexer that picks an oscillator, followed by a divider that scales it. The frequency you observe on the pin is a <strong>derived value</strong>:</p>
+
+<div class="equation">
+  f<sub>clock</sub> &nbsp;=&nbsp;
+  <span class="frac">
+    <span class="top">f<sub><span class="vr">selected source</span></sub></span>
+    <span class="bot"><span class="fs">divider</span></span>
+  </span>
+  <span class="eq-note">resolve the source → look up its base frequency → apply the divider — every time, at read time</span>
+</div>
+
+<p>Nothing in the hardware "stores" the number 1 MHz for MCLK. What the CS (Clock System) module stores is <em>configuration</em>: a 3-bit source-select field and a 3-bit divider field per clock. Ask "what is MCLK's frequency?" and the only honest answer walks the tree:</p>
+
+<ol>
+  <li><strong>Resolve the source:</strong> which oscillator does the mux currently select? (<code>CSCTL2.SELM</code>)</li>
+  <li><strong>Get the base frequency:</strong> what does <em>that oscillator</em> run at right now? (DCO setting × drift, crystal value, or a fixed internal rate)</li>
+  <li><strong>Apply the divider:</strong> divide by the configured /1…/32. (<code>CSCTL3.DIVM</code>)</li>
+</ol>
+
+<p>Skip step 1 — assume the source is always the DCO — and you get a number that is <em>sometimes</em> right (when DCO happens to be selected) and wildly wrong otherwise. That is exactly the bug commit 701853d fixed. With MCLK switched to the 32.768-kHz LFXT crystal, the old model still reported <strong>1 000 000 Hz</strong> — off by a factor of ~30.5.</p>
+
+<div class="callout tip">
+  <div class="callout-label">💡 The mental model</div>
+  Think of a clock as a <em>plumbing route</em>, not a tank. The question "how much water comes out of the MCLK tap?" depends on which reservoir the valve currently points at and how far the restrictor is closed. Caching yesterday's flow rate — or always quoting the DCO reservoir — answers a different question than the one asked.
+</div>
+</section>
+
+<!-- ============================================================ -->
+<section>
+<h2>2. The FR5994 Clock Tree — Sources, Muxes, and Dividers</h2>
+
+<p>The MSP430FR5994's CS module (SLAU367P chapter 3) generates three system clocks from six possible oscillators. The routing is set by <strong>CSCTL2</strong> (three source-select fields) and <strong>CSCTL3</strong> (three divider fields):</p>
+
+<table>
+  <tr><th>Clock</th><th>Mux field</th><th>Selectable sources (SLAU367P §3.3.2)</th><th>Divider field</th><th>Feeds</th></tr>
+  <tr><td><strong>MCLK</strong></td><td><code>CSCTL2.SELM</code></td><td>LFXT, VLO, LFMODCLK, DCO, MODCLK, HFXT</td><td><code>CSCTL3.DIVM</code></td><td>CPU and system</td></tr>
+  <tr><td><strong>SMCLK</strong></td><td><code>CSCTL2.SELS</code></td><td>same six as MCLK</td><td><code>CSCTL3.DIVS</code></td><td>Timers, eUSCI (UART/SPI/I2C)</td></tr>
+  <tr><td><strong>ACLK</strong></td><td><code>CSCTL2.SELA</code></td><td>LFXT, VLO, LFMODCLK <em>only</em></td><td><code>CSCTL3.DIVA</code></td><td>Low-power peripherals, RTC, slow timers</td></tr>
+</table>
+
+<p>Two structural facts matter:</p>
+<ul>
+  <li><strong>ACLK's mux is narrower.</strong> ACLK is the "auxiliary" low-frequency clock, intended to keep ticking in low-power modes — so its mux only offers the three low-frequency sources. <code>CLOCK:ACLK:SOURCE DCO</code> is not a policy choice the simulator makes; there is <em>no SELA encoding</em> for DCO on this silicon.</li>
+  <li><strong>All three dividers share one encoding.</strong> DIVM/DIVS/DIVA are 3-bit fields whose defined encodings are /1, /2, /4, /8, /16, /32. There is no /64 and no /128 — values the pre-fix model happily accepted.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h3>What the model stores per clock</h3>
+<pre><code><span class="cm"># silmulator/devices/msp430.py — ClockState after 701853d</span>
+<span class="attr">@dataclass</span>
+<span class="kw">class</span> <span class="cls">ClockState</span>:
+    mclk_source: str = <span class="str">"DCO"</span>   <span class="cm"># DCO | LFXT | HFXT | VLO | MODCLK | LFMODCLK</span>
+    mclk_div: int = <span class="num">1</span>
+    smclk_source: str = <span class="str">"DCO"</span>  <span class="cm"># same source set as MCLK (CSCTL2.SELS)</span>
+    smclk_div: int = <span class="num">1</span>
+    aclk_source: str = <span class="str">"LFXT"</span>  <span class="cm"># LFXT | VLO | LFMODCLK (CSCTL2.SELA)</span>
+    aclk_div: int = <span class="num">1</span>
+    dco_frequency: float = <span class="num">1e6</span>  <span class="cm"># Hz</span>
+    lfxt_frequency: float = <span class="num">32768.0</span>
+    <span class="cm"># HFXT has no fixed nominal in hardware — it is an external crystal whose</span>
+    <span class="cm"># range is configured via CSCTL4.HFFREQ (SLAU367P §3.3.4, not modeled).</span>
+    <span class="add">hfxt_frequency: float = 4e6  # simulator-only default</span>
+    vcc: float = <span class="num">3.3</span></code></pre>
+
+<p>Note what is stored: <strong>sources and dividers</strong> — configuration — plus base frequencies for the two crystals and the DCO. No per-clock frequency field exists anywhere. That is deliberate: storing <code>mclk_frequency</code> would be a second copy of derivable truth, and the two copies would eventually disagree (the same double-store bug class as the old independent timer <code>period</code> field from lesson 0010's commit family).</p>
+
+<div class="callout warn">
+  <div class="callout-label">⚠ Where is REFO?</div>
+  If you have used MSP430F5xx/6xx parts, you may reach for REFO — the 32-kHz trimmed reference oscillator in those parts' UCS clock module. The FR59xx CS module <strong>does not have it</strong>: no SELM/SELS/SELA encoding maps to REFO (same for DCOCLKDIV). The pre-fix model accepted <code>CLOCK:MCLK:SOURCE REFO</code> and rejected MODCLK — exactly backwards for this chip.
+</div>
+</section>
+
+<!-- ============================================================ -->
+<section>
+<h2>3. The Fix — What Commit 701853d Actually Changed</h2>
+
+<p>Issue #20 came out of the MSP430 compliance audit. Five clock probes flagged the same theme: the model stored clock configuration but neither validated it against the FR5994's real encodings nor <em>used</em> it when answering frequency queries.</p>
+
+<h3>Before: FREQUENCY? hardwired to one oscillator per clock</h3>
+<pre><code><span class="cm"># silmulator/devices/msp430.py — BEFORE 701853d</span>
+<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">_clk_mclk_freq</span>(self, cmd: <span class="cls">ScpiCommand</span>) -> str:
+    <span class="del">freq = self._dco_actual() / self.clock.mclk_div</span>      <span class="cm"># ← always DCO</span>
+    <span class="kw">return</span> f<span class="str">"{freq:.0f}"</span>
+
+<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">_clk_aclk_freq</span>(self, cmd: <span class="cls">ScpiCommand</span>) -> str:
+    <span class="del">freq = self.clock.lfxt_frequency / self.clock.aclk_div</span> <span class="cm"># ← always LFXT</span>
+    <span class="kw">return</span> f<span class="str">"{freq:.0f}"</span></code></pre>
+
+<p>The source-select commands <em>stored</em> the selection (<code>self.clock.mclk_source = v</code>) but the query never read it back. SMCLK's source and all SMCLK/ACLK dividers were not validated at all (<code>CLOCK:SMCLK:SOURCE BANANA</code> succeeded), MCLK's validation set wrongly contained REFO and DCOCLKDIV, and dividers allowed /64 and /128.</p>
+
+<h3>After: one resolver walks the tree</h3>
+<pre><code><span class="cm"># silmulator/devices/msp430.py — AFTER 701853d</span>
+<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">_clock_source_frequency</span>(self, source: str) -> float:
+    <span class="str">"""Effective frequency (Hz) of one clock-system source, pre-divider.
+
+    SLAU367P §3.2: DCO is the drift-affected trimmed oscillator
+    (``_dco_actual()``); LFXT/HFXT read the crystal fields on
+    ``ClockState``; VLO and MODCLK are fixed-frequency internal
+    oscillators with no configurable register; LFMODCLK is MODCLK/128.
+    """</span>
+    <span class="kw">if</span> source == <span class="str">"DCO"</span>:
+        <span class="kw">return</span> self._dco_actual()          <span class="cm"># dco_frequency × fault-profile drift</span>
+    <span class="kw">if</span> source == <span class="str">"LFXT"</span>:
+        <span class="kw">return</span> self.clock.lfxt_frequency   <span class="cm"># 32768 default</span>
+    <span class="kw">if</span> source == <span class="str">"HFXT"</span>:
+        <span class="kw">return</span> self.clock.hfxt_frequency   <span class="cm"># 4e6 simulator-only default</span>
+    <span class="kw">if</span> source == <span class="str">"VLO"</span>:
+        <span class="kw">return</span> VLO_FREQUENCY_HZ            <span class="cm"># 9400.0 — fixed, no register</span>
+    <span class="kw">if</span> source == <span class="str">"MODCLK"</span>:
+        <span class="kw">return</span> MODCLK_FREQUENCY_HZ         <span class="cm"># 4.8e6 — fixed, no register</span>
+    <span class="kw">if</span> source == <span class="str">"LFMODCLK"</span>:
+        <span class="kw">return</span> LFMODCLK_FREQUENCY_HZ       <span class="cm"># MODCLK / 128 = 37500</span>
+    <span class="kw">raise</span> <span class="cls">ScpiError</span>(-<span class="num">221</span>, f<span class="str">"No simulated frequency for clock source {source}"</span>)
+
+<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">_clk_mclk_freq</span>(self, cmd: <span class="cls">ScpiCommand</span>) -> str:
+    <span class="add">freq = self._clock_source_frequency(self.clock.mclk_source) / self.clock.mclk_div</span>
+    <span class="kw">return</span> f<span class="str">"{freq:.0f}"</span>   <span class="cm"># SMCLK and ACLK queries are the same shape</span></code></pre>
+
+<h3>Validation now mirrors the register encodings</h3>
+<pre><code><span class="cm"># CSCTL2.SELM/SELS: MCLK and SMCLK share one source mux. REFO and DCOCLKDIV</span>
+<span class="cm"># have no encoding on the FR59xx CS; MODCLK and LFMODCLK do.</span>
+VALID_MCLK_SMCLK_SOURCES = <span class="fn">frozenset</span>({<span class="str">"LFXT"</span>, <span class="str">"VLO"</span>, <span class="str">"LFMODCLK"</span>, <span class="str">"DCO"</span>, <span class="str">"MODCLK"</span>, <span class="str">"HFXT"</span>})
+<span class="cm"># CSCTL2.SELA: ACLK's source mux is a narrower subset of the above.</span>
+VALID_ACLK_SOURCES = <span class="fn">frozenset</span>({<span class="str">"LFXT"</span>, <span class="str">"VLO"</span>, <span class="str">"LFMODCLK"</span>})
+<span class="cm"># CSCTL3.DIVM/DIVS/DIVA: all three dividers share the same 3-bit, power-of-two</span>
+<span class="cm"># encoding that tops out at /32 (values above /32 have no encoding).</span>
+VALID_CLOCK_DIVIDERS = <span class="fn">frozenset</span>({<span class="num">1</span>, <span class="num">2</span>, <span class="num">4</span>, <span class="num">8</span>, <span class="num">16</span>, <span class="num">32</span>})</code></pre>
+
+<p>Every setter now checks its set before storing — a bad source raises <code>ScpiError(-224, "Illegal clock source: …")</code>, a bad divider raises <code>ScpiError(-222, "Illegal divider: …")</code>, and in both cases <strong>the previous state is left unchanged</strong>. Note the divider error code <em>changed</em> from -224 to -222: a divider is numeric data outside the representable range (-222, "Data out of range"), while a source token outside the enum is an illegal parameter value (-224).</p>
+
+<h3>The timer path was fixed the same way</h3>
+<pre><code><span class="cm"># _timer_tick_frequency — timers now see the real tree, not a hardwired source</span>
+<span class="kw">if</span> ts.clock_source == <span class="str">"SMCLK"</span>:
+    <span class="del">src = self._dco_actual() / self.clock.smclk_div</span>
+    <span class="add">src = self._clock_source_frequency(self.clock.smclk_source) / self.clock.smclk_div</span>
+<span class="kw">elif</span> ts.clock_source == <span class="str">"ACLK"</span>:
+    <span class="del">src = self.clock.lfxt_frequency / self.clock.aclk_div</span>
+    <span class="add">src = self._clock_source_frequency(self.clock.aclk_source) / self.clock.aclk_div</span></code></pre>
+
+<h3>Tests flipped from expected-failure to passing</h3>
+<p>Two audit-derived tests dropped their <code>@pytest.mark.xfail(strict=True)</code> decorators, becoming permanent regression guards:</p>
+<pre><code><span class="cm"># tests/test_msp430_edge_cases.py — AFTER 701853d (xfail decorators removed)</span>
+<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">test_mclk_divider_64_rejected</span>(self, mcu):
+    <span class="cm"># SLAU367P §3.3.3: DIVM encodings stop at /32.</span>
+    mcu.process(<span class="str">"CLOCK:MCLK:DIVIDER 64"</span>)
+    <span class="kw">assert</span> <span class="fn">errors</span>(mcu) == <span class="num">1</span>
+
+<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">test_mclk_source_modclk_accepted</span>(self, mcu):
+    <span class="cm"># SLAU367P §3.3.2 (CSCTL2.SELM): MODCLK is encoding 4.</span>
+    mcu.process(<span class="str">"CLOCK:MCLK:SOURCE MODCLK"</span>)</code></pre>
+
+<p>Five audit probes were rescored, including the headline one — <code>probe_mclk_freq_ignores_source</code> now asserts that an LFXT-sourced MCLK reads <code>lfxt_frequency / mclk_div</code>, the compliant behaviour, instead of documenting the DCO lie.</p>
+</section>
+
+<!-- ============================================================ -->
+<section>
+<h2>4. Fixed-Rate Sources: VLO, MODCLK, LFMODCLK (and the HFXT Special Case)</h2>
+
+<p>The six oscillators fall into three kinds, and the resolver treats each kind differently:</p>
+
+<table>
+  <tr><th>Source</th><th>Kind</th><th>Frequency in the model</th><th>Where it comes from</th></tr>
+  <tr><td><code>DCO</code></td><td>Trimmed internal oscillator</td><td><code>dco_frequency × faults.dco_factor()</code> (default 1 MHz)</td><td>Configurable via <code>CLOCK:DCO:FREQUENCY</code>; CORNER fault profile adds ±3.5% drift</td></tr>
+  <tr><td><code>LFXT</code></td><td>External LF crystal</td><td><code>clock.lfxt_frequency</code> (default 32 768 Hz)</td><td>Board property — the watch crystal</td></tr>
+  <tr><td><code>HFXT</code></td><td>External HF crystal</td><td><code>clock.hfxt_frequency</code> (default 4 MHz)</td><td><strong>Simulator-only default</strong> — see below</td></tr>
+  <tr><td><code>VLO</code></td><td>Fixed internal oscillator</td><td><code>VLO_FREQUENCY_HZ = 9400.0</code></td><td>SLAU367P §3.2.6 — ~9.4 kHz typical, no configuration register</td></tr>
+  <tr><td><code>MODCLK</code></td><td>Fixed internal oscillator</td><td><code>MODCLK_FREQUENCY_HZ = 4.8e6</code></td><td>SLAU367P §3.2.5 — ~4.8 MHz typical, no configuration register</td></tr>
+  <tr><td><code>LFMODCLK</code></td><td>Derived from MODCLK</td><td><code>LFMODCLK_FREQUENCY_HZ = MODCLK_FREQUENCY_HZ / 128</code> = 37 500 Hz</td><td>SLAU367P §3.2.5 — MODCLK through a fixed /128</td></tr>
+</table>
+
+<pre><code><span class="cm"># Fixed-frequency clock sources with no configurable register (SLAU367P</span>
+<span class="cm"># §3.2.5 MODCLK, ~4.8 MHz typical; §3.2.6 VLOCLK, ~9.4 kHz typical).</span>
+<span class="cm"># LFMODCLK is MODCLK divided by 128 (SLAU367P §3.2.5).</span>
+MODCLK_FREQUENCY_HZ = <span class="num">4.8e6</span>
+VLO_FREQUENCY_HZ = <span class="num">9400.0</span>
+LFMODCLK_FREQUENCY_HZ = MODCLK_FREQUENCY_HZ / <span class="num">128</span></code></pre>
+
+<p>Notice that <code>LFMODCLK_FREQUENCY_HZ</code> is <em>computed from</em> <code>MODCLK_FREQUENCY_HZ</code> rather than written as a second literal <code>37500.0</code>. Same principle as the tree itself: LFMODCLK <strong>is</strong> MODCLK/128 — encoding the relationship, not the result, means the two constants can never drift apart.</p>
+
+<h3>Why VLO and MODCLK are "about" their frequencies</h3>
+<p>VLO (the very-low-power oscillator) and MODCLK are uncalibrated RC oscillators. The datasheet quotes them as <em>typical</em> values with wide tolerances — VLO can legitimately sit anywhere around 9.4 kHz and wanders with temperature and supply. The simulator models the typical value; the lesson for firmware is that <strong>anything timed from VLO is approximate by design</strong> (it exists to keep watchdogs and wakeups alive cheaply, not to timestamp anything).</p>
+
+<h3>The HFXT honesty comment</h3>
+<p>HFXT gets a different treatment, and the commit documents why:</p>
+<pre><code><span class="cm"># HFXT has no fixed nominal in hardware — it is an external crystal whose</span>
+<span class="cm"># range is configured via CSCTL4.HFFREQ (SLAU367P §3.3.4, not modeled).</span>
+<span class="cm"># This is a simulator-only default so HFXT-sourced clocks resolve to a</span>
+<span class="cm"># concrete frequency.</span>
+hfxt_frequency: float = <span class="num">4e6</span>  <span class="cm"># Hz</span></code></pre>
+<p>There is no "typical HFXT frequency" to model — the board designer chooses the crystal. The model needs <em>some</em> value so an HFXT-sourced clock can answer a query, so it picks 4 MHz and labels it a simulator-only default rather than pretending it is a hardware fact. Compare VeREF+ from lesson 0009: board-supplied values get modeled as declared state, not invented constants.</p>
+</section>
