+<section>
+<h2>5. Worked Examples — Verified Against the Code</h2>
+
+<p>All three FREQUENCY? handlers compute <code>_clock_source_frequency(source) / div</code> and format with <code>f"{freq:.0f}"</code>. That format specifier <strong>rounds to the nearest integer</strong> (Python rounds exact halves to the even neighbour) — unlike the ADC path from lesson 0009, which truncates with <code>int()</code>. Keep the two behaviours straight.</p>
+
+<h3>Worked example 1 — the headline probe: MCLK from LFXT</h3>
+<pre><code>CLOCK:MCLK:SOURCE LFXT
+CLOCK:MCLK:FREQUENCY?
+  = lfxt_frequency / mclk_div = 32768 / 1
+  → <span class="num">"32768"</span>          <span class="cm"># the OLD model answered "1000000" — DCO/1, a 30.5× lie</span></code></pre>
+
+<h3>Worked example 2 — ACLK from LFMODCLK through /8 (a fractional result)</h3>
+<pre><code>CLOCK:ACLK:SOURCE LFMODCLK
+CLOCK:ACLK:DIVIDER 8
+CLOCK:ACLK:FREQUENCY?
+  = (4.8e6 / 128) / 8 = 37500 / 8 = 4687.5
+  → f"{4687.5:.0f}" → <span class="num">"4688"</span>   <span class="cm"># .0f rounds; 4687.5 → 4688 (ties-to-even)</span></code></pre>
+<p>Real clock trees produce non-integer frequencies all the time — the SCPI response is a rounded <em>report</em> of a fractional physical rate. Any test asserting <code>4687</code> (expecting truncation) fails: this handler is not <code>int()</code>.</p>
+
+<h3>Worked example 3 — SMCLK from VLO, then MODCLK</h3>
+<pre><code>CLOCK:SMCLK:SOURCE VLO
+CLOCK:SMCLK:FREQUENCY?        → 9400 / 1   → <span class="num">"9400"</span>
+
+CLOCK:SMCLK:SOURCE MODCLK
+CLOCK:SMCLK:DIVIDER 32
+CLOCK:SMCLK:FREQUENCY?        → 4.8e6 / 32 → <span class="num">"150000"</span></code></pre>
+
+<h3>Worked example 4 — rejections leave state untouched</h3>
+<pre><code>CLOCK:MCLK:DIVIDER 64         <span class="cm"># -222 "Illegal divider: 64" — no DIVM encoding</span>
+CLOCK:MCLK:DIVIDER?           → <span class="num">"1"</span>    <span class="cm"># still the default; the probe asserts this</span>
+
+CLOCK:ACLK:SOURCE DCO         <span class="cm"># -224 "Illegal clock source: DCO" — no SELA encoding</span>
+CLOCK:MCLK:SOURCE REFO        <span class="cm"># -224 — REFO does not exist on the FR59xx CS</span>
+CLOCK:MCLK:SOURCE MODCLK      <span class="cm"># accepted — SELM encoding 4</span></code></pre>
+
+<h3>Worked example 5 — the divider chain reaches a timer</h3>
+<pre><code>CLOCK:ACLK:SOURCE VLO         <span class="cm"># ACLK = 9400 / 1 = 9400 Hz</span>
+TIMERA0:CLOCK:SOURCE ACLK
+TIMERA0:CLOCK:DIVIDER 4       <span class="cm"># timer tick = 9400 / 4 = 2350 Hz</span>
+TIMERA0:INTERVAL? 2350        <span class="cm"># ≈ 1.0 s — 2350 ticks at 2350 Hz</span></code></pre>
+<p><code>_timer_tick_frequency</code> performs the same walk: ACLK's <em>source</em> (VLO → 9400) → ACLK's divider (/1) → the timer's own divider (/4). Before the fix this chain silently started from 32768 (LFXT) regardless of the selection — every VLO-clocked timer interval was wrong by 32768/9400 ≈ 3.49×.</p>
+
+<div class="callout warn">
+  <div class="callout-label">⚠ Drift rides only the DCO branch</div>
+  <code>_dco_actual()</code> multiplies by <code>faults.dco_factor()</code> (1.0 NOMINAL, ±3.5% CORNER). The resolver applies drift <em>only</em> when the selected source is DCO — crystals and the fixed internal oscillators return their base values untouched. Switching SMCLK from DCO to MODCLK therefore also switches off simulated DCO drift for everything SMCLK feeds.
+</div>
+</section>
