+<section>
+<h2>9. Scenarios — Check Your Understanding</h2>
+
+<div class="scenario">
+  <div class="scenario-q">Scenario A: A low-power design runs ACLK from VLO and clocks TIMERA0 from ACLK with the timer divider at /4. Write the SCPI sequence, predict the timer tick frequency, and state how many ticks span approximately one second. What was the tick frequency in the pre-701853d model, and by what factor were intervals wrong?</div>
+  <details>
+    <summary>Show answer</summary>
+    <div class="ans">
+      <pre style="background:var(--code-bg);padding:.8rem;border-radius:5px;font-size:.82rem">CLOCK:ACLK:SOURCE VLO         ← ACLK = 9400 / 1 = 9400 Hz
+TIMERA0:CLOCK:SOURCE ACLK
+TIMERA0:CLOCK:DIVIDER 4       ← tick = 9400 / 4 = 2350 Hz
+TIMERA0:INTERVAL? 2350        → ≈ 1.0 s</pre>
+      <p style="margin-top:.5rem">The tick rate is 2350 Hz, so ~2350 ticks ≈ one second. Before the fix, <code>_timer_tick_frequency</code> hardwired ACLK to <code>lfxt_frequency / aclk_div</code> = 32768 Hz, giving a tick rate of 8192 Hz — every interval computed from it was short by 32768/9400 ≈ <strong>3.49×</strong>. A "1-second" heartbeat simulated as ~0.29 s, so firmware tuned against the simulator would run 3.5× slow on real silicon. VLO carries no drift factor (only the DCO branch multiplies by <code>faults.dco_factor()</code>), so 2350 Hz is exact in the NOMINAL and CORNER profiles alike.</p>
+    </div>
+  </details>
+</div>
+
+<div class="scenario">
+  <div class="scenario-q">Scenario B: A teammate reports "the clock model is broken — I set <code>CLOCK:SMCLK:DIVIDER 64</code> and the divider query still says 1, but no test crashed." What do you check, and what do you tell them?</div>
+  <details>
+    <summary>Show answer</summary>
+    <div class="ans">
+      <p>Check the error queue: <code>SYSTEM:ERROR?</code> (or the test helper <code>errors(mcu)</code>). There will be one queued error: <strong>-222, "Illegal divider: 64"</strong>. Nothing crashed because SCPI devices report bad parameters via the error queue, not exceptions to the client — and rejection deliberately leaves the previous state (<code>smclk_div == 1</code>) untouched, which is why the query still says 1.</p>
+      <p>Then explain the hardware: CSCTL3.DIVS is a 3-bit field whose defined encodings stop at /32 — /64 has no encoding, so no FR5994 can ever be configured that way (SLAU367P §3.3.3). The pre-fix model accepted 64 (and even 128), which meant SIL tests could bless clock configurations no silicon can express. <code>test_mclk_divider_64_rejected</code> — a strict xfail flipped to a real test by 701853d — pins the rejection. If they need a slower clock, the answer is a slower <em>source</em> (VLO, LFMODCLK) or a peripheral-level divider, exactly like real board bring-up.</p>
+    </div>
+  </details>
+</div>
+
+<div class="scenario">
+  <div class="scenario-q">Scenario C: You are writing a SIL test to prove firmware recomputes its UART divisor when SMCLK moves from the 1-MHz DCO to MODCLK/4. Using only SCPI clock commands, what sequence drives the scenario, and what must <code>CLOCK:SMCLK:FREQUENCY?</code> report at each step? Why would this test have been impossible to write honestly before 701853d?</div>
+  <details>
+    <summary>Show answer</summary>
+    <div class="ans">
+      <pre style="background:var(--code-bg);padding:.8rem;border-radius:5px;font-size:.82rem">CLOCK:SMCLK:FREQUENCY?        → "1000000"   (default: DCO 1e6 / 1)
+CLOCK:SMCLK:SOURCE MODCLK     ← SELS = MODCLK (encoding 4)
+CLOCK:SMCLK:DIVIDER 4
+CLOCK:SMCLK:FREQUENCY?        → "1200000"   (4.8e6 / 4)</pre>
+      <p style="margin-top:.5rem">The firmware's divisor for 9600 baud must move from 1000000/9600 ≈ 104 to 1200000/9600 = 125 — the test asserts the reprogrammed value against the <em>reported</em> SMCLK. Before 701853d this test was unwritable in two ways: first, <code>CLOCK:SMCLK:SOURCE</code> accepted MODCLK only by accident of being unvalidated (it accepted <code>BANANA</code> too — <code>probe_smclk_source_unvalidated</code>), so the selection asserted nothing; second, FREQUENCY? would have reported 1 MHz/div <em>both</em> before and after the switch, so the assertion "the reported frequency changed" could never pass. A test that cannot observe the thing it verifies is not a test — source-aware queries are what make the clock tree observable.</p>
+    </div>
+  </details>
+</div>
+</section>
