+{id:'r3',ch:'reject',type:'mc',
+ q:'A colleague argues: "Masking is MORE realistic — real hardware truncates, it doesn\'t report errors. The simulator should mask." What is the strongest counter-argument?',
+ opts:[
+  {t:'The simulator sees the un-truncated intent (SCPI carries the full number, a 16-bit bus cannot), so it has diagnostic information real hardware never has — rejecting uses that information; masking throws it away',c:true, w:'Correct. On silicon, truncation happens before the register: the C compiler or the bus already narrowed the value, so the part never "knows" about the overflow. The SCPI layer is wider, so the simulator uniquely CAN detect the bug — and a SIL tool\'s purpose is diagnosis, not re-enacting the crime.'},
+  {t:'Masking is computationally more expensive than rejecting',c:false,w:'A bitwise AND is as cheap as a comparison. The argument is about diagnostics, not performance.'},
+  {t:'Real hardware actually queues SCPI errors on overflow',c:false,w:'Real MSP430 registers have no SCPI layer at all. The error queue is a simulator-side facility; the question is how best to use it.'},
+  {t:'Masking would break the width invariant that count <= 0xFFFF',c:false,w:'Masking preserves that invariant by construction — that is why the flipped test accepts it. Its weakness is silence, not incorrect state.'},
+ ]
