+// ─── CHANNEL: code ────────────────────────────────────────────
+{id:'c1',ch:'code',type:'mc',
+ q:'What was the exact defect in _clk_mclk_freq before commit 701853d?',
+ snip:`<span class="cm"># BEFORE</span>
+freq = self._dco_actual() / self.clock.mclk_div
+<span class="cm"># AFTER</span>
+freq = self._clock_source_frequency(self.clock.mclk_source) / self.clock.mclk_div`,
+ opts:[
+  {t:'It computed the frequency from the DCO unconditionally — the stored mclk_source was written by the SOURCE command but never read by the query',c:true, w:'Correct. The selection round-tripped (SOURCE? echoed it back) but had zero effect on FREQUENCY?. With MCLK on LFXT the query still said 1000000. Write-only state: stored, echoed, never consulted — the audit probe mclk_freq_ignores_source named it precisely.'},
+  {t:'It forgot to apply the divider',c:false,w:'The divider WAS applied (mclk_div is in both versions). The missing step was resolving the source before dividing.'},
+  {t:'It returned the raw float without formatting',c:false,w:'The f"{freq:.0f}" formatting existed before and after. The defect was upstream: the wrong base frequency.'},
+  {t:'It read smclk_source instead of mclk_source — a copy-paste slip',c:false,w:'It read no source field at all. All three clocks were hardwired: MCLK and SMCLK to _dco_actual(), ACLK to lfxt_frequency.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'c2',ch:'code',type:'w',
+ q:'Walk through _clock_source_frequency() added by 701853d: what does each branch return, which single branch carries fault-profile drift, and what does the final raise protect against?',
+ snip:`<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">_clock_source_frequency</span>(self, source: str) -> float:
+    <span class="kw">if</span> source == <span class="str">"DCO"</span>: <span class="kw">return</span> self._dco_actual()
+    <span class="kw">if</span> source == <span class="str">"LFXT"</span>: <span class="kw">return</span> self.clock.lfxt_frequency
+    <span class="kw">if</span> source == <span class="str">"HFXT"</span>: <span class="kw">return</span> self.clock.hfxt_frequency
+    <span class="kw">if</span> source == <span class="str">"VLO"</span>: <span class="kw">return</span> VLO_FREQUENCY_HZ
+    <span class="kw">if</span> source == <span class="str">"MODCLK"</span>: <span class="kw">return</span> MODCLK_FREQUENCY_HZ
+    <span class="kw">if</span> source == <span class="str">"LFMODCLK"</span>: <span class="kw">return</span> LFMODCLK_FREQUENCY_HZ
+    <span class="kw">raise</span> <span class="cls">ScpiError</span>(-<span class="num">221</span>, f<span class="str">"No simulated frequency for clock source {source}"</span>)`,
+ c:[{l:'DCO → _dco_actual() = dco_frequency × faults.dco_factor()',t:['_dco_actual','dco_factor','drift','× drift']},
+    {l:'LFXT/HFXT → the crystal fields on ClockState (32768 / 4e6 defaults)',t:['lfxt_frequency','hfxt_frequency','crystal','clockstate','32768']},
+    {l:'VLO/MODCLK/LFMODCLK → module constants 9400 / 4.8e6 / 37500',t:['9400','4.8e6','37500','constant','fixed']},
+    {l:'only the DCO branch is drift-affected',t:['only the dco','only dco','drift only','untouched','no drift']},
+    {l:'fallthrough raises -221 settings conflict — defends against unresolvable sources',t:['-221','raise','settings conflict','defensive','unknown','fallthrough']}],
+ m:`Branch by branch:
+- DCO → _dco_actual() = clock.dco_frequency × faults.dco_factor(). This is the ONLY drift-affected branch: NOMINAL profile returns ×1.0, CORNER applies the ±3.5% tolerance of SLASE54D.
+- LFXT → clock.lfxt_frequency (default 32768 — the watch crystal, a board property).
+- HFXT → clock.hfxt_frequency (default 4e6 — a documented simulator-only default, since real HFXT is whatever crystal the board fits).
+- VLO → VLO_FREQUENCY_HZ = 9400.0; MODCLK → MODCLK_FREQUENCY_HZ = 4.8e6; LFMODCLK → LFMODCLK_FREQUENCY_HZ = 4.8e6/128 = 37500. Fixed internal oscillators — no configuration register exists, so nothing to store.
+The final raise ScpiError(-221, "No simulated frequency for clock source …") is the same settings-conflict code the timer path uses for TACLK/INCLK (external inputs with no defined simulated frequency). Since the setters validate sources on the way in, this line is defensive — but it turns any future inconsistency (e.g. a new source added to the frozenset but not the resolver) into a loud queued error instead of a silently wrong number.`
+},
+
+{id:'c3',ch:'code',type:'mc',
+ q:'Why does ClockState carry hfxt_frequency: float = 4e6 with a comment calling it "a simulator-only default"?',
+ opts:[
+  {t:'HFXT is an external crystal with no hardware nominal — real parts configure only its RANGE via CSCTL4.HFFREQ (not modeled), so the model must invent a concrete value and be honest that it did',c:true, w:'Correct. VLO and MODCLK have datasheet-typical frequencies to model; HFXT\'s frequency is a board decision. The comment marks 4 MHz as a simulation convenience so HFXT-sourced clocks can resolve — the same honesty pattern as VeREF+ in lesson 0009, where board-supplied values are declared, not pretended.'},
+  {t:'4 MHz is the FR5994\'s factory-trimmed HFXT frequency',c:false,w:'There is no factory trim for an external crystal — the chip does not know what the board will fit. That is exactly why the comment says "no fixed nominal in hardware".'},
+  {t:'It caps HFXT so MCLK cannot exceed the 16-MHz device maximum',c:false,w:'No speed-limit enforcement is involved; it is simply a default base value for the resolver to return.'},
+  {t:'It is dead code — HFXT is not a selectable source',c:false,w:'HFXT is in VALID_MCLK_SMCLK_SOURCES (SELM/SELS encoding 5) and _clock_source_frequency has an HFXT branch that reads this field.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'c4',ch:'code',type:'mc',
+ q:'How did commit 701853d rewrite probe_mclk_freq_ignores_source?',
+ snip:`<span class="cm"># BEFORE: return freq == dev.clock.dco_frequency / dev.clock.mclk_div</span>
+<span class="cm"># AFTER:</span>
+<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">probe_mclk_freq_ignores_source</span>(dev):
+    <span class="str">"""MCLK:FREQUENCY? follows the selected source: LFXT-sourced MCLK reads LFXT/div."""</span>
+    dev.process(<span class="str">"CLOCK:MCLK:SOURCE LFXT"</span>)
+    freq = float(dev.process(<span class="str">"CLOCK:MCLK:FREQUENCY?"</span>))
+    <span class="kw">return</span> freq == dev.clock.lfxt_frequency / dev.clock.mclk_div`,
+ opts:[
+  {t:'The expected value flipped from dco_frequency/div to lfxt_frequency/div — the probe now asserts the source-aware behaviour instead of documenting the DCO lie',c:true, w:'Correct. Same stimulus (select LFXT, query), opposite expectation. Probes encode what SLAU367P says should happen; before the fix this one "passed" by confirming the bug existed, and rescoring it makes the compliant behaviour the permanent check.'},
+  {t:'The probe now selects VLO instead of LFXT',c:false,w:'The stimulus is unchanged (LFXT). Only the expected value — which oscillator the reported frequency must track — flipped.'},
+  {t:'The probe was inverted to return not(old_result)',c:false,w:'It was rewritten with an explicit correct expectation, not negated — negation would keep the bug as the reference point.'},
+  {t:'It now compares against a hardcoded 32768 literal',c:false,w:'It compares against dev.clock.lfxt_frequency / dev.clock.mclk_div — derived from live state, so the probe stays valid if a test changes the crystal value or divider.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'c5',ch:'code',type:'w',
+ q:'Two strict xfails were deleted from test_msp430_edge_cases.py by 701853d. Name both tests, state what each asserts, and explain why @pytest.mark.xfail(strict=True) was the right marker while the bugs were still open.',
+ c:[{l:'test_mclk_divider_64_rejected — DIVIDER 64 queues one error',t:['divider_64','divider 64','one error','errors(mcu) == 1']},
+    {l:'test_mclk_source_modclk_accepted — MODCLK (SELM encoding 4) accepted with no error',t:['modclk_accepted','modclk accepted','encoding 4','source modclk']},
+    {l:'strict xfail documents a known bug and FAILS the suite on unexpected pass',t:['strict','xpass','fails if it passes','known bug','documented']},
+    {l:'fixing the code forces the marker off — the test becomes a regression guard',t:['regression','forces','remove the marker','flip','promote']}],
+ m:`1. test_mclk_divider_64_rejected — sends CLOCK:MCLK:DIVIDER 64 and asserts errors(mcu) == 1, citing SLAU367P §3.3.3: DIVM encodings stop at /32.
+2. test_mclk_source_modclk_accepted — sends CLOCK:MCLK:SOURCE MODCLK and asserts it succeeds, citing SLAU367P §3.3.2: MODCLK is SELM encoding 4 (the old model rejected this documented source).
+While the bugs were open, each test asserted the CORRECT behaviour and was expected to fail — xfail keeps the suite green without deleting the knowledge. strict=True is the teeth: if the test unexpectedly passes (XPASS), the suite FAILS, so the xfail cannot rot. The moment 701853d made the behaviour compliant, the strict markers erupted, forcing the committer to delete the decorators and promote both tests to permanent regression guards — the test file doubles as a ledger of known non-compliances that automatically demands updating when reality changes.`
+},
+
+{id:'c6',ch:'code',type:'mc',
+ q:'A timer is configured with TIMERA0:CLOCK:SOURCE TACLK and INTERVAL? is queried. Which error results, and why is it the same code as _clock_source_frequency\'s fallthrough?',
