+<script>
+// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
+// QUESTION BANK  (26 questions — grounded in commit 4cb71a1)
+// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
+const BANK = [
+// ─── CHANNEL: regs ────────────────────────────────────────────
+{id:'g1',ch:'regs',type:'w',
+ q:'From memory, reproduce SLAU367P Table 12-1: for each PxDIR/PxREN/PxOUT combination, give the pin configuration. Which register changes meaning between rows, and what are its two meanings?',
+ c:[{l:'DIR=0, REN=0 → input, no pull (floats if undriven)',t:['float','no pull','ren=0','ren 0','unconnect']},
+    {l:'DIR=0, REN=1: OUT=0 → pulldown, OUT=1 → pullup',t:['pulldown','pull-down','pullup','pull-up']},
+    {l:'DIR=1 → output; PxOUT is the drive level',t:['output','drive level','driven']},
+    {l:'PxOUT is repurposed: drive level vs pull-direction select',t:['repurpos','two meanings','dual','second job','direction select','two roles']}],
+ m:`Table 12-1 (SLAU367P §12.2.4):
+- PxDIR=0, PxREN=0, PxOUT=x → input, no pull. The pin floats if nothing drives it.
+- PxDIR=0, PxREN=1, PxOUT=0 → input with PULL-DOWN.
+- PxDIR=0, PxREN=1, PxOUT=1 → input with PULL-UP.
+- PxDIR=1, PxREN=x → output; PxOUT is the driven level.
+PxOUT is the register that changes meaning — register repurposing. When the pin is an output it is the drive level; when the pin is an input with PxREN set, the same bit selects which rail the pull resistor ties to (0=GND, 1=VCC). PxREN itself only ever means "resistor connected or not" — one enable bit whose direction lives in another register.`
+},
+
+{id:'g2',ch:'regs',type:'mc',
+ q:'A pin has PxDIR=0 (input) and PxREN=1 (resistor enabled). Which register bit decides whether that resistor is a pull-up or a pull-down?',
+ snip:`<span class="cm"># silmulator/devices/msp430.py — the dataclass comment (AFTER 4cb71a1)</span>
+<span class="cm"># PxREN (SLAU367P §12.2.4, Table 12-1): one pull resistor enable bit per</span>
+<span class="cm"># pin. There is no independent pullup/pulldown register in hardware —</span>
+<span class="cm"># while a bit here is set, the matching PxOUT bit selects the pull</span>
+<span class="cm"># direction (0=pulldown, 1=pullup).</span>`,
+ opts:[
+  {t:'The pin\'s PxOUT bit — repurposed as the direction select while the pin is an input (0=pulldown, 1=pullup)',c:true, w:'Correct. PxOUT has two jobs: drive level on output pins, pull-direction select on REN-enabled input pins. That is why the model derives pullup = REN &amp; OUT and pulldown = REN &amp; ~OUT.'},
+  {t:'A second bit inside PxREN — it is a 2-bit-per-pin field',c:false,w:'PxREN is one bit per pin: enable only. There is no room in it for a direction; the direction lives in PxOUT.'},
+  {t:'PxDIR — direction means pull direction here',c:false,w:'PxDIR selects input vs output for the pin itself. The PULL direction is PxOUT\'s repurposed job.'},
+  {t:'A dedicated PxPUD register at the next address',c:false,w:'No such register exists on the MSP430 — that was effectively what the old model invented with its independent pullup/pulldown masks, and why it could express impossible states.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'g3',ch:'regs',type:'mc',
+ q:'After commit 4cb71a1, which of these is STORED state on GPIOPort, and which are computed?',
+ opts:[
+  {t:'Stored: direction, output, input, resistor_enable (plus interrupt fields); computed: pullup and pulldown as read-only @property views',c:true, w:'Correct. The commit deleted the stored pullup/pulldown masks, added resistor_enable (PxREN), and re-created pullup/pulldown as properties: REN &amp; OUT and REN &amp; ~OUT &amp; 0xFF. Store the doc registers, derive the concepts.'},
+  {t:'Stored: pullup, pulldown; computed: resistor_enable = pullup | pulldown',c:false,w:'Backwards — deriving REN from stored pull masks would still allow pullup &amp; pulldown overlap. The doc register (REN) is stored; the pull views are derived.'},
+  {t:'Everything is stored; the handlers keep pullup/pulldown in sync on every write',c:false,w:'That was a rejected design: synced copies rely on every writer remembering both. Properties make desync structurally impossible.'},
+  {t:'Everything is computed from a single 32-bit packed register',c:false,w:'The model keeps one int per documented register (direction, output, input, resistor_enable…), mirroring SLAU367P\'s register map, not a packed invention.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'g4',ch:'regs',type:'w',
+ q:'Define "register repurposing" as a general MCU pattern, and give at least two examples from this project\'s MSP430 model (one must be PxOUT). What rule does the pattern impose on simulator design?',
+ c:[{l:'one register, different meaning depending on other state',t:['depend','second','two roles','two jobs','different meaning','dual','reuse']},
+    {l:'PxOUT: drive level vs pull-direction select',t:['pxout','pull direction','drive level']},
+    {l:'TAxCCRn compare vs capture (CAP bit) / PxSEL muxing',t:['ccr','cap bit','capture','compare','pxsel','mux']},
+    {l:'model the registers, not one storage per meaning',t:['not one','invent','store the register','doc register','derive','flatten']}],
+ m:`Register repurposing: silicon designers give one register bit different meanings depending on other configuration state, instead of spending a new register per concept.
+Examples in this codebase:
+- PxOUT — the drive level when PxDIR=1, but the pull-direction select when PxDIR=0 and PxREN=1 (issue #21, this drill).
+- TAxCCRn — a compare value firmware writes, or a capture latch hardware writes, depending on the CAP bit in TAxCCTLn (lesson 0010's neighbourhood).
+- PxSEL combinations hand the whole pin to another peripheral function entirely.
+The rule it imposes: a simulator must store the DOCUMENTED registers and let meaning be contextual, rather than flattening each meaning into its own storage. The old model stored "pullup" and "pulldown" (meanings) instead of REN and OUT (registers) — and two stores for one truth can always be driven apart, which is exactly what probe_gpio_pull_both demonstrated.`
+},
+
+{id:'g5',ch:'regs',type:'mc',
+ q:'Why does the MSP430 repurpose PxOUT for pull direction instead of providing a dedicated pull-direction register?',
+ opts:[
+  {t:'When a pin is an input its PxOUT bit is otherwise idle — reusing it saves register address space and silicon, at the cost of one logical setting spanning two registers',c:true, w:'Correct. The OUT latch exists anyway and does nothing while PxDIR=0, so hardware gives it a second job. The price is exactly what this lesson is about: "pull-up on pin 3" is REN bit 3 AND OUT bit 3 — two registers, one setting.'},
+  {t:'Electrical necessity — the pull resistor physically connects to the output driver',c:false,w:'The resistor connects to a rail selected by the OUT latch\'s value, but that is a design choice; other MCU families do ship dedicated pull-direction registers (e.g. separate PUR/PDR). It is an economy, not physics.'},
+  {t:'It is a documentation error in SLAU367P that TI never fixed',c:false,w:'Table 12-1 is the specified, intentional behaviour — TI\'s own init examples write P1OUT to pick the pull direction.'},
+  {t:'To make pull-up and pull-down independently combinable on one pin',c:false,w:'The opposite: sharing one direction bit is precisely what makes them mutually exclusive per pin. Both-at-once has no encoding.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+// ─── CHANNEL: derive ──────────────────────────────────────────
+{id:'d1',ch:'derive',type:'mc',
+ q:'resistor_enable = 0x30 and output = 0x96. What do the derived views return? (0x96 = 1001 0110)',
+ snip:`<span class="at">@property</span>
+<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">pullup</span>(self) -> int:
+    <span class="kw">return</span> self.resistor_enable &amp; self.output &amp; <span class="num">0xFF</span>
+
+<span class="at">@property</span>
+<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">pulldown</span>(self) -> int:
+    <span class="kw">return</span> self.resistor_enable &amp; ~self.output &amp; <span class="num">0xFF</span>`,
+ opts:[
+  {t:'pullup = 0x10, pulldown = 0x20',c:true, w:'Correct. REN covers pins 5,4. OUT bit 4 = 1 → pin 4 pulls up: 0x30 &amp; 0x96 = 0x10. OUT bit 5 = 0 → pin 5 pulls down: 0x30 &amp; ~0x96 &amp; 0xFF = 0x30 &amp; 0x69 = 0x20. Union is 0x30 = REN, intersection is 0.'},
+  {t:'pullup = 0x20, pulldown = 0x10',c:false,w:'Reversed. Check bits: 0x96 = 1001 0110 has bit 4 set (0x10) and bit 5 clear (0x20). OUT=1 selects pull-UP, so pullup gets 0x10.'},
+  {t:'pullup = 0x30, pulldown = 0x00',c:false,w:'Only if OUT had both bits 5 and 4 set. Bit 5 of 0x96 is 0, so pin 5 pulls down.'},
+  {t:'pullup = 0x96, pulldown = 0x69',c:false,w:'Both views are masked by REN — bits without an enabled resistor never appear in either view. Only pins 5 and 4 can show up.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'d2',ch:'derive',type:'w',
+ q:'Write the two derived-view expressions exactly as the GPIOPort properties implement them, and explain why the trailing "&amp; 0xFF" is required in Python but would be unnecessary in C on a uint8_t.',
+ c:[{l:'pullup = resistor_enable &amp; output &amp; 0xFF',t:['resistor_enable & output','ren & out','ren&out']},
+    {l:'pulldown = resistor_enable &amp; ~output &amp; 0xFF',t:['~output','~out','& ~']},
+    {l:'Python ~ is unbounded / negative (two\'s complement of an infinite int)',t:['unbounded','infinite','negative','-','arbitrary precision','bignum']},
+    {l:'0xFF clamps the view to the port\'s 8 lines (width discipline)',t:['0xff','8 bits','8-bit','clamp','width','eight']}],
+ m:`pullup   = self.resistor_enable & self.output & 0xFF
+pulldown = self.resistor_enable & ~self.output & 0xFF
+The & 0xFF matters because Python ints are unbounded: ~0xA5 is -166, conceptually an infinite string of leading 1-bits, not 0x5A. Without the mask, pulldown could carry set bits above bit 7 whenever resistor_enable did (and the property must defend against that). ANDing with 0xFF clamps the result to the port's 8 physical I/O lines — the same explicit-width discipline lesson 0010 required for 16-bit timer registers. In C, a uint8_t variable is physically 8 bits, so ~out is already truncated by the type; Python has no such type, so the model must enforce width by hand.`
+},
+
+{id:'d3',ch:'derive',type:'mc',
+ q:'For ANY values of resistor_enable and output, what is pullup &amp; pulldown?',
+ opts:[
+  {t:'0 — identically: (REN &amp; OUT) &amp; (REN &amp; ~OUT) contains OUT &amp; ~OUT, which is always zero',c:true, w:'Correct. The mutual exclusion is algebraic, not enforced by validation code. No sequence of writes can make both views claim the same pin — the inexpressible both-pulls state became unrepresentable, which is stronger than being checked.'},
+  {t:'resistor_enable — every enabled pin appears in both',c:false,w:'REN is their UNION (pullup | pulldown), not their intersection. Each enabled pin appears in exactly one view, selected by its OUT bit.'},
+  {t:'It depends on the write order of PULLUP/PULLDOWN commands',c:false,w:'Write order determines which view each pin lands in, but the intersection is zero regardless — OUT bit is either 0 or 1, never both.'},
+  {t:'0xFF when all resistors are enabled',c:false,w:'Even with REN = 0xFF, each pin\'s OUT bit routes it to exactly one view. The AND is still 0.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'d4',ch:'derive',type:'mc',
+ q:'And what is pullup | pulldown, for any state?',
+ opts:[
+  {t:'resistor_enable — (REN &amp; OUT) | (REN &amp; ~OUT) = REN &amp; (OUT | ~OUT) = REN',c:true, w:'Correct. Every enabled resistor pulls exactly one way, so the two views partition REN. Together with pullup &amp; pulldown == 0, the pair of invariants fully characterises the fix: a partition of the enable mask, keyed by OUT.'},
+  {t:'output — the views reconstruct PxOUT',c:false,w:'Bits of OUT on pins without an enabled resistor appear in neither view. You cannot recover OUT from the views, only OUT &amp; REN.'},
+  {t:'0xFF — all pins are always either up or down',c:false,w:'Pins with REN clear have NO pull at all — they float (or read the injected value). Only enabled pins appear.'},
+  {t:'Undefined — properties can disagree transiently between writes',c:false,w:'Properties are computed at read time from one consistent snapshot of REN/OUT; there is no transient window, which is exactly why they replaced stored copies.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'d5',ch:'derive',type:'w',
+ q:'Define "inexpressible state", give issue #21\'s concrete example, and explain why a simulator that can represent one silently voids SIL test results.',
+ c:[{l:'a state with no hardware encoding / silicon cannot reach',t:['no encoding','cannot express','cannot reach','unreachable','no silicon','not exist','physically impossible']},
+    {l:'example: pull-up AND pull-down active on one pin (old independent masks)',t:['both pull','pullup and pulldown','same pin','simultaneous']},
+    {l:'tests that pass in such a state certify nothing about hardware',t:['certif','void','meaningless','blesses','pass in a state','not valid on hardware']},
+    {l:'ties to reachable state space (lesson 0010)',t:['reachable','state space','subset']}],
+ m:`An inexpressible state is a simulator configuration that no combination of register writes can produce on the real part — it lies outside the hardware's reachable state space (lesson 0010's term).
+Issue #21's example: with independent pullup/pulldown masks, "GPIO:PORT1:PULLUP 0x01" then "GPIO:PORT1:PULLDOWN 0x01" left both masks with bit 0 set — one pin pulling toward VCC and GND at once. On silicon there is one PxREN bit and one PxOUT direction bit per pin: both-pulls has no encoding.
+Why it voids SIL results: the whole value of software-in-the-loop is that behaviour observed in simulation transfers to hardware. A test that runs (and passes) while the simulated device sits in an unreachable configuration verifies firmware against physics that will never occur — the pass is evidence of nothing, and the miswritten firmware config that created the state ships unflagged. The fix makes the state unrepresentable: pullup & pulldown == 0 by construction, so the bug class cannot even be expressed to test.`
+},
+
+// ─── CHANNEL: alias ───────────────────────────────────────────
+{id:'a1',ch:'alias',type:'mc',
+ q:'Fresh device. GPIO:PORT1:PULLUP 0x03, then GPIO:PORT1:PULLDOWN 0x01. What are resistor_enable and output afterwards?',
+ snip:`<span class="cm"># _gpio_pullup write path:            # _gpio_pulldown write path:</span>
+p.resistor_enable |= mask             p.resistor_enable |= mask
+p.output |= mask                      p.output &amp;= ~mask`,
+ opts:[
+  {t:'resistor_enable = 0x03, output = 0x02 — pin 1 pulls up, pin 0 was retargeted to pull down',c:true, w:'Correct. PULLUP 0x03: REN 0x00→0x03, OUT 0x00→0x03. PULLDOWN 0x01: REN |= 0x01 (still 0x03), OUT &amp;= ~0x01 → 0x02. Queries: PULLUP? = 0x03 &amp; 0x02 = 0x02; PULLDOWN? = 0x03 &amp; ~0x02 = 0x01. Last write per pin wins.'},
+  {t:'resistor_enable = 0x02, output = 0x03 — the PULLDOWN write disabled pin 0\'s resistor',c:false,w:'PULLDOWN never clears REN — it ORs the mask in (the resistor stays connected) and clears the OUT bit (points it at GND).'},
+  {t:'resistor_enable = 0x03, output = 0x03 — PULLDOWN only records intent in its own mask',c:false,w:'There is no "own mask" anymore. The alias composes the real registers, and its OUT &amp;= ~mask is what flips pin 0 to pull-down.'},
+  {t:'An error is queued — the pin already has a pull configured',c:false,w:'No error: reconfiguring a pull is a legal register write on hardware too. The second command simply retargets pin 0.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'a2',ch:'alias',type:'w',
+ q:'Describe exactly what a PULLUP write and a PULLDOWN write do to stored state after 4cb71a1, what their queries return, and why the commit message calls them aliases that "compose the real registers".',
+ c:[{l:'PULLUP mask: REN |= mask; OUT |= mask',t:['|= mask','or the mask','out |=','sets ren and out']},
+    {l:'PULLDOWN mask: REN |= mask; OUT &amp;= ~mask',t:['&= ~','clears out','clear the out']},
+    {l:'queries return the derived views REN &amp; OUT / REN &amp; ~OUT',t:['derived','ren & out','ren & ~out','property','view']},
+    {l:'no state of their own — every effect lands in REN/OUT',t:['no state','no own','compose','single source','real registers','deprecated']}],
+ m:`Write paths (mask = int(cmd.params[0], 0) & 0xFF):
+- PULLUP mask: p.resistor_enable |= mask; p.output |= mask — connect the resistor(s) AND point them at VCC.
+- PULLDOWN mask: p.resistor_enable |= mask; p.output &= ~mask — connect the resistor(s) AND point them at GND.
+Queries return the derived properties: PULLUP? → f"0x{p.pullup:02X}" (REN & OUT), PULLDOWN? → f"0x{p.pulldown:02X}" (REN & ~OUT & 0xFF).
+"Compose the real registers" means the aliases own no storage: every observable effect is a combination of writes to resistor_enable and output — the two registers SLAU367P documents. Old scripts keep working (backward compatibility is the only reason the commands survive, marked Deprecated in their docstrings), but they can no longer construct a state the registers can't hold: PULLDOWN after PULLUP retargets pins instead of contradicting them.`
+},
+
+{id:'a3',ch:'alias',type:'mc',
+ q:'Why did commit 4cb71a1 keep PULLUP/PULLDOWN at all instead of deleting them along with the stored masks?',
+ opts:[
+  {t:'Backward compatibility — existing SCPI scripts and tests use them; re-expressed as compositions of REN/OUT they stay correct while impossible states stay impossible',c:true, w:'Correct. Deleting them would break every existing user for no semantic gain. The deprecation pattern: keep the old surface, reroute it through the new single source of truth, document the preferred replacement (RESISTOR:ENABLE + OUTPUT).'},
+  {t:'SLAU367P documents PULLUP and PULLDOWN commands',c:false,w:'It documents registers (PxREN/PxOUT), not SCPI commands. The vendor-doc diff actually classifies PULLUP/PULLDOWN as MISSING_FROM_GENERATED — simulator conveniences with no doc basis.'},
+  {t:'The GUI depends on them and cannot be updated',c:false,w:'Nothing in the commit says that; the motivation in the docstrings is the alias/deprecation relationship to RESISTOR:ENABLE.'},
+  {t:'They are faster than separate REN and OUTPUT writes',c:false,w:'Convenience, not performance — one command instead of two is ergonomics; the reason they SURVIVE is compatibility.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'a4',ch:'alias',type:'mc',
+ q:'A script sends GPIO:PORT1:PULLUP 0x80 and later switches pin 7 to output. Which hidden side effect of the alias matters here?',
+ opts:[
+  {t:'The alias set OUT bit 7, so the moment pin 7 becomes an output it drives HIGH — PxOUT resumed its drive-level role',c:true, w:'Correct. PULLUP writes OUT as well as REN, because on hardware the pull-up IS "OUT bit = 1". Register repurposing cuts both ways: that same latch is the drive level once PxDIR flips. The identical write sequence on a real FR5994 also drives high.'},
+  {t:'The alias set DIR bit 7, making the pin an output immediately',c:false,w:'Neither alias touches direction — only resistor_enable and output.'},
+  {t:'The resistor keeps pulling while the pin drives, causing a simulated short',c:false,w:'The model\'s input read puts driven pins entirely under OUT &amp; DIR; there is no contention model. The interesting side effect is the OUT bit itself.'},
+  {t:'Nothing — aliases only affect derived views, not stored registers',c:false,w:'Exactly backwards: aliases affect ONLY stored registers (REN, OUT); the views are derived from them.'},
+ ]
