+{id:'v5',ch:'valid',type:'w',
+ q:'Trace GPIO:PORT1:RESISTOR:ENABLE through the vendor-doc diff: its classification before and after 4cb71a1, what changed in the generated JSON entry, and where PULLUP/PULLDOWN landed. What is the CONFLICT count now?',
+ c:[{l:'before: CONFLICT (doc feature existed only as structurally different PULLUP/PULLDOWN)',t:['conflict']},
+    {l:'after: MATCH (model implements the doc command directly)',t:['match']},
+    {l:'JSON: model_equivalent/equivalence_note removed; valid_values + echo_check added',t:['model_equivalent','equivalence_note','valid_values','echo_check']},
+    {l:'PULLUP/PULLDOWN → MISSING_FROM_GENERATED (simulator conveniences); CONFLICT count 1 → 0',t:['missing_from_generated','conveniences','0','zero','last']}],
+ m:`Before: the doc-derived spec (custom_blocks/msp430fr5994_generated.json) listed GPIO:PORT1:RESISTOR:ENABLE citing SLAU367P §12.2.4, but the model only had PULLUP/PULLDOWN. The entry carried model_equivalent = ["GPIO:PORT1:PULLUP", "GPIO:PORT1:PULLDOWN"] plus an equivalence_note explaining the structural divergence (independent masks can express both pulls — a state the hardware cannot). The diff pinned it as the report's single CONFLICT.
+After: the model registers GPIO:PORTn:RESISTOR:ENABLE directly, so the JSON entry dropped the escape hatch and gained an ordinary probe: valid_values ["0x00","0xFF"] with echo_check true. Classification flipped CONFLICT → MATCH (test_pull_resistor_conflict_resolved asserts both directions: not in conflicts, in matches).
+PULLUP and PULLDOWN now appear as MISSING_FROM_GENERATED — implemented by the model, claimed by no doc entry — the correct bucket for simulator conveniences, alongside TOGGLE/SET/CLEAR.
+Counts: MATCH 17 → 18, MISSING_FROM_GENERATED 20 → 22, CONFLICT 1 → 0 — and test_conflicts_are_warnings_not_blockers now asserts counts()[CONFLICT] == 0.`
+},
