+ q:'A script sends CPU:PC 0x4001. What happens?',
+ snip:`<span class="kw">if not</span> <span class="num">0</span> &lt;= v &lt;= <span class="num">0xFFFFF</span>:
+    <span class="kw">raise</span> <span class="cls">ScpiError</span>(-<span class="num">222</span>, <span class="str">"PC out of range (0-0xFFFFF)"</span>)
+<span class="kw">if</span> v &amp; <span class="num">1</span>:
+    <span class="kw">raise</span> <span class="cls">ScpiError</span>(-<span class="num">222</span>, <span class="str">"PC must be word-aligned (bit 0 is always 0)"</span>)`,
+ opts:[
+  {t:'-222 "PC must be word-aligned (bit 0 is always 0)" is queued and the PC is unchanged',c:true, w:'Correct. 0x4001 is in range (≤ 0xFFFFF) but 0x4001 & 1 = 1, so the alignment check raises. SLAU367P §4.3.1: instructions are word-aligned; bit 0 of the PC is hardwired to 0 — an odd PC has no silicon state.'},
+  {t:'The PC is rounded down to 0x4000',c:false,w:'The model rejects, it never "fixes up" — silently coercing would hide the caller\'s bug, which is the opposite of what a validating simulator is for.'},
+  {t:'The PC is stored as 0x4001 with no error',c:false,w:'That was the pre-07f1f69 ACCEPTS_INVALID behaviour. The audit probe now requires odd values to queue exactly one error and leave dev._pc untouched.'},
+  {t:'-224 "Illegal parameter value" is queued',c:false,w:'The handler uses -222 "Data out of range" for both PC checks — same code the timer registers use for over-width writes. -224 is the illegal-value code used elsewhere (e.g. ADC:REFERENCE BANANA).'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'p2',ch:'pc',type:'mc',
+ q:'CPU:PC 0x123457 violates BOTH rules (too wide and odd). Which error message does the caller actually see?',
+ opts:[
+  {t:'"PC out of range (0-0xFFFFF)" — the range check runs first and raises before the alignment check executes',c:true, w:'Correct. 0x123457 = 1,193,047 > 1,048,575. The checks are sequential; raise aborts the handler, so the odd bit is never examined. probe_cpu_pc_validated uses exactly this value and asserts one error, PC unchanged.'},
+  {t:'"PC must be word-aligned (bit 0 is always 0)"',c:false,w:'The alignment check is second. The range check raises first for 0x123457, so the word-aligned message is only seen for in-range odd values like 0x4001.'},
+  {t:'Both errors are queued, one per violated rule',c:false,w:'raise aborts the handler at the first failed check — one command, at most one queued error here.'},
+  {t:'Neither — the value is masked to 0x23457, which is in range',c:false,w:'The PC handler validates and rejects; masking is the REGISTER policy. Coercing 0x123457 into 0x23457 would silently change the write\'s meaning.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'p3',ch:'pc',type:'w',
+ q:'Write out the two validation rules _cpu_pc enforces after 07f1f69, the SCPI error each raises, and the hardware fact behind each rule.',
+ c:[{l:'rule 1: 0 <= v <= 0xFFFFF (20-bit space)',t:['0xfffff','range','20-bit','20 bit']},
+    {l:'rule 2: v & 1 == 0 (even / word-aligned)',t:['v & 1','odd','even','word-align','bit 0']},
+    {l:'both raise ScpiError -222',t:['-222']},
+    {l:'hardware: 1-MB address space; PC bit 0 hardwired 0 (SLAU367P §4.3.1)',t:['hardwired','always 0','1 mb','slau367','4.3.1','instructions are word']}],
+ m:`Rule 1 — range: the value must satisfy 0 <= v <= 0xFFFFF, else ScpiError(-222, "PC out of range (0-0xFFFFF)"). Hardware fact: CPUX addresses are 20 bits — 1 MB, 0x00000-0xFFFFF. There is no 21st address bit.
+Rule 2 — alignment: v & 1 must be 0, else ScpiError(-222, "PC must be word-aligned (bit 0 is always 0)"). Hardware fact: SLAU367P §4.3.1 — instructions are word-aligned and bit 0 of the PC is hardwired to 0; no instruction fetch, jump, or debugger poke can create an odd PC.
+The checks run in that order, so a value violating both (0x123457) reports the range message. Both use -222 "Data out of range", matching the policy lesson 0010 established for the 16-bit timer registers.`
+},
+
+{id:'p4',ch:'pc',type:'mc',
+ q:'Why does commit 07f1f69 REJECT an over-wide PC (-222) but MASK an over-wide register write (& 0xFFFFF)? Both handlers were fixed in the same commit.',
+ opts:[
+  {t:'Masking mirrors what physical flip-flops do with a data write; an odd/over-wide PC corresponds to no reachable hardware state, so it is refused loudly',c:true, w:'Correct. Two hardware truths, two policies: a data register genuinely truncates to its width (write masking), while PC bit 0 is architecturally hardwired to 0 — accepting or "fixing" an impossible PC would let SIL tests exercise fictional states. The audit scored the old PC handler ACCEPTS_INVALID for exactly this.'},
+  {t:'The register handler was fixed by a different author with a different style',c:false,w:'Both changes are in the same commit with the same SLAU367P citations. The asymmetry is deliberate modelling, not style drift.'},
+  {t:'Masking is faster than raising an exception',c:false,w:'Performance is irrelevant at SCPI-command rates. The distinction is fidelity: which behaviour matches the silicon.'},
+  {t:'The PC cannot be masked because it is stored in a different data type',c:false,w:'Both are plain Python ints. The difference is policy, chosen per the hardware semantics of each register.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'p5',ch:'pc',type:'mc',
+ q:'Which of these CPU:PC writes is ACCEPTED by the fixed handler?',
+ opts:[
+  {t:'CPU:PC 0xFFFFE',c:true, w:'Correct. 0xFFFFE = 1,048,574: even (bit 0 clear) and ≤ 0xFFFFF — the highest legal PC value. Both checks pass.'},
+  {t:'CPU:PC 0xFFFFF',c:false,w:'0xFFFFF is in range but ODD (ends in F → bit 0 set), so it raises the word-aligned -222. The largest legal PC is 0xFFFFE.'},
+  {t:'CPU:PC 0x100000',c:false,w:'0x100000 = 1,048,576 = 2^20 is one past the top of the 20-bit space (max 0xFFFFF), so the range check raises -222.'},
+  {t:'CPU:PC -2',c:false,w:'Negative values fail the 0 <= v side of the range check. Even-ness does not save a value outside the address space.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+// ─── CHANNEL: reset ───────────────────────────────────────────
+{id:'r1',ch:'reset',type:'w',
+ q:'Walk through _reset_vector_pc() line by line: what does it read, how is the word assembled, when does the 0x4000 default apply, and what happens when only ONE vector byte has been written?',
+ snip:`<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">_reset_vector_pc</span>(self) -> int:
+    lo = self._memory.get(<span class="num">0xFFFE</span>)
+    hi = self._memory.get(<span class="num">0xFFFF</span>)
+    <span class="kw">if</span> lo <span class="kw">is</span> <span class="cls">None</span> <span class="kw">and</span> hi <span class="kw">is</span> <span class="cls">None</span>:
+        <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="num">0x4000</span>
+    <span class="kw">return</span> ((hi <span class="kw">or</span> <span class="num">0</span>) &lt;&lt; <span class="num">8</span>) | (lo <span class="kw">or</span> <span class="num">0</span>)`,
+ c:[{l:'reads bytes at 0xFFFE (lo) and 0xFFFF (hi)',t:['0xfffe','0xffff','lo','hi']},
+    {l:'little-endian word: (hi << 8) | lo',t:['little-endian','little endian','<< 8','hi << 8','shift']},
+    {l:'0x4000 default ONLY when both bytes are None (unwritten)',t:['both','none','unwritten','never been written','and hi is none']},
+    {l:'half-written vector honoured as-is: missing byte treated as 0',t:['or 0','half','one byte','treated as 0','as-is','honoured']}],
+ m:`1. It reads the two vector bytes from the memory dict: lo at 0xFFFE, hi at 0xFFFF. MSP430 is little-endian, so the LOWER address holds the LOW byte.
+2. If BOTH are None — the vector has never been written — it returns the default 0x4000, the FR5994's FRAM code start (SLASE54D memory map).
+3. Otherwise it assembles the little-endian word: ((hi or 0) << 8) | (lo or 0). With lo=0x00, hi=0xC0 → 0xC000.
+4. A half-written vector does NOT trigger the default (the guard requires both to be None): write only 0xFFFE,0x34 and reset gives (0 << 8) | 0x34 = 0x0034. The missing byte is treated as 0 and the result is honoured as-is — garbage in the vector produces a faithful garbage jump, like hardware.
+Both _cpu_reset and the *RST path call this function; reset is a fetch, not a constant.`
+},
+
+{id:'r2',ch:'reset',type:'mc',
+ q:'A test runs: MEMORY:WRITE 0xFFFE,0x00 → MEMORY:WRITE 0xFFFF,0xC0 → CPU:RESET → CPU:PC?. What string comes back?',
+ opts:[
+  {t:'0xC000',c:true, w:'Correct. Little-endian word read: (0xC0 << 8) | 0x00 = 49,152 = 0xC000. This is the vector_ok half of probe_cpu_reset_vector_pc.'},
+  {t:'0x00C0',c:false,w:'0x00C0 would be the big-endian reading. 0xFFFE holds the LOW byte (0x00 here); 0xFFFF holds the HIGH byte (0xC0).'},
+  {t:'0x4000',c:false,w:'The 0x4000 default applies only when NEITHER vector byte has been written. Both were written here, so the fetched word wins.'},
+  {t:'0xFFFE',c:false,w:'0xFFFE is the vector\'s ADDRESS, not its content. Reset loads the word stored AT 0xFFFE/0xFFFF into the PC.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'r3',ch:'reset',type:'mc',
+ q:'Why did the audit score the old "self._pc = 0xC000" reset as NON_COMPLIANT — what two distinct things were wrong with it?',
+ opts:[
+  {t:'Reset should FETCH the PC from the vector at 0xFFFE (mechanism), and 0xC000 is not even the right constant for the FR5994, whose code FRAM starts at 0x4000 (value)',c:true, w:'Correct. Mechanism: a real BOR/POR/PUC ends with a memory read of the reset vector — the entry point belongs to the firmware image, not the silicon. Value: 0xC000 is the flash start of older, smaller MSP430s; the audit\'s wording is "0xC000 is not FR5994-specific (code FRAM starts at 0x4000)".'},
+  {t:'Only that 0xC000 is odd and therefore an illegal PC',c:false,w:'0xC000 is even and perfectly legal as a PC value. The problems were the hardcoded MECHANISM (no vector fetch) and the wrong-part CONSTANT.'},
+  {t:'Only that the constant should have been 0x4000',c:false,w:'Swapping one constant for another would still fail the probe: probe_cpu_reset_vector_pc requires that a WRITTEN vector (0xC000 via MEMORY:WRITE) actually redirects reset. The fetch mechanism is the core fix; 0x4000 is only the unwritten-vector fallback.'},
+  {t:'CPU:RESET should not touch the PC at all',c:false,w:'Reset absolutely must set the PC — that is its defining effect. The question is WHERE the value comes from: the vector word, not a literal in the Python source.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'r4',ch:'reset',type:'w',
+ q:'The PC is 20 bits wide, yet reset can never land above 0xFFFF. Explain why that is faithful to hardware, and how code in extended FRAM (0x10000+) ever gets executed.',
+ c:[{l:'MSP430X interrupt/reset vectors are still 16-bit words',t:['16-bit word','16 bit','vectors are','stayed 16']},
+    {l:'(hi << 8) | lo has a ceiling of 0xFFFF',t:['0xffff','ceiling','cannot exceed','max']},
+    {l:'entry point must live in the lower 64 KB',t:['lower 64','below 0x10000','64 kb','64kb']},
+    {l:'upper FRAM reached after startup via CALLA/BRA (20-bit targets)',t:['calla','bra','extended instruction','after startup','20-bit target']}],
+ m:`MSP430X widened the registers and the address space but kept the interrupt/reset vector table as 16-bit words (the table sits at 0xFF80-0xFFFF). _reset_vector_pc() mirrors this exactly: ((hi or 0) << 8) | (lo or 0) is built from two bytes and mathematically cannot exceed 0xFFFF.
+Consequently the reset entry point — and every interrupt service routine entry — must live in the lower 64 KB. That is a real constraint linkers enforce on FR5994 projects.
+Code in extended FRAM (0x10000-0x43FFF, 208 KB of the FR5994\'s 256 KB) is reached only after startup, using the extended instructions whose operands carry full 20-bit addresses: CALLA, BRA, and the .A forms. A model whose reset could land at 0x28000 would ACCEPT a state real silicon cannot produce.`
+},
+
+{id:'r5',ch:'reset',type:'mc',
+ q:'Only MEMORY:WRITE 0xFFFE,0x34 has been executed (0xFFFF untouched). What PC does the next CPU:RESET produce?',
+ opts:[
+  {t:'0x0034 — the guard needs BOTH bytes unwritten for the default; the missing high byte is treated as 0',c:true, w:'Correct. lo = 0x34, hi = None → the "lo is None and hi is None" guard is false, so the default is skipped and PC = ((None or 0) << 8) | 0x34 = 0x0034. A half-written vector is honoured as-is — the model does not "fix up" partial state.'},
+  {t:'0x4000 — the default applies whenever the vector is incomplete',c:false,w:'The default requires lo is None AND hi is None. One written byte disables it — check the boolean carefully.'},
+  {t:'0x3400 — the single byte is taken as the high byte',c:false,w:'0xFFFE is the LOW byte of the little-endian word. The high byte (0xFFFF) is the one missing, and it defaults to 0.'},
+  {t:'-222 is raised because 0x0034 is not a plausible entry point',c:false,w:'_reset_vector_pc() performs no validation — like hardware, reset loads whatever the vector holds. 0x0034 is even and in range anyway.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+// ─── CHANNEL: code ────────────────────────────────────────────
+{id:'c1',ch:'code',type:'mc',
+ q:'What exactly does the renamed audit probe probe_cpu_reg_20bit assert?',
+ snip:`<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">probe_cpu_reg_20bit</span>(dev: <span class="cls">MSP430</span>) -> bool:
+    <span class="str">"""CPUX registers are 20-bit; the model masks writes to 20 bits (0xFFFFF)."""</span>
+    dev.process(<span class="str">"CPU:REGISTER 4,0x12345"</span>)
+    <span class="kw">return</span> dev.process(<span class="str">"CPU:REGISTER? 4"</span>) == <span class="str">"0x12345"</span>`,
+ opts:[
+  {t:'Writing 0x12345 to R4 and querying it returns the exact string "0x12345" — the 20-bit value survives the round trip',c:true, w:'Correct. The probe was probe_cpu_reg_16bit, which asserted the BUG (read-back == "0x2345"). Renamed and flipped, it now asserts compliance: bits 19:16 survive. Note it compares the formatted string, so it also pins the 04X minimum-width formatting.'},
+  {t:'Writing 0x12345 queues a -222 error',c:false,w:'Register writes mask, they never reject on width. No error is involved in this probe.'},
+  {t:'The register file has exactly 16 registers',c:false,w:'The 0-15 index range is checked by a different path (-222 "Register number out of range"). This probe is about VALUE width.'},
+  {t:'R4 is cleared to 0 by CPU:RESET',c:false,w:'That is _cpu_reset behaviour (registers = [0]*16), tested elsewhere. This probe never resets.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'c2',ch:'code',type:'w',
+ q:'probe_cpu_reset_vector_pc has two phases. Describe each phase\'s SCPI traffic and expected PC, and explain why the probe needs both to pass.',
+ c:[{l:'phase 1: CPU:RESET with unwritten vector → PC == 0x4000 default',t:['0x4000','default','unwritten','default_ok']},
+    {l:'phase 2: MEMORY:WRITE 0xFFFE,0x00 + 0xFFFF,0xC0 + reset → 0xC000',t:['0xc000','0xfffe','0xffff','vector_ok','memory:write']},
+    {l:'both required: default alone could be a new constant; fetch alone misses the fallback',t:['both','constant','fallback','would pass with','and']}],
+ m:`Phase 1 (default_ok): send CPU:RESET with the vector bytes never written; assert dev._pc == 0x4000 — the FR5994 FRAM-code-start fallback.
+Phase 2 (vector_ok): write the vector little-endian — MEMORY:WRITE 0xFFFE,0x00 then MEMORY:WRITE 0xFFFF,0xC0 — reset again, and assert dev._pc == 0xC000 = (0xC0 << 8) | 0x00.
+The probe returns default_ok AND vector_ok. Both are needed because each alone can be satisfied by a wrong implementation: a model that just swapped the constant 0xC000 for 0x4000 passes phase 1 but fails phase 2 (the written vector would be ignored); a model that always fetched but defaulted to something arbitrary — or crashed on an empty memory dict — would fail phase 1. Together they pin the real semantics: reset FETCHES the vector, with a part-accurate fallback when none exists.`
+},
+
+{id:'c3',ch:'code',type:'mc',
+ q:'Commit 07f1f69 also edited probe_wdt_stub, which previously ended with "dev._pc == 0xC000". Why, and what is the new assertion?',
+ snip:`pc_before = dev._pc
+dev.process(<span class="str">"WDT:PASSWORD 0x1234"</span>)  <span class="cm"># wrong password: real part resets</span>
+state = dev.process(<span class="str">"WDT:STATE?"</span>)
+<span class="kw">return</span> state == <span class="str">"RUNNING"</span> <span class="kw">and</span> _errors(dev) == <span class="num">0</span> <span class="kw">and</span> dev._pc == pc_before`,
+ opts:[
+  {t:'The probe\'s real claim was "a wrong WDT password does NOT reset the CPU (PC unchanged)"; it had hardcoded the old reset constant, so it now captures pc_before and compares against that',c:true, w:'Correct. dev._pc == 0xC000 only meant "unchanged" while reset happened to leave PC at 0xC000. Once reset became a vector fetch, the constant became wrong — the fix decouples the WDT probe from the reset implementation by asserting against a captured baseline.'},
+  {t:'The WDT now resets the PC to 0x4000 on a wrong password',c:false,w:'No — the WDT is still a stub (that is the finding). The probe asserts the PC does NOT change; only the way "does not change" is expressed was fixed.'},
+  {t:'The probe was deleted because WDT behaviour became compliant',c:false,w:'It was edited, not deleted; WDT remains a stub and the probe still documents that a wrong password fails to cause a PUC.'},
+  {t:'0xC000 became an illegal PC value after the validation was added',c:false,w:'0xC000 is even and in range — perfectly legal. The issue was coupling the WDT probe to a reset-behaviour constant that no longer exists.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'c4',ch:'code',type:'mc',
