+{id:'c5',ch:'code',type:'w',
+ q:'The commit deleted an @pytest.mark.xfail(strict=True) decorator from test_lpm35_accepted. Explain the strict-xfail ledger pattern: what the marker meant before the fix, and what strict=True forces to happen at fix time.',
+ c:[{l:'xfail documents a KNOWN non-compliance as an expected failure',t:['known','expected failure','documents','ledger','audit finding']},
+    {l:'strict=True makes an unexpected PASS (XPASS) fail the suite',t:['xpass','strict','fails the suite','unexpectedly passes']},
+    {l:'fixing the behaviour forces removing the marker → test becomes a regression guard',t:['remove','promote','regression guard','forces']},
+    {l:'here: LPM3.5 (SLAU367P §2.2.4) was rejected; now accepted alongside LPM4.5',t:['lpm3.5','2.2.4','accepted','valid set','lpm4.5']}],
+ m:`Before the fix, test_lpm35_accepted asserted the CORRECT behaviour (SLAU367P §2.2.4: "LPM3.5 and LPM4.5 are additional low-power modes") but was decorated @pytest.mark.xfail(strict=True) because the model rejected LPM3.5 — the VALID set in _pm_mode was missing it. The marker turns the test into a ledger entry: a documented, cited, known non-compliance that still runs on every CI pass.
+strict=True is the enforcement half: if the test ever unexpectedly passes (XPASS), the suite FAILS. So the moment 07f1f69 added "LPM3.5" to the VALID set, the strict xfail forced the author to delete the decorator — promoting the test to a plain regression guard in the same commit as the fix. The ledger cannot silently drift out of date.
+Same pattern lesson 0009 showed for the ADC reference tests; here the commit also updated the audit finding to lpm35_accepted while keeping POWER:MODE PARTIAL (modes still do not affect clocks).`
+},
+
+{id:'c6',ch:'code',type:'mc',
+ q:'The register/PC query paths still format with f"0x{value:04X}". Why did the 20-bit fix NOT require changing this format string?',
+ opts:[
+  {t:'04X is a minimum width with zero-padding — values needing five hex digits print all five, so 0x12345 renders correctly as-is',c:true, w:'Correct. Python\'s width specifier pads short values (0x345 → "0x0345") but never truncates long ones (0x12345 → "0x12345", 0xFFFFF → "0xFFFFF"). probe_cpu_reg_20bit\'s string comparison against "0x12345" quietly proves this.'},
+  {t:'It DID change — the commit rewrote it to 05X',c:false,w:'The diff leaves the format untouched. 04X already handles five-digit values because it is a floor, not a ceiling.'},
+  {t:'Because queries return integers, not strings',c:false,w:'SCPI responses are strings — the handler returns the formatted f-string, which is exactly what the probe compares against.'},
+  {t:'Because the mask guarantees values fit in four hex digits',c:false,w:'Backwards: the new 0xFFFFF mask allows values that NEED five digits. It works because 04X is only a minimum width.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+// ─── CHANNEL: families ────────────────────────────────────────
+{id:'f1',ch:'families',type:'w',
+ q:'Sort these into the two reset-flow camps and describe each camp\'s mechanism: MSP430, AVR, ARM Cortex-M, 6502, 8051, x86.',
+ c:[{l:'pointer-fetch camp: MSP430, 6502, Cortex-M — reset READS an address from memory',t:['pointer','fetch','6502','cortex','reads an address','dereference']},
+    {l:'execute-at-address camp: AVR, 8051, x86 — reset JUMPS to a fixed location holding code',t:['avr','8051','x86','fixed address','instruction','jumps','execute']},
+    {l:'MSP430: word at 0xFFFE, little-endian; 6502: 0xFFFC/0xFFFD',t:['0xfffe','0xfffc','little-endian','little endian']},
+    {l:'Cortex-M fetches TWO words: SP from 0x0, handler from 0x4',t:['sp','0x00000000','0x4','two words','stack pointer']}],
+ m:`Pointer-fetch camp — reset performs a memory READ and the vector holds an ADDRESS (data):
+- MSP430: PC ← little-endian word at 0xFFFE/0xFFFF (top of the 64-KB map).
+- 6502: PC ← little-endian word at 0xFFFC/0xFFFD — the closest cousin to the MSP430 flow.
+- ARM Cortex-M: TWO fetches — initial SP from 0x00000000, reset-handler address from 0x00000004 (bit 0 set = Thumb).
+Execute-at-fixed-address camp — reset performs a JUMP and the reset location holds CODE:
+- AVR: execution begins at flash 0x0000 (the "vector" is usually a JMP instruction). Harvard architecture — the vector is not even in the data address space.
+- 8051: execution begins at 0x0000.
+- x86: execution begins at physical 0xFFFFFFF0, which holds a far jump into firmware.
+Silmulator\'s _reset_vector_pc() only works as a plain memory-dict read because the MSP430 is von Neumann AND pointer-fetch.`
+},
+
+{id:'f2',ch:'families',type:'mc',
+ q:'Which other CPU\'s reset flow most closely mirrors the MSP430\'s?',
+ opts:[
+  {t:'The 6502 — hardware fetches a little-endian pointer from a vector near the top of the 64-KB map (0xFFFC/0xFFFD vs 0xFFFE/0xFFFF)',c:true, w:'Correct. Same camp (pointer-fetch), same endianness, same top-of-memory table, single word loaded into PC only. The differences are just the two-byte offset and the surrounding vector-table layout.'},
+  {t:'The AVR — both are low-power MCUs so both start at address 0',c:false,w:'AVR is the OTHER camp: execution begins at flash 0x0000, where an instruction (not a pointer) lives — and it\'s Harvard, so the vector isn\'t data-addressable at all.'},
+  {t:'The x86 — both fetch from the top of memory',c:false,w:'x86 EXECUTES at 0xFFFFFFF0 (the location holds a jump instruction); MSP430 READS 0xFFFE (the location holds an address). Same neighbourhood, opposite mechanism.'},
+  {t:'ARM Cortex-M — identical single-word fetch',c:false,w:'Cortex-M is the same camp but fetches TWO words (SP from 0x0, handler from 0x4, table at the BOTTOM of memory, Thumb bit set). The 6502 is the near-twin.'},
+ ]
+},
+
+{id:'f3',ch:'families',type:'mc',
+ q:'A startup test ported from a Cortex-M harness asserts that after reset the entry address read back from the core has bit 0 SET. On the MSP430 model this is impossible. Why?',
+ opts:[
+  {t:'Bit 0 semantics invert between the families: Cortex-M vectors carry the Thumb bit (bit 0 = 1), while MSP430 PC bit 0 is hardwired to 0 and CPU:PC rejects odd values with -222',c:true, w:'Correct. On Cortex-M, an even reset-handler address is the broken case (a fault at boot); on MSP430, an ODD PC is the impossible case. Any harness that carries PC assertions across the two families inverts its own expectations.'},
+  {t:'The MSP430 model has no way to read the PC back',c:false,w:'CPU:PC? returns the formatted PC. The impossibility is the odd VALUE, not the query.'},
+  {t:'MSP430 vectors are write-protected',c:false,w:'MEMORY:WRITE 0xFFFE/0xFFFF works fine — that is how tests load the vector. The clash is the bit-0 meaning.'},
