+<section>
+<h2>4. Reject vs Mask — the Policy Choice for Over-Width Writes</h2>
+
+<p>When a write doesn't fit the register, a simulator has three options. Only one is wrong:</p>
+
+<table>
+  <tr><th>Policy</th><th>Behaviour on <code>COUNT 0x12345</code></th><th>Verdict</th></tr>
+  <tr><td><strong>Store verbatim</strong></td><td><code>count = 74565</code> — a state no TAxR can hold</td><td>❌ The bug. Every later read is fiction.</td></tr>
+  <tr><td><strong>Mask</strong> (truncate to width)</td><td><code>count = 0x12345 &amp; 0xFFFF = 0x2345</code></td><td>✓ Hardware-faithful for <em>bus</em> writes</td></tr>
+  <tr><td><strong>Reject</strong> (queue an error)</td><td><code>count</code> unchanged, SCPI error <code>-222</code> queued</td><td>✓ Hardware-faithful <em>and</em> loud — this repo's choice</td></tr>
+</table>
+
+<p>Both mask and reject keep the state space honest, and the flipped test deliberately accepts either policy — <code>assert errors(mcu) == 1 <strong>or</strong> mcu.timer_a[0].count &lt;= 0xFFFF</code> — because both are defensible readings of the silicon. Why did this repo pick <strong>reject with -222</strong>?</p>
+
+<ul>
+  <li><strong>The write is already a firmware bug.</strong> On the real part a 16-bit bus write physically can't carry 0x12345; C code that computes an over-width timer value has truncated it (or invoked implementation-defined behaviour) <em>before</em> the store. A SCPI parameter is wider than a bus, so the simulator sees the un-truncated intent — and an error queue entry points a finger at it. Masking would reproduce the hardware result while hiding the mistake.</li>
+  <li><strong>Silent masking mimics the silent hardware failure.</strong> A masked <code>0x1F400 → 0xF400</code> "works" and produces a period ~4× off. The whole point of SIL is to catch that in simulation with a diagnosis, not to faithfully reproduce the confusion.</li>
+  <li><strong>It matches the codebase's precedent</strong> — <code>ADC:RESOLUTION 9</code>, <code>ADC:REFERENCE BANANA</code>, illegal GPIO pins: this model consistently validates and rejects rather than coercing.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h3>-222 vs -224: two flavours of rejection</h3>
+<p>Issue #19's handlers use both standard SCPI codes, and the distinction is worth internalising:</p>
+<table>
+  <tr><th>Code</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Used when</th><th>Example</th></tr>
+  <tr><td class="mono">-222</td><td>Data out of range</td><td>Numeric parameter outside the register's representable range</td><td><code>COUNT 0x10000</code>, <code>CC1:VALUE 70000</code>, <code>COUNT -1</code></td></tr>
+  <tr><td class="mono">-224</td><td>Illegal parameter value</td><td>Value not in a finite encoding set (membership test)</td><td><code>DIVIDER 128</code>, <code>CC1:MODE BANANA</code>, <code>MODE SIDEWAYS</code></td></tr>
+</table>
+
+<div class="callout tip">
+  <div class="callout-label">💡 The design rule</div>
+  A <em>range</em> violation (the value type is right, the magnitude isn't) gets -222. A <em>set-membership</em> violation (the value has no encoding at all) gets -224. The divider is -224 even though 128 is numeric, because the divider is an enumerated set of reachable products, not a continuous range.
+</div>
+</section>
+
