+<!-- ============================================================ -->
+<section>
+<h2>1. A Clock Signal Is Source × Divider, Not a Stored Number</h2>
+
+<p>The single most important idea in this lesson: <strong>a system clock has no frequency of its own.</strong> MCLK, SMCLK, and ACLK are not oscillators — they are <em>routes</em>. Each one is a multiplexer that picks an oscillator, followed by a divider that scales it. The frequency you observe on the pin is a <strong>derived value</strong>:</p>
+
+<div class="equation">
+  f<sub>clock</sub> &nbsp;=&nbsp;
+  <span class="frac">
+    <span class="top">f<sub><span class="vr">selected source</span></sub></span>
+    <span class="bot"><span class="fs">divider</span></span>
+  </span>
+  <span class="eq-note">resolve the source → look up its base frequency → apply the divider — every time, at read time</span>
+</div>
+
+<p>Nothing in the hardware "stores" the number 1 MHz for MCLK. What the CS (Clock System) module stores is <em>configuration</em>: a 3-bit source-select field and a 3-bit divider field per clock. Ask "what is MCLK's frequency?" and the only honest answer walks the tree:</p>
+
+<ol>
+  <li><strong>Resolve the source:</strong> which oscillator does the mux currently select? (<code>CSCTL2.SELM</code>)</li>
+  <li><strong>Get the base frequency:</strong> what does <em>that oscillator</em> run at right now? (DCO setting × drift, crystal value, or a fixed internal rate)</li>
+  <li><strong>Apply the divider:</strong> divide by the configured /1…/32. (<code>CSCTL3.DIVM</code>)</li>
+</ol>
+
+<p>Skip step 1 — assume the source is always the DCO — and you get a number that is <em>sometimes</em> right (when DCO happens to be selected) and wildly wrong otherwise. That is exactly the bug commit 701853d fixed. With MCLK switched to the 32.768-kHz LFXT crystal, the old model still reported <strong>1 000 000 Hz</strong> — off by a factor of ~30.5.</p>
+
+<div class="callout tip">
+  <div class="callout-label">💡 The mental model</div>
+  Think of a clock as a <em>plumbing route</em>, not a tank. The question "how much water comes out of the MCLK tap?" depends on which reservoir the valve currently points at and how far the restrictor is closed. Caching yesterday's flow rate — or always quoting the DCO reservoir — answers a different question than the one asked.
+</div>
+</section>
+
+<!-- ============================================================ -->
+<section>
+<h2>2. The FR5994 Clock Tree — Sources, Muxes, and Dividers</h2>
+
+<p>The MSP430FR5994's CS module (SLAU367P chapter 3) generates three system clocks from six possible oscillators. The routing is set by <strong>CSCTL2</strong> (three source-select fields) and <strong>CSCTL3</strong> (three divider fields):</p>
+
+<table>
+  <tr><th>Clock</th><th>Mux field</th><th>Selectable sources (SLAU367P §3.3.2)</th><th>Divider field</th><th>Feeds</th></tr>
+  <tr><td><strong>MCLK</strong></td><td><code>CSCTL2.SELM</code></td><td>LFXT, VLO, LFMODCLK, DCO, MODCLK, HFXT</td><td><code>CSCTL3.DIVM</code></td><td>CPU and system</td></tr>
+  <tr><td><strong>SMCLK</strong></td><td><code>CSCTL2.SELS</code></td><td>same six as MCLK</td><td><code>CSCTL3.DIVS</code></td><td>Timers, eUSCI (UART/SPI/I2C)</td></tr>
+  <tr><td><strong>ACLK</strong></td><td><code>CSCTL2.SELA</code></td><td>LFXT, VLO, LFMODCLK <em>only</em></td><td><code>CSCTL3.DIVA</code></td><td>Low-power peripherals, RTC, slow timers</td></tr>
+</table>
+
+<p>Two structural facts matter:</p>
+<ul>
+  <li><strong>ACLK's mux is narrower.</strong> ACLK is the "auxiliary" low-frequency clock, intended to keep ticking in low-power modes — so its mux only offers the three low-frequency sources. <code>CLOCK:ACLK:SOURCE DCO</code> is not a policy choice the simulator makes; there is <em>no SELA encoding</em> for DCO on this silicon.</li>
+  <li><strong>All three dividers share one encoding.</strong> DIVM/DIVS/DIVA are 3-bit fields whose defined encodings are /1, /2, /4, /8, /16, /32. There is no /64 and no /128 — values the pre-fix model happily accepted.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h3>What the model stores per clock</h3>
+<pre><code><span class="cm"># silmulator/devices/msp430.py — ClockState after 701853d</span>
+<span class="attr">@dataclass</span>
+<span class="kw">class</span> <span class="cls">ClockState</span>:
+    mclk_source: str = <span class="str">"DCO"</span>   <span class="cm"># DCO | LFXT | HFXT | VLO | MODCLK | LFMODCLK</span>
+    mclk_div: int = <span class="num">1</span>
+    smclk_source: str = <span class="str">"DCO"</span>  <span class="cm"># same source set as MCLK (CSCTL2.SELS)</span>
+    smclk_div: int = <span class="num">1</span>
+    aclk_source: str = <span class="str">"LFXT"</span>  <span class="cm"># LFXT | VLO | LFMODCLK (CSCTL2.SELA)</span>
+    aclk_div: int = <span class="num">1</span>
+    dco_frequency: float = <span class="num">1e6</span>  <span class="cm"># Hz</span>
+    lfxt_frequency: float = <span class="num">32768.0</span>
+    <span class="cm"># HFXT has no fixed nominal in hardware — it is an external crystal whose</span>
+    <span class="cm"># range is configured via CSCTL4.HFFREQ (SLAU367P §3.3.4, not modeled).</span>
+    <span class="add">hfxt_frequency: float = 4e6  # simulator-only default</span>
+    vcc: float = <span class="num">3.3</span></code></pre>
+
+<p>Note what is stored: <strong>sources and dividers</strong> — configuration — plus base frequencies for the two crystals and the DCO. No per-clock frequency field exists anywhere. That is deliberate: storing <code>mclk_frequency</code> would be a second copy of derivable truth, and the two copies would eventually disagree (the same double-store bug class as the old independent timer <code>period</code> field from lesson 0010's commit family).</p>
+
+<div class="callout warn">
+  <div class="callout-label">⚠ Where is REFO?</div>
+  If you have used MSP430F5xx/6xx parts, you may reach for REFO — the 32-kHz trimmed reference oscillator in those parts' UCS clock module. The FR59xx CS module <strong>does not have it</strong>: no SELM/SELS/SELA encoding maps to REFO (same for DCOCLKDIV). The pre-fix model accepted <code>CLOCK:MCLK:SOURCE REFO</code> and rejected MODCLK — exactly backwards for this chip.
+</div>
+</section>
+
