+<section>
+<h2>4. Fixed-Rate Sources: VLO, MODCLK, LFMODCLK (and the HFXT Special Case)</h2>
+
+<p>The six oscillators fall into three kinds, and the resolver treats each kind differently:</p>
+
+<table>
+  <tr><th>Source</th><th>Kind</th><th>Frequency in the model</th><th>Where it comes from</th></tr>
+  <tr><td><code>DCO</code></td><td>Trimmed internal oscillator</td><td><code>dco_frequency × faults.dco_factor()</code> (default 1 MHz)</td><td>Configurable via <code>CLOCK:DCO:FREQUENCY</code>; CORNER fault profile adds ±3.5% drift</td></tr>
+  <tr><td><code>LFXT</code></td><td>External LF crystal</td><td><code>clock.lfxt_frequency</code> (default 32 768 Hz)</td><td>Board property — the watch crystal</td></tr>
+  <tr><td><code>HFXT</code></td><td>External HF crystal</td><td><code>clock.hfxt_frequency</code> (default 4 MHz)</td><td><strong>Simulator-only default</strong> — see below</td></tr>
+  <tr><td><code>VLO</code></td><td>Fixed internal oscillator</td><td><code>VLO_FREQUENCY_HZ = 9400.0</code></td><td>SLAU367P §3.2.6 — ~9.4 kHz typical, no configuration register</td></tr>
+  <tr><td><code>MODCLK</code></td><td>Fixed internal oscillator</td><td><code>MODCLK_FREQUENCY_HZ = 4.8e6</code></td><td>SLAU367P §3.2.5 — ~4.8 MHz typical, no configuration register</td></tr>
+  <tr><td><code>LFMODCLK</code></td><td>Derived from MODCLK</td><td><code>LFMODCLK_FREQUENCY_HZ = MODCLK_FREQUENCY_HZ / 128</code> = 37 500 Hz</td><td>SLAU367P §3.2.5 — MODCLK through a fixed /128</td></tr>
+</table>
+
+<pre><code><span class="cm"># Fixed-frequency clock sources with no configurable register (SLAU367P</span>
+<span class="cm"># §3.2.5 MODCLK, ~4.8 MHz typical; §3.2.6 VLOCLK, ~9.4 kHz typical).</span>
+<span class="cm"># LFMODCLK is MODCLK divided by 128 (SLAU367P §3.2.5).</span>
+MODCLK_FREQUENCY_HZ = <span class="num">4.8e6</span>
+VLO_FREQUENCY_HZ = <span class="num">9400.0</span>
+LFMODCLK_FREQUENCY_HZ = MODCLK_FREQUENCY_HZ / <span class="num">128</span></code></pre>
+
+<p>Notice that <code>LFMODCLK_FREQUENCY_HZ</code> is <em>computed from</em> <code>MODCLK_FREQUENCY_HZ</code> rather than written as a second literal <code>37500.0</code>. Same principle as the tree itself: LFMODCLK <strong>is</strong> MODCLK/128 — encoding the relationship, not the result, means the two constants can never drift apart.</p>
+
+<h3>Why VLO and MODCLK are "about" their frequencies</h3>
+<p>VLO (the very-low-power oscillator) and MODCLK are uncalibrated RC oscillators. The datasheet quotes them as <em>typical</em> values with wide tolerances — VLO can legitimately sit anywhere around 9.4 kHz and wanders with temperature and supply. The simulator models the typical value; the lesson for firmware is that <strong>anything timed from VLO is approximate by design</strong> (it exists to keep watchdogs and wakeups alive cheaply, not to timestamp anything).</p>
+
+<h3>The HFXT honesty comment</h3>
+<p>HFXT gets a different treatment, and the commit documents why:</p>
+<pre><code><span class="cm"># HFXT has no fixed nominal in hardware — it is an external crystal whose</span>
+<span class="cm"># range is configured via CSCTL4.HFFREQ (SLAU367P §3.3.4, not modeled).</span>
+<span class="cm"># This is a simulator-only default so HFXT-sourced clocks resolve to a</span>
+<span class="cm"># concrete frequency.</span>
+hfxt_frequency: float = <span class="num">4e6</span>  <span class="cm"># Hz</span></code></pre>
+<p>There is no "typical HFXT frequency" to model — the board designer chooses the crystal. The model needs <em>some</em> value so an HFXT-sourced clock can answer a query, so it picks 4 MHz and labels it a simulator-only default rather than pretending it is a hardware fact. Compare VeREF+ from lesson 0009: board-supplied values get modeled as declared state, not invented constants.</p>
+</section>
+
