+<section>
+<h2>6. Why This Matters for SIL Testing — the Lying-Frequency Bug Class</h2>
+
+<p>A frequency query that ignores the selected source doesn't fail — it <strong>answers confidently with the wrong number</strong>. Everything downstream that trusts the number inherits the lie.</p>
+
+<h3>Downstream victim 1: timer intervals</h3>
+<p>Timers divide SMCLK or ACLK. Firmware that switches ACLK to VLO for a low-power heartbeat expects ticks at ~9.4 kHz. The old model ticked such timers at 32 768 Hz (LFXT, hardwired), so a "1-second" watchdog pet interval simulated as ~0.29 s. A SIL test around that timing passed in simulation and starved the watchdog on silicon — or vice versa.</p>
+
+<h3>Downstream victim 2: UART baud math</h3>
+<p>On the real FR5994, the eUSCI_A baud-rate generator divides SMCLK (or ACLK). Firmware computes the divisor from the clock frequency it <em>believes</em> it has:</p>
+<pre><code><span class="cm">/* firmware under test — BUG: SMCLK was switched to VLO to save power,</span>
+<span class="cm">   but the baud divisor still assumes the 1-MHz DCO */</span>
+<span class="fn">select_smclk_source</span>(VLO);                <span class="cm">/* CSCTL2.SELS = VLOCLK      */</span>
+UCA0BRW = <span class="num">1000000</span> / <span class="num">9600</span>;                <span class="cm">/* = 104 — for a 1-MHz clock */</span>
+<span class="cm">/* actual baud = 9400 / 104 ≈ 90 baud, not 9600 — a ~106× error       */</span></code></pre>
+<ul>
+  <li><strong>Old model:</strong> a SIL script asking <code>CLOCK:SMCLK:FREQUENCY?</code> to cross-check the firmware's assumption got <code>1000000</code> back — the same wrong number the firmware assumed. Simulator and bug agreed; the test passed.</li>
+  <li><strong>New model:</strong> the query returns <code>9400</code>. Any assertion tying baud to the reported SMCLK now fails loudly in simulation, where it is cheap to fix.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h3>The pattern: derived-value queries must derive</h3>
+<p>This is the same disease lesson 0009 diagnosed in the ADC (a hardcoded 3.3-V reference) wearing a different peripheral: <strong>a query for a derived value answered from the wrong input</strong>. The cure is always the same shape — resolve the configuration chain at read time:</p>
+<div class="equation">
+  answer(query) = f(<span class="vr">current configuration</span>)
+  <span class="eq-note">never f(default configuration), never a cached snapshot — the resolver walks source → base frequency → divider on every call</span>
+</div>
+
+<h3>Why resolve at read time, not at write time?</h3>
+<p>One could imagine computing the frequency when the source is selected and storing it. That breaks in three ways, all live in this codebase:</p>
+<ul>
+  <li><code>CLOCK:DCO:FREQUENCY 8e6</code> after selecting DCO must change what MCLK reports — a snapshot taken at selection time would not.</li>
+  <li>Dividers change independently of sources; two writers to one cached value.</li>
+  <li>The CORNER fault profile's DCO drift is applied inside <code>_dco_actual()</code> at query time; a cache would freeze the drifted value.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<div class="callout warn">
+  <div class="callout-label">⚠ The general lesson</div>
+  Stored configuration plus derived answers is one honest architecture; stored answers is a lie generator. When you see a getter that returns state without consulting the selector that governs it, you have found this bug class — grep for sibling getters immediately (here the audit found all three FREQUENCY? handlers plus the timer path in one sweep).
+</div>
+</section>
+
+<!-- ============================================================ -->
+<section>
+<h2>7. Often Confused</h2>
+
+<table>
+  <tr><th>Confusion</th><th>The distinction</th></tr>
+  <tr>
+    <td><strong>"MCLK's frequency" as a stored property</strong></td>
+    <td>MCLK has no frequency field — only a source and a divider. The frequency is computed per query: <code>_clock_source_frequency(mclk_source) / mclk_div</code>. Storing it would duplicate derivable truth (the double-store bug class).</td>
+  </tr>
+  <tr>
+    <td><strong>Clock (MCLK/SMCLK/ACLK) vs. source (DCO/VLO/…)</strong></td>
+    <td>Sources oscillate; clocks route. DCO is an oscillator you can point MCLK <em>at</em>; "MCLK" names the output of the SELM mux and DIVM divider, whoever feeds it. The old bug was exactly conflating the two: treating MCLK as "the DCO, divided".</td>
+  </tr>
+  <tr>
+    <td><strong>MODCLK vs. LFMODCLK</strong></td>
+    <td>One oscillator, two taps. MODCLK is the ~4.8-MHz module oscillator; LFMODCLK is the same oscillator through a fixed /128 → 37.5 kHz. The code encodes the relationship: <code>LFMODCLK_FREQUENCY_HZ = MODCLK_FREQUENCY_HZ / 128</code>.</td>
+  </tr>
+  <tr>
+    <td><strong>VLO vs. LFXT</strong></td>
+    <td>Both are "slow clocks" but VLO is an internal, uncalibrated ~9.4-kHz RC oscillator (free, imprecise, no crystal needed) while LFXT is an external 32 768-Hz crystal (precise, costs a part and two pins). 32768/9400 ≈ 3.49 — confusing them skews every derived interval by that factor.</td>
+  </tr>
+  <tr>
+    <td><strong>SELM/SELS vs. SELA</strong></td>
+    <td>MCLK and SMCLK share one six-entry source menu; ACLK's menu has only the three low-frequency entries. <code>DCO</code> is a valid answer for <code>CLOCK:SMCLK:SOURCE</code> and a -224 for <code>CLOCK:ACLK:SOURCE</code> — same token, different mux.</td>
+  </tr>
+  <tr>
+    <td><strong>-224 vs. -222 in the clock handlers</strong></td>
+    <td>-224 "Illegal parameter value": a token outside the enum (<code>SOURCE REFO</code>, <code>SOURCE BANANA</code>). -222 "Data out of range": a number outside the encodable range (<code>DIVIDER 64</code>). Commit 701853d deliberately moved the divider error from -224 to -222.</td>
+  </tr>
+  <tr>
+    <td><strong><code>:.0f</code> rounding vs. <code>int()</code> truncation</strong></td>
+    <td>The clock FREQUENCY? handlers format with <code>f"{freq:.0f}"</code>, which rounds (37500/8 = 4687.5 → "4688"). The ADC code path uses <code>int()</code>, which truncates (2047.5 → 2047). Predicting exact simulator outputs requires knowing which one the path uses.</td>
+  </tr>
+</table>
+</section>
+
+<!-- ============================================================ -->
+<section>
+<h2>8. Quiz</h2>
+
+<div class="quiz-wrap">
+
+  <!-- Q1 -->
+  <div class="question" data-question>
+    <p class="q-text"><span class="q-num">Q1.</span> After <code>CLOCK:ACLK:SOURCE LFMODCLK</code> and <code>CLOCK:ACLK:DIVIDER 8</code>, what does <code>CLOCK:ACLK:FREQUENCY?</code> return?</p>
+    <ul class="options">
+      <li data-correct="true"  data-why="LFMODCLK = 4.8e6/128 = 37500 Hz; 37500/8 = 4687.5; f-string :.0f rounds (ties-to-even) → 4688.">4688</li>
+      <li data-correct="false" data-why="4687 assumes int() truncation. The clock handlers format with f&quot;{freq:.0f}&quot;, which rounds 4687.5 up to the even neighbour 4688 — only the ADC path truncates.">4687</li>
+      <li data-correct="false" data-why="37500 is LFMODCLK before the divider. The DIVIDER 8 command was accepted (8 is a valid DIVA encoding) and must be applied.">37500</li>
+      <li data-correct="false" data-why="600 would be 4.8e6/8000 — mixing up MODCLK and LFMODCLK. LFMODCLK is MODCLK/128 = 37.5 kHz, then /8 → 4687.5.">600</li>
